Barack Obama will be the fourth U.S. president to receive the Nobel Peace Prize
U.S. President Barack Obama will arrive in Oslo, Norway to spend 26 hours and receive the Nobel Peace Prize, as the fourth U.S. president in history.
The diploma, medal and check of 1.4 million dollars will receive them, among other things, for the exceptional effort to strengthen global diplomacy and cooperation between nations.
White House chief and his wife Michelle will arrive in the Norwegian metropolis in the morning and have a lot to do.
First, visit of the Nobel Institute, where for the first time he will meet with the five members of the committee that, in October, elected him between 172 people and 33 organizations.
Afterwards, the presidential couple is planning a meeting with the king of Norway, Harald V, and Queen Sonia.
In the afternoon the visit will culminate with a ceremony in which Obama will receive the prestigious award.
He will be the fourth U.S. president to receive it, even though the third to receive him.
The White House has already announced that, upon receiving the award, it will speak of the Afghan war.
The President does not want to avoid such an issue, knowing that he is receiving the award as president of a country that is currently at war in two countries.
His war efforts increased even more with the sending of additional troops to Afghanistan, which he does not forget to mention any of his critics.
At the ceremony, Obama will also receive the gold medal, diploma, and check of ten million Swedish kroners (approximately 24 million Czech kroners).
It is proposed to donate charitable paraffins, but has not yet decided which particular organization.
During his complete program, the President and his wife will participate in a banquet attended, in addition to the Norwegian kings, the prime minister and another 250 guests.
Obama from the beginning had a rather reserved attitude towards the prize.
He mentioned, for example, having the feeling that he didn’t fully deserve it.
Likewise, he repeated several times that the prize is not only for him, but for all those who fight for the same values as him.
Immediately after the announcement he mentioned that it represents "a challenge to take action".
Defender of nature accused of blackmail
Litoměřice's police accused the president of the civic association Společnost ochránců přírody Litoměřice (Society of Nature Advocates Litoměřice).
Last year he appealed several times against some construction licensing procedures and to withdraw the appeals demanded money from investors for their own gain, Litoměřice police spokeswoman Alena Romová said.
The president of the Litoměřice Nature Defender Society is Lubomír Studnička.
He is currently detained and is facing a sentence of up to three years in prison.
Brno doesn't like too much bins to separate trash
In Prague, citizens can separate beverage packaging and in the villages of southern Moravia you can find containers to separate garbage everywhere.
What about Brno?
It takes several minutes there to find the respective containers.
I don't want to say that in Brno the trash won't be separated at all.
It seems to me that, in order to be the second largest city in the Republic, municipalities are not quite interested in this problem.
Brno is totally lagging behind in the separation of garbage, not only by the fact that we citizens can only use containers to separate paper, glass and plastic, but above all because of the number of containers for such waste.
Have they ever tried, for example, to throw in the center a bottle of PET into the right container?
It's almost impossible, especially it takes a lot of time and patience.
The only place I've found so far is Moravské Náměstí square, in front of Potrefená Huse.
Don't let Mr Onderka and his companions be angry with me, but that seems too little to me.
Needless to mention that elsewhere it is also not easy to separate garbage.
That's why a lot of my friends prefer not to separate it.
For vagrancy
What happens is that the containers are far away.
It doesn't surprise me.
I myself offered, after a party, to throw away several glass and plastic bottles.
But I didn't see any red containers nearby on Kaunicova Street.
Luckily, going to the tram I found the right place.
However, the trash was already overflowing from the container.
It’s still the center of Brno, the most representative part of the city, doesn’t it?!
What happens is that ecology and aesthetics don’t seem to interest Brno’s socialists too much.
They prefer to buy the city the extra-league of hockey, which embarrasses us throughout the republic, and not to buy more containers or offer the separation of other materials so that Brno is cleaner.
I'm glad to be able to compare my hypotheses, after surfing the Internet a bit, with official statistics.
According to EKO-KOM society, Brno is truly the worst in the Jihomoravský kraj region.
"Paroubek's" budget removed money from rents and sickness subsidies
After the left, despite the government’s view, added funds for farmers or officials, there may not be enough money in the budget for rents, sickness subsidies or construction savings.
But neither for the interests of state debt or international litigation.
This will hurt everyone who governs in the second half of next year, Finance Minister Eduard Janota says.
When the Social Democrats and the Communists imposed the increase for some groups, they trusted that the government subsequently asked for a loan for sensitive expenses, rents or sickness subsidies. "That's just to defer problems for the future, it's to land mines," says Janota.
What happens is that, when you borrow money, you have to pay higher and higher interest and, one day, you have to return the money.
The state doesn’t have many options to get money.
If you don’t reduce the state’s social subsidies or operating expenses, you have to raise taxes.
To all those who added the left now: firefighters, teachers, farmers.
Later, his sons.
People don't have to worry about income or sickness subsidies, they'll receive them. 1.800 million disappeared from these accounts.
But the price to pay will be the increase in public debt; next year there will be only 163,000 million missing in the treasury, but more.
Despite Minister Janota’s effort, the debt will continue to rise alongside interest expenditures.
The state will need money to cover them and it will no longer come with a slight increase in VAT or reduce maternity subsidies, as the government did in its anti-deficit package.
Due to dislocations of money for the benefit of some groups of voters, next year there will also be five billion to complete the construction of highways and railways.
It is, above all, the sectors in which the tender has already been held and where the construction process has already begun, whose projects cannot be changed and neither can its price be reduced.
The most you can do is keep them, which is not free either.
That means delay or cessation of the thirty large constructions; the possibility of obtaining European funds is also in jeopardy, warns the spokesman of the Ministry of Transport Karel Hanzelka.
ČSSD proposed a Solomonic solution for the reduction of funds: it will obtain money from the dividends of ČEZ, which is mostly owned by the State, and will add the transport fund.
The problem is that the dividend’s value is never safe or a bottomless sack.
Last year of ČEZ allocated 18,000 million to the budget.
Each year of the dividends are liquidated ecological damages, serve to complete the account for rents or serve as a reserve for the prepared income reform.
In addition, with this trick, according to the rules of the EU, the real budget deficit increases to the level of 5.7% of GDP.
The carnival of money transfers doesn't end here.
Almost three billion were obtained from the budget to grant several indemnities to victims of communism and crimes, or for litigation expenses, even in construction savings.
It’s unclear whether the state will need such funds.
It's a lottery.
If we lose any international litigation, maybe we'll have to pay for it, Janota says.
Afterwards, the Minister will have to save money inside the spring or, in the worst case, take advantage of the government’s budget reserve.
There are also one hundred million left for the Statní pokladna (State Box) project, whose price is two billion and which has already been initiated. It must allow you to monitor online what the different authorities are spending on.
The first stage begins in January.
If the finance minister doesn’t find the money elsewhere, the project will have to stop and sanctions will arrive, Janota warns.
Your future smartphone can work with two operating systems
Americans will have a mobile phone in which the user will be able, pressing a key, to switch between various operating systems.
Revealed plans sound promising.
At the touch of a key, they pass in a few seconds from Windows Mobile to Android.
Precisely this possibility is the objective of the American company VMware, which concentrates mainly on the development of virtualizing software for computers.
On your mobile you will be able to carry two user profiles at the same time.
They will be able to shift between them and divide them between home and work.
The two operate at the same time, Srinivas Krishnamurti of the company VMware indicated in the interview for Computerworld magazine.
It occurred in November last year and several days ago was demonstrated for the first time.
It will be released in 2012.
The virtualization of smartphones is no longer fiction.
VMware has already successfully demonstrated to journalists how the smartphone works with two operating systems.
It was the Nokia N800, modified with 128 MB RAM, where the aforementioned systems, Windows Mobile and Android work, at the same time.
The development of the new technology for mobile phones is at its peak.
VMware cooperates with European and U.S
Lack of snow in the mountains worries hoteliers
It's not just telescine maintainers in Krkonoše that they're worried about snowless slopes.
Lack of snow means that people don't book ski stays in hotels and guesthouses.
That’s why in Krkonoše you can still get free rooms for all winter dates, including for Christmas Eve and New Year’s Eve.
We've got a lot of visitors on our pages.
People look at the offers, check the prices, but they are still afraid to book with commitment.
And if they call us, they immediately ask about our opinion, if this Christmas there will be snow or mud," said the maintainer of the Spindl.Info Martin Jandura information portal.
Those who want to pass in Špindlerův Mlýn a New Year's Eve according to their expectations, should not hesitate more to book the stay.
Those who prefer to save the money and put the quality of accommodation second, can try to wait a few more days.
Špindlerův Mlýn hoteliers offer, above all, weekly stays for New Year's Eve.
I think they will continue to offer them, but they will soon give in and offer shorter stays.
And then the free rooms will sell quickly, Jandura estimated.
Ingotours, a Vrchlabí travel agency, continues to offer free beds for all winter dates.
Now what interests us the most is Christmas week, which is not yet complete.
Half of the places offered remain free.
That's why we've already prepared some last-minute Christmas stays," Ingtours director Petr Schiefert said.
Vrchlabí's travel agency can still offer New Year's Eve in Krkonoše, but there are fewer free spots.
The first fourteen days of February are sold very well, the remaining winter dates are sold regularly.
This year the interest in winter stays is harmed by the lack of snow in Krkonoše.
People are waiting to see if there will be snow.
No one wants to spend the end of year on the mountain without snow.
If the clues remain snowy during the week, the Christmas dates will also run out," Schiefert says.
On the contrary, it’s a bit easier to occupy the rooms at the Horizont hotel in Pec pod Sněžkou; last year it was more difficult.
We're about five percent better.
For New Year’s Eve and Christmas, we have the last places free.
Interest increases during Polish holidays in January and February also sells well," said the director of Pec's best hotel, Karel Rada.
Eighty percent of New Year's Eve stays are also occupied in the Omnia hotel, near the central car park in Janské Lázně.
On the contrary, in the most recent hotel in Janské Lázně at Christmas, half of the beds are still free.
This January was a big surprise for us, as we found approximately sixty percent occupancy capacity.
February has been a little weaker, but as soon as snow falls, the interest in Krkonoše stays will surely increase, said Erik Sporysch, the director of the Omnia hotel.
However, at the moment it seems that Krkonoše’s hoteliers will have the desire of snow waiting.
Temperatures should drop on Thursday and, if there is precipitation, in the mountains it should snow.
But after again temperatures will rise, reported Jiří Jakubský of Hradec Králové, of the Hydrometeorological Institute of the Czech Republic.
U.S.: Repetition is the Mother of Wisdom
It’s funny that Barack Obama, the president with the reputation of the smartest man, as a diligent student repeats in the Afghan question the strategy of his predecessor, whom he himself took for the dumbest.
When he finally revealed his long-awaited Afghan approach, it was shown to be a copy of the script used for Iraq by Bush, created three years ago.
Not only in the same text is the locution “just like in Iraq” several times, but he also did not hesitate to use the title of Bush’s January 2007 statement for his own statement: “A New Way Forward.”
It was himself, who three years ago criticized Bush’s idea of sending new military reinforcements to Iraq, the country that is suffering from the civil war. And he criticized it as “an irresponsible decision with catastrophic consequences.”
The following months, however, showed that Bush’s strategy, based on the recommendations of field captains, was the only possible and successful, so that there is no talk of Iraq in the news today.
Bush’s reputation didn’t improve, yet he objectively left his successor a much more favourable initial situation than might be expected.
In Afghanistan, Barack Obama expects the miracle to be repeated.
The field captains again asked the military contingent, especially the commander-in-chief of the U.S
He asked 40,000 men to improve the situation.
The president hesitated for three months, but didn't come up with anything better, so at least he tricked the general into 10,00 soldiers.
He managed to get Europe to promise him another 5,000, although he himself previously had at least 10,000.
The most problematic thing about Obama’s entire strategy seems his goal, to start removing troops from Afghanistan in 18 months and finalizing this procedure in three years. Obama’s entire doctrine generated more questions from the answers he offered.
Many doubt that the increase bears the same fruit as the procedure in Iraq.
Despite the fratricidal wars in recent years, Iraq’s society is relatively well-organized, its leaders respect the agreements.
The situation in Afghanistan is different: it is not possible to trust anyone and no deal is fulfilled.
Under these conditions it is difficult to prepare the Afghan security forces, if they are used to changing the boss every day according to their needs.
Political institutions, albeit formally established, will remain only a village of Potemkin; patriarchal tribal relations will remain behind it.
Balancing the interests of different ethnic groups is extraordinarily complicated due to confusing relations, even more complicated by Pakistani and Iranian influences.
Not even establishing a state that is “acable of action”, a minimum objective of the entire operation, seems very real.
The horizon of Obama’s strategy clashes over his voluntarism.
Over the past eight years, the situation in Afghanistan has continued to worsen and is now about to erupt.
Only a romantic soul, who knows nothing of reality, can believe that the military sent to war to "return early home", will perform some miracle here.
From the political point of view, setting the date of the withdrawal of the troops is sheer irresponsibility, since the Taliban will regard it as an indirect declaration of American defeat.
The last NATO summit showed that Europeans are increasingly less willing to participate in military operations in Afghanistan.
European politicians don’t know, and they don’t want to explain to their constituents either, what relationship Germany or Italy’s security relates to the war with the Hindu Kush.
The other factor, able to radically affect the situation, is the development of events in Iran.
If the situation continues to worsen and the increased sanctions do not exert any effect on the Iranians, the U.S. will have to make a difficult decision: try to solve the problem with military force or not.
It will be necessary to look for answers precisely when, according to Obama, America initiates the triumphal withdrawal of troops from Afghanistan.
However, each solution like this will mean a dramatic setback for the Afghan situation.
The only positive point for Barack Obama is the fact that there are three years before the presidential election and that’s why there’s still plenty of time to find another strategy, if this is a total failure.
Goldman Sach’s executives will not receive cash bonuses again
U.S. bank chief executive Goldman Sachs won’t receive cash bonuses this year.
The entity announced it to react to the harsh criticism of its salary practices.
The group of 30 senior managers will receive, instead of money, shares that cannot be sold within at least five years.
In addition, actions may be withdrawn in the event that they have assumed excessive risks.
According to Reuters, Goldman Sach thus became the leader in the effort to unite bonuses on Wall Street with long-term power.
I guess Wall Street is fully aware of the longer path it must undertake, said former JPMorgan investor banker Douglas Elliott.
The problem lies in the details, he added.
Goldman Sach Bank faced criticism after removing nearly US$17,000 million (almost 300,000 million CZK) for bonuses in the first three quarters of this year.
The total sum of the bonuses in the entity for this year, according to Reuters, probably exceeds, despite the measure announced today, the 20,000 million dollars.
High bonuses in the banking sector became, with the arrival of the financial crisis, a sensitive issue in politics.
Britain announced on Wednesday that it plans to tax at once, with a tax of fifty percent, bonuses for banks that exceed 25,000 pounds (approximately 712,000 CZK).
France is also preparing similar measures.
This year the women received the Nobel Prizes in all categories but Physics
Five women, which is a record figure, received this year’s Nobel Prizes in Stockholm in Stockholm today from King Charles XVI Gustav of Sweden, in specialized categories and in literature.
In addition to the four scientists, there was also a German writer of Romanian origin, Herta Müller.
The Nobel Prize in medicine was received by two U.S. biologists, Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider, as well as their compatriot Jack Szostak for research in the field of chromosomes.
The Nobel Prize in chemistry was received by the Israeli Ada Jonath and the Americans Venkatraman Ramakrishna and Thomas Steitz for the explanation of the structure and function of ribosomes.
The last awarded prize, the Nobel Prize in Economics, was received by the American Elinor Ostrom and his compatriot Oliver Williamson for the analysis of an economic report.
The only specialized category, without an award-winning woman, was Physics.
This award was received today by scientists Charles Kao for research in the field of optical cables, and George Smith with Willard Boyle for the invention of the CCD chip, which forms the basis of all digital cameras, faxes or telescopes.
Each winner picked up the diploma, the Nobel medal, and the certificate of money received.
This sum in each category is up to ten million Swedish kroners (approximately 25 million Czech krone).
In the case of several winners in each category, the prize is distributed to each other.
The traditionally most awaited Nobel, that of Peace, was received this afternoon in Oslo, Norway, U.S. President Barack Obama.
In his speech he recognized the controversy of having been awarded, since he is at the beginning of his career and is also at the head of a country that participates in two wars: in Iraq and in Afghanistan.
To defend his policy he added, however, that in certain situations wars are essential to achieve peace, even if their price is high.
Minister Janota considers the resignation. Klaus invited him to the Castle
President Václav Klaus will receive on Friday morning the finance minister Eduard Janota, who is considering his stay in the government, since he does not agree with the version of the state budget for next year, approved on Wednesday by the deputies chamber.
It is assumed that the budget, and perhaps also Janota’s stay in the cabinet, will be the topics of his meeting on Friday.
The budget is also criticized by Klaus.
According to him, the deficit is too large, which continues to deepen the crisis of public finances.
About the quotation was reported by the spokesman of President Radim Ochvat.
Regarding his permanence in the role, Janota wants to negotiate on Monday with Prime Minister Jan Fischer.
According to the budget law, prepared to be signed by Klaus, the Czech Republic should govern with a deficit of 163,000 million kroners.
The deputies, however, on Wednesday approved proposals from the left for transfers of funds and increased money for civil servants’ salaries, social services and direct payments to farmers.
The government does not agree with the proposals of the Social Democrats, as they covertly increase the budget deficit.
Between the left and the government, Klaus and the right on the other, there was a serious conflict of domestic policy. Due to this conflict, future activities of Fischer's bureaucratic cabinet begin to be controversial, whose form was agreed in spring by the SDG, ČSSD and the Green Party.
Critics say that the budget, in the version approved by the Chamber of Deputies, de facto liquidates the package of saving measures, imposed in autumn by the Fischer government to curb public indebtedness.
Klaus can but does not have to sign the law on the state budget; today he declared during a breakfast with businessmen that politicians had long disregarded "the serious problem", that is, "the unsustainable deficit" of public finances.
According to him today they no longer have a good solution.
Only a strong government, which can be supported in a broader consensus among political parties, could improve this situation, the president says.
SDG leader Mirek Topolánek mentioned that the budget is appalling and that the government should think about its future existence.
SDG Vice President Petr Nečas told ČTK that the bureaucratic cabinet concept with the support of ČSSD, SDGs and the Greens evidently no longer works.
Prime Minister Fischer reacted and called similar words "strong sentences."
ČSSD chief Jiří Paroubek reacted to Topolánek by saying that the budget deficit is appalling, prepared for this year by former Topolanek government. It will exceed 200,000 million kroners.
ČSSD on Wednesday said the approval of the state budget for next year was successful.
The populars were happy too.
Mandela in Clint Eastwood's new film, played by Morgan Freeman
The South Africans claim that the new Hollywood film, Invictus: Invincible, recounts a lot about the country, its struggle and its victories, although some criticisms say that the leading roles are played by American actors.
History speaks of sport, racial relations and Nelson Mandela.
The tireless fighter against the racist apartheid regime in the South African Republic and its first black president, Nelson Mandela, is portrayed in his biography by Oscar-winning American actor Morgan Freeman.
Freeman said Mandela, 91, asked him to play him in the Eastwood film.
Another important role is played by Matt Damon.
I said, "When I play you, I'll have access to you.
The seventy-two-year-old actor, who received the award for his role in Eastwood’s drama of boxing world Milion Dollar Baby, played the role of the leader of the slavery abolition movement, who ended up being slaved, from the fictitious U.S. president, and even from God in Almighty Evan. But he rarely represented someone who is still alive and, for many people, it’s as important as Mandela.
The former South African statesman spent 27 years in prison for his efforts to end apartheid in the South African Republic.
He was released in 1990 and for four years became president of the country.
In 1993 he received the Nobel Peace Laureate.
Invictus - Invincible is a Latin word and the title of a poem by the English author William Ernest Henley, published in 1875.
The challenge was to talk like him
The film is a true story about Nelson Mandela, who joined forces with South African rugby captain Francois Pienaar to help unite the country. Mandela, the new president, knows that after apartheid his nation remains racially and economically divided.
He believes that he is able to unite his people through sport, and that is why he decides to unite rugby players, who belong worldwide to outsiders.
The South African team manages to reach the World Cup in 1995.
Freeman worked several years for Mandela’s story to hit theaters.
He had no other goal than to play this role in such a way that it was as close as possible to reality, Freeman said.
Naturally, the biggest challenge was to talk like him.
The actor said that if he and the global politician were from the same area, he would have tried to meet Mandela, have dinner with him, be behind the scenes before his speech.
The most important thing was that he wanted to shake Mandela’s hand.
I found out that by squeezing your hand, I received your energy, it is transmitted and I had the feeling of knowing how you feel, he said.
It’s important for me to try to become a different person.
Matt Damon plays François Pienaar, the captain of the national rugby team, in which white players predominated.
The actor said he had six months to prepare for a role in the tough rugby world.
A big surprise for him was, when he finally met for the first time with Pienaar at home.
I remember knocking on the door, he opened and the first thing I told François Pienaar in my life was, "In the movie I look much taller."
Despite the obvious differences between the characters of the actors and their real models, and under the management of director Eastwood, Invictus: Invincible received positive reviews and there is also talk of ambitions for the Oscar.
Daily Variety magazine film critic Todd McCarthy summarized his assessment with these words: "It was a very good story, a very well-made movie."
In movie reviews on the website rottentomatoes.com, Invictus received 76 percent positive reviews.
Secrecy of splendour over Norway resolved: Russians have tested a missile
A special luminous effect of unknown origin was observed on Norway.
Russia’s Ministry of Defense ended up acknowledging yesterday that near the borders with Norway tested a ballistic intercontinental missile.
The launch failed again. The "Bulava"-type missile, formerly destined to be the pride of the Russian army, is becoming the nightmare of Russian generals and Prime Minister Vladimir Putin.
Not even his repeated presence in the missile tests has had the desired effect on the success of test flights.
Bulavá, in most cases, did not fly out or an air failure occurred.
In Russian newspapers they write of "Bulavah" as "a missile that does not fly."
A missile that doesn't fly, but shines.
During this, the 13th test, no problems occurred at first.
At the end of the flight there were again technical problems in the aircraft.
The engine probably exploded in the third stage of the missile.
This time "Bulavá" was launched from the Dmitry Donskoy nuclear submarine, which floated submerged in the White Sea.
This type of missile makes it possible to launch from a boat that is moving and submerged at the same time.
That's why it's likely that the mysterious light over Norway, caused by an unidentified flying object, was actually the failed "Bulavah."
Equal Norwegians from the outset did not waver that it was a Russian missile.
The generals this time defend themselves by stating that one cannot simply speak of a failure.
The first two stages of the missile endured and the breakdown occurred in the third.
In the previous cases, the engines broke down immediately in the first stage.
Although none of the launches passed completely without errors, according to the management of the Ministry of Defense only six of the thirteen were failed.
The success of almost fifty percent full of optimism to the generals and that is why they continue to affirm that "Bulavah" one day will fly without problems and that it will even manage to carry up to ten nuclear hypersonic blocks, whose maximum total weight is 1.15 tons.
The legendary Fetisov signed at the age of 51 an annual contract with CSKA of Moscow
Legendary defender Viacheslav Fetisov will return at the age of 51 for a professional hockey match.
Former world champion, Olympic champion and Stanley Cup winner will help his CSKA Moscow team and possibly play Friday in KHL's duel against St. Petersburg.
Fetisov completed his professional career at the age of forty in 1998 and currently presides over the CSKA.
After Denis Kulyash was hurt, we urgently need another defender.
Fetisov constantly practices and is committed to helping the team.
We just have to settle some formal issues, the AP told Sergei Nemchinov, the coach of the Moscow club.
It is not yet clear whether the famous player’s return will refer to a single match.
I think Fetisov is one of those who manage to lift the spirits of the other players, Nemchinov said.
According to AP if Fetisov returns to the ice rinks, he will become the longest-running professional player in Russian hockey.
Legendary forward Gordie Howe played in the NHL for Hartford at the age of fifty-one, during the entire season, and scored 15 goals and 26 assists.
The eldest of the group of five Fetisov-famous players, Kasatonov - Makarov, Larionov, Krutov, is one of the best and most successful hockey players in history.
Playing for the Soviet Union he won two Olympic golds, seven world championships, triumphed in the Canadian Cup and the junior world championship.
In the second part of his career he also had successes in the NHL: playing with Detroit he won the Stanley Cup twice.
Since 2001, he has been a member of the Glory Hall of Hockey.
Hyundai met union requirements. It will cancel all supplementary work
The management of the automobile factory in Nošovice agreed with the unions to cancel all supplementary work.
Precisely because of the frequent supplementary work, the unions declared an emergency by strike on Monday.
The information was confirmed by Petr Vaněk, the spokesman for the Hyundai automobile factory.
The management, however, wants for this year to agree with the unions one more Saturday shift.
We cannot resign ourselves to our manufacturing plan, since it is essential. That is why we proposed voluntary supplementary work for Saturday, December 19, that is, it will work on two shifts, Vaněk said.
According to server Sedmička.cz, the company offered a 400 CZK bonus to employees who work Saturday’s shift.
The bonus will also be paid for last Saturday, November 28.
According to Vaňka, the company decided to change the way of motivating its employees with the additional work offered.
Employees can charge the bonus for overtime or they can take a day off on December 28, that i.e. extending the Christmas holidays on a day, Vaněk said.
In addition, all Saturday shift employees will receive money for the trip and for food.
Because additional work was the most urgent and problematic point of comments and complaints from trade unionists and strikers, who stopped manufacturing last week, management yesterday afternoon immediately decided to cancel all supplementary work for December, Vaněk said.
Employees at the auto factory were forced to work two more hours in addition to eight-hour shifts and this situation lasted virtually every day until last week.
After signing the agreement the unions will cancel the strike emergency.
Another negotiation between the management of the factory and the trade unionists must take place on Monday morning.
Union chief Petr Kuchař said on Wednesday that if they pledged to meet the requirements and the document was signed, they are willing to cancel the strike emergency.
The situation at the auto factory worsened last week on Wednesday, when approximately 400 employees spontaneously went on strike over frequent supplemental work.
They also protested against poor payment conditions and alleged harassment.
Management said employees had to work overtimes to meet the requirements and to be able to supply the cars.
The unionists in their declaration of the emergency by strike on Monday, also demanded that none of the employees, who last Wednesday interrupted the manufacture, were penalized in any way.
With regard to supplementary work, trade unions require that the company reduce it to a minimum.
In addition, this year requires a remuneration of 5,000 kroner for this work.
Nošovice Hyundai’s car factory currently has 2,000 employees.
Serial manufacturing was started last November.
Until last September it manufactured approximately 80,000 cars; its current productive potential is 200,000 cars a year.
Czech discovery: an effective substance even in 'callecidos' HIV viruses
The team of Czech and German scientists tests new compounds capable of moderating the spread of HIV in the body
The main advantage of this improved substance is the fact that it is also effective against drug-resistant viruses.
In some cases this is more important than the fact that the compound is not as effective in a common variant of the virus, such as some already accessible drugs.
IDAS can't be cured until now.
However, the lives of patients can be significantly prolonged thanks to a mixture of drugs that prevent the replication of the HIV virus in the body.
However, its use has many side effects.
If the replication of the virus is not completely suppressed, so-called resistant viruses will be created; against them, medications no longer work.
The work of experts from three institutes of the Czech Academy of Sciences, VŠCHT Prague and Heidelberg University demonstrates the possibility of mastering the resistance of the virus.
They showed that substances called metallocarboranes have an effect on albumin, responsible for the spread of the HIV virus.
Metallocarborans are composed of boron, hydrogen, carbon and cobalt.
These compounds block the spread of the virus differently from all the drugs used today, and that is why they can solve the problem of resistance.
In their work the scientists find new, ‘improved’ compounds, prepared knowing the molecular mechanism of their binding to virus albumin.
Metallocarborans have a single three-dimensional structure: two polyhedral cages composed of boron, hydrogen and carbon atoms are bonded by the metal atom, in this case by cobalt.
A traitoring and resistant virus
HIV proteasis is an albumin of the HIV virus, and it is essential for the life cycle of the virus.
Without HIV proteasis, fractionation of the HIV virus does not occur and mature and infectious corpuscles of the virus would not be created.
If we stop HIV proteasis, we also stop the spread of the virus in the patient’s body, the scientists explain in a press story published by the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry at the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic.
In an article published in the scientific journal Journal of Medicinal Chemistry scientists describe a series of compounds, in which two pairs of cages (see the table) are linked by a short organic series, which is still systematically modified.
A bit weaker, but more effective
Testing of the effect of this series of substances against HIV proteasis was carried out in a specimen, and also against its resistant variants, obtained from HIV patients.
The effect of metallocarborans on the common variant of the enzyme is not as strong as in clinically used drugs, however they do not lose their effect on resistant variants, in which drugs often do not work.
The mechanism of the single effect, its other qualities such as biological and chemical stability, low toxicity and the possibility of other chemical modifications, which metallocarborans are interesting compounds for research with the aim of proposing effective anti-HIV medicines, says Pavlina Řezáčová, the head of the laboratory of structural biology at the Institute of organic and biochemistry chemistry and of the Institute of molecular genetics in the Academy.
Budget fight. ODS attacked Fischer, he defend
SDG boss Mirek Topolánek said that the budget approved for next year is "appalling" and Prime Minister Jan Fischer's government should, according to him, think about its future existence.
The SDG statements were pointed out by the prime minister as strong sentences.
They say it leaves time for analysis.
SDG vice-president Petr Nečas said the concept of the bureaucratic cabinet with the support of ČSSD, ODS and the Green Party evidently no longer works.
According to Nečas, the prime minister has to decide if in the Chamber of Deputies he wants to rely on the minority, who is trying to fight the growing deficit, or whether he wants to remain in office with the help of the parliamentary left.
SDG’s attitude and social democration towards the problem of the public deficit is completely different, Nečas warned.
The Chamber of Deputies yesterday approved the budget for 2010, however with the amending proposals on the part of the left, for more than 12 billion kroners.
Deputies increased money for the salaries of state officials, for social services and direct payments to farmers.
The government reproached the SDG proposals, as they increase the budget deficit.
The Czech Republic should govern with a deficit of 163 billion kroner.
SDGs before the vote left the chamber and thus fulfilled the government’s wish, that the modified budget is better than a provisional budget.
Disillusion and uselessness
Finance Minister Eduard Janota spoke of disillusionment and feeling of uselessness; Prime Minister Jan Fischer mentioned that the government will revitale the current situation.
Nečas is convinced that if Janota would seriously threaten the resignation in the event of approval of the amending proposals, the situation could have been different.
According to Česká televize Janota asked for a meeting with the prime minister.
He said he's thinking of staying in the function.
The reason is their disillusionment over the budget negotiations.
If Fischer agrees with another fight against the budget deficit, according to Nečas in the government they have nothing to done by ČSSD nominees, who imposed the changes in the budget.
The prime minister should also say clearly, if he will resign, if the chamber passes other laws that increase the deficit, for example those that refer to sickness subsidies, rents, or the amendment of the service law.
Topolánek: SDG-nominated ministers aren’t to blame
It's about Prime Minister Fischer and the whole government.
SDG-nominated ministers have done nothing wrong, Topolánek said in U.S
He recalled that in several ministries in the posts of lieutenants there are people belonging to TOP 09, who voted against the budget.
Nečas stressed that if Fischer decides to resign the principle of the struggle with the height of the deficit of the public finances, "it is absolutely logical and correct, to rely on the leftist majority, which is currently in the chamber of deputies."
However, he will not be able to count on the support and tolerance of SDG members.
I haven't thought about that, when it's time, I'll deal with it, Defense Minister Martin Barták, who was appointed to the government precisely by the members of the SDGs.
Prime Minister Fischer in the afternoon left for a European Council meeting in Brussels.
SDG ceded at Prime Minister's request
Nečas stressed that ODS on Wednesday in the deputy chamber would likely vote against the amended budget, if the prime minister does not explicitly call on SDG deputies to allow acceptance of the budget.
We don’t feel like orphans, we’re not young children, he reacted to the question, if ODS doesn’t care about the absence of the head of the party in parliament and in the Czech Republic during the budget negotiations.
I didn't realize my vote was missing.
When he resigned, he knew that in the following months it was above all the Bolshevization of the Chamber of Deputies and the destruction of everything positive, Topolánek himself reacted.
According to ČD’s new train schedule, fewer trains will circulate for almost the same fares
České dráhy (Czech Railways) from 13 December will cancel or restrict some less-used fast and local trains and, on the contrary, reinforce some links.
The total expanse of traffic will be reduced by two percent compared to today, after the new train schedule goes into effect.
Most of the rates even the basic and customer price will not change.
More chances of links to the capital will win for example the inhabitants of the Ostravsko region, dráhy for this line will add a pendolinous carto.
The direct train, on the contrary, will not circulate to Bratislava again.
On the Prague - Písek - České Budějovice line will circulate fewer fast trains, direct links Prague - Letohrad will be restricted.
In the regions, the largest reduction in drahy-planned transport in the Hradec Králové region, eight percent in total, on the contrary, transportation will increase in Prague and the surrounding area.
Two night trains will also be canceled on the Prague - Tábor - České Budějovice and some fast trains on the Prague - Písek - České Budějovice route will be reduced to just a few weeks.
One of the biggest changes is the introduction of a new hotline from Milovice to Prague, the inhabitants of Milovice have to change in Lysá nad Labem.
It is the 15. line of the Esko system, in the surroundings of the cities, its opening is possible thanks to the electrification of Lysá nad Labem to Milovice.
Long-distance links to the capital will end at Hlavní Nádraží Prague Station.
ČD will introduce more SC Pendolino links between Prague and Ostrava and trains to Šumperk/Jeseník will run every two hours.
From December 13, travelers will not travel to Bratislava in Pendolino.
This year’s regional railway account: 200 million more than before
The fast trains are commissioned and paid by the Ministry of Transport, to cover the so-called demonstrable losses Dráhy will receive next year four billion kroners in total, just like this year. Local trains are commissioned by the regions, next year they will pay eight billion kroners in total, and the State will help them almost three billion.
Regional trains are 200 million more expensive this year.
For the fast and local drahy trains have a new ten-year contract, until now the contract was always a year.
Dráhy are happy with the system, and perhaps that’s why they didn’t increase the prices of a large majority of tickets.
Changes will take place only on one-day tickets - ČD Net and cheap eLiška internet tickets.
For travelers without a customer card the price ČD Net will increase from 450 kroners to 600 kroners.
ELiška's price will depend on the distance, until now there was the unique price of 160 kronors for trips between any two regional cities.
Filipino gunmen continue to detain more than 50 people, the children were released
The gunmen today abducted 75 people in the southern Philippines, including a number of primary school pupils and their teachers.
This was stated by the AFP agency, which previously reported only 65 abductees.
All 17 children arrested and one teacher were released after eight hours.
The abduction occurred in South Agusan province.
They say that approximately 19 gunmen uses the hostages as a living shield in the escape before the police.
Local negotiators are trying to agree on the delivery of the remaining hostages.
According to the AFP agency, the armed belong to the New People's Navy (NPA), an armed fraction of the Communist Party of the Philippines (CPP).
According to the AP, the police previously pursued the kidnappers due to the violence, whose cause was the adjustment of the accounts between two clans of local families.
In the south of the Philippines in the province of Maguindanao it was because of the recent massacre, in which 57 people were killed, imposed by martial law.
Among the victims of the November 23 massacre, related to the governor’s elections, were 30 journalists.
Due to the suspicion of involvement in the massacre they arrested the governor of this province in the southern Philippines and his father Andal Ampatuan, patriarch of an influential Ampatuan dynasty.
Muslim separatists also operate on Mindanao Island.
However, on Tuesday they renewed the peace dialogues with the Philippine government.
RSA’s story refers to everyone, says Morgan Freeman aka Mandela
In the coming days a new Hollywood film, by Clint Eastwood Invictus (its official title in Czech is Invictus: Neporažený), which captures a part of the biography of former president of the South African Republic Nelson Mandela.
The role of the well-known anti-apartheid fighter is played by Morgan Freeman, whom he chose years ago for Mandela himself.
The second role, that of legendary rugby champion Francoise Pienaar, was played by Matt Damon.
With the premiere they are born from the reactions of critics and the public
Nelson Mandela
His full name is Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela. He was born on July 18, 1918 in Mvezo, South Africa.
In 1988 he received the Sacharov Prize for Freedom of Thought, in 1993 he received, along with Klerk, the Nobel Peace Prize.
He was President of the Republic of South Austria in 1994-19999.
Great hopes, as it comes to the public at the time, when in the Republic of South, preparations for the world championship in football culminate. At the same time the media are shaken by alarming news about the growing racial hatred and discrimination of the inhabitants by the dominant black majority.
According to the AP news agency, the reactions to this new RSA film have been positive, despite numerous reservations against the distribution of the starring roles for the “uninteresting” Hollywood stars.
Morgan Freeman, however, is proud of the new film.
For many years I dreamed of the role and prepared myself well to play it.
Many times I came in person with Nelson Mandela, for - as it is said - to charge his energy, he mentioned in an interview after the festive premiere in Los Angeles.
I think it's good, if through the film we can remember Mandela's character and message.
It’s a history of the Republic of South Africa, but it’s about everyone, he added.
In national rugby team suit
The film is based on British writer John Carlin's book with the title freely translated In the Role of the Enemy: Nelson Mandela and the Game. Thanks to this game the nation was born. It portrays the South African human rights lawyer during his first presidential election term.
Morgan Freeman in the film is trying to unite two groups of inhabitants, racially and for long years apart. Just like Nelson Mandela in 1995, he as the protagonist also puts on the South African national rugby team and enters the playing field. RSA then won the World Cup.
For entering the grass in the suit, which in RSA is considered something almost sacred, Mandela fourteen years ago won great ovations, especially of the white public and the recognition of the white inhabitants.
The football championship will perhaps manage to help people unite and form a nation, said rugby player Chester Williams, the only "black" in the national team in 1995.
It will still be seen, if the tense situation in the RSA improves.
White South Africans however are skeptical, as he said in an interview only for LN a rugby fan Christopher Dawson, who fled their country to Brittany.
The championship begins on June 11, 2010.
In Lounsko a transformer with a value of tens of millions of kroners burns
The fire caught in the morning a transformer of the Čeps company in a switch box near the Výškov village in Lounsko.
According to Lukáš Marvan, the spokesman for firefighters in the Ústí region, the fire is extinguished by six groups.
Previously firefighters wanted to let the transformer be destroyed by the fire, however they later changed their minds and the fire is extinguished with a mixture of water, foam and a special extinguishing matter.
The fire was caused by a technical defect, the transformer short circuit.
There were none injured, according to Pavla Mandátová, the company’s spokeswoman, the breakdown does not jeopardize the safe operation of the distributive system.
The estimated damage is several million kroners, enumerating it exactly won’t be possible before the fire goes out.
According to Mandatová it will be necessary to replace the destroyed transformer with a new one.
During the ten years of Čeps activity nothing similar happened, breakdowns of this type are sporadic, he added.
According to Sona Holingerová Hendrychová, ČEZ society’s spokeswoman for the northern Chequia, electricity supplies were not cut off, and the electric substation fire did not affect the operation of homes, offices or businesses.
A.s. Čeps působí in the territory of the Czech Republic as the exclusive maintainer of the distributional system, of the electrical conduction of 400 kV and 220 kV.
It retains in good condition, renews and develops 39 switch boxes with 67 transformers, which carry the electrical energy from the transmission system to the distribution system, in addition it maintains the electrical conduction of 4339 kilometers long.
Jančura will lodge a complaint for slander to three ČSSD hethmans
The owner of Student Agency Radim Jančura today will file a complaint for slander to three social democratic hetmans.
These are Michal Hašek from the Jihomoravský kraj region, Radko Martinek from Pardubický kraj and the hetman from Olomoucký kraj Martin Tesařík.
Jančura mentioned it in Twenty minutes in Radiožurnál.
Demand has to do with the company’s effort to maintain regional rail transport in some regions.
All regions, however, ended up signing long-term contracts with České dráhy.
All the three hetmans are attacking us and talking about the fact that we are "taking the raisins of the cake", however we brought only the offer for what they themselves wanted.
They affirm that we are more expensive than České dráhy, which is not true. We know the prices of České dráhy, which were set last week.
So there's nothing left but to defend ourselves.
It's because of the slander towards the Student Agency society, he told Radiožurnál Jančura.
He mentioned at the same time having the tests of all the regions. According to him they have not taken necessary measures in harmony with European law.
That's why it's preparing an incentive for the European Commission.
Hašek considers this denunciation for the continuation of Mr. Jančura's medial show, which serves as free advertising for his company and himself.
Naturally I am prepared to defend my good reputation and that of my region and I appeal to the other Hetmans to do so as well.
Suppose, also on this occasion it will be clearly demonstrated that Mr Jančura manipulates the public in the matter of regional railway transport, and uses liar and false arguments, Hašek said.
Jančura announced last week that it would invest several million kroners in advertising, which would aim to warn of these measures in the regions.
Radiožurnál told him that he would interrupt the campaign after Christmas and he wants to continue it after the New Year.
He said that the campaign will be carried out against corruption as such.
Jančura started the campaign after the regions agreed with České dráhy to maintain regional rail links and for the commission of approximately 150 billion kroners did not summon an tender.
According to Jančura, similar steps are at odds with the law and the opinion of the European Court.
Jančura claims to have been threatened by an ČSSD MP, due to his criticism of railway transport in the regions.
The only threat was that if we don't leave it, we can lose bus licenses.
He introduced himself as deputy of ČSSD, I know, what man it is, I am not going to say his name, said Jančura today for Radiožurnál.
Pneumococal vaccine will be free and voluntary
Pneumococal vaccine from January will put an end to usual customs.
As a first vaccine it must be free for families and at the same time voluntary.
However, opinions already appear, that the mandatory vaccine would be more effective.
Preparations for the introduction of the free pneumococcal vaccine culminate.
The Institute of Medicines is already setting maximum vaccine prices, which will be covered by insurance companies.
Experts also already have it clear in the vaccination procedure.
The New Vaccine
Vaccines for children born in August
Children born since August 2, last year will be able to receive the pneumococal vaccine free of charge.
The condition is to have not received a dose of the vaccine before.
Those who started the vaccine last year or earlier, have to cover other doses of the vaccine as well.
The first dose of the vaccine has to be received between the third and fifth month's age.
Last year a quarter of the families voluntarily let their children vaccinated against pneumococci.
The parents, however, paid for a dose 1600 kronors and more.
To vaccinate younger children, four doses are needed.
Experts generally estimate that insurance companies will spend on vaccines from 300 to 450 thousand kronor annually.
However, they will save treatment, which is needed for unvaccinated children.
So far the state spent for the entire vaccination of children approximately 500 thousand annually.
Covered vaccination was initiated by the so-called Janota package.
Pneumococcus is the cause of otitis media, but also of serious infections including meningitis and septicemia.
Of pneumococal infections in our country every year 28 children not older than 10 years of age die according to research in hospitals.
In addition to the Czech, pneumocochal vaccine coated in children was introduced in approximately 40 countries.
Is the vaccine mandatory?
However at the same time opinions appear, that this vaccine could be mandatory in the future.
From January it must be covered by insurance and voluntary companies.
The mandatory vaccine would be better, said Rudolf Kalovský, the head of the Nahlas civic association.
Its association has long propagated pneumocochal vaccines.
Experts at an international conference, in which our association participated, were not completely satisfied with the fact that we have the voluntary vaccine.
It's not completely ideal.
On the other hand we are happy to achieve at least this.
I think that this is the path that we now have to undertake, Kalovský said.
The Ministry of Health however does not want to change the vaccination procedure against pneumococci.
At the moment we do not think about it at all, said its spokesman Vlastimil Sršeň.
Voluntary vaccine has its disadvantages, doctors say
The president of the society of pediatricians Odborná společnost praktických dětských lékařů Hana Cabrnochová recognizes that the voluntary vaccine has its disadvantages.
If all the children would receive the vaccine, it will be possible to reason about the reduction of the number of doses. "In the situation, when the vaccine costs such a high amount, we relatively understand the political decision to have started the vaccination as voluntary," says Cabrnochová.
Experts hope that from next year more than 80 percent of young parents will let their babies vaccinate against pneumococci.
Pneumococal vaccine is also a big change because as the first vaccination in children is covered by health companies, not by the state.
Insurance companies should spend more money on prophylaxis, to later save on treatment, Cabrnochová explains.
Doctors will have to buy vaccines for themselves.
Pediatricians, however, are concerned about buying vaccines.
Previously, vaccines had to be purchased by insurance companies and distributed to doctors, just as when the state gives them other vaccines.
Some MPs, however, were afraid that the insurance companies could "take the money out of health insurance."
Doctors will have to buy the vaccines and wait, until they are paid by the insurance companies.
It is estimated that pediatricians will invest in vaccines 40 thousand per month, which is a remarkable sum.
It won't be easy, says Cabrnochová.
The son of an American artist involved "in the theft of a museum"
Renowned American fantasy artist Frank Frazetta’s son has been accused of trying to steal paintings valued at 20 million U.S. dollars (12 million pounds) from his father’s museum.
Pennsylvania police say Alfonso Frank Frazetta was trapped while carrying 90 frames in his vehicle and trailer.
They claim that Frazetta and another man used an excavator to break into the museum in the Pocono Mountains region.
Frazetta father, 81, is famous for his portrayal of characters such as Conan the Barbarian and Tarzan.
The Associated Press reported that the artist was in Florida at the time of the incident.
AP reported that an unidentified police official claimed that a family dispute may have motivated young Frazetta.
The agency reported that Frazatta son, 52, testified to police that his father had ordered him "to enter the museum by any means to move all the paintings to a deposit."
The agency said Frazetta father denied granting such permission.
Labour defends tax increases in the budget
Ministers have defended tax increases and spending cutting in the pre-budget report, in the face of criticism from the opposition, businesses and trade unions.
Conservatives said Alistair Darling "wasted" an opportunity to show his seriousness about the deficit cut, delaying decisions until after the election.
The Finance Minister also received harsh criticism for harming low- and middle-income workers.
Among its main proposals are an increase of 0.5% of Social Security spending and a cap of 1 % in public sector wage agreements from 2011.
Cholera for Social Security
Unions have lamented that low-wage workers are punished with a recession generated by others, and warned about future “problems.”
The increase in Social Security spending, which will grow about three billion pounds per year, has angered the business community, which claims this is a tax on jobs, when the focus should be on economic recovery.
The increase, limited to those earning more than £20,000 per year, will affect approximately 10 million workers.
KEY POINTS OF PRE-BUDGET REPORT
Social Security with an additional 0.5% increase, from April 2011
Economy will contract more than expected 4.75% this year
New 50 percent tax on bankers’ bonds
The increase of 1 point of corporate tax for small firms was ruled out
Tax Breakdowns for Electric Cars and Wind Turbines
The state pension would increase by 2.5%
According to estimates from the Ministry of Finance, a person earning £30,000 will lose £90 a year and whoever earns £40,000 will lose £190, while a person earning £10,000 per year will earn £110.
Ministers said their goal of halving the deficit by 2013 implied "difficult decisions", but insisted that 60% of the supplementary tax burden would fall on 5% of the highest wages.
Financy Minister Stephen Timms denied that tax hikes and delineated spending cuts were a “grain of sand in the desert” compared to what was needed.
"These are big figures. It will allow us to cut the deficit by half, which is absolutely essential over the next four years," he told the BBC.
Mr. Timms said he had confidence that the economy would grow again by the end of the year.
However, the Labour party’s forecasts regarding future economic growth, which will influence the amount to be borrowed and how quickly the deficit is reduced, has been questioned after the Minister of Finances was forced to modify the first figures.
In his statement, he stated that the economy would likely contract by 4.75% this year, much worse than the 3.5% decrease forecast in April, while the order for loans would be £3 billion above what was previously estimated.
"Wasted Opportunity"
The Conservatives, who will launch a new advertising campaign on Thursday, warning about the Labour party’s “debt crisis,” they said the loan application projection of £789 billion for the next six years was unsustainable.
Ministers had failed to present a credible plan on how they would return the money, adding, "cynically" dodging the difficult options until after the elections to be held in June 2010.
"They haven't included anything meaningful about what they're going to do," Finance Ministry Minister Philip Hammond said. "They had the opportunity to do so and have wasted it."
“All major announcements will be made after the election, whoever wins
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
The Labour party insistes that schools, hospitals and the police will be protected from future cuts, unlike conservative proposals, which would seek a further reduction of the deficit in the shorter term.
But the Democratic liberals claimed that the money raised by tax hikes and spending cuts would be used to sustain next year’s spending, not to reduce the deficit, arguing that the bills were like “sandy castles.”
BBC political editor Nick Robinson said the timing chosen for the announcements, such as rising inflation from some incapacity subsidies next April, mentioned above, would leave the finance minister exposed to accusations of election manoeuvres, as the money could simply be recovered the following year.
However, the Labour party insists that people are being provided more in need and the situation will be reviewed in September 2010.
"False budget"
Although ministers did not publish post-2011 spending data, economists said it was clear that the adjustment of public spending would only be felt in the medium term.
The respected Institute of Fiscal Studies said the Finance Minister’s figures involved substantial cuts in many areas, potentially including transportation, higher education, science and advocacy in the future.
“This is really deferring the pain for later,” its director, Robert Chote, said.
"All major announcements will come after the election, whoever wins," added Roger Bootle, economic consultant for Deloitte's accountants.
"This has been the false pre-budget report. Markets realize this."
FIB inquires about "American arrests in Pakistan"
The FBI is investigating the arrest in Pakistan of five U.S. men suspected of extremist connections.
The U.S. embassy in Pakistan told the BBC that the men were arrested in a raid on a house in Sarghoda, eastern Punjab province.
The FBI has said it is investigating whether they are the same men who disappeared from their homes in the state of Virginia, U.S., last month.
The U.S. Foreign Ministry said it was also seeking information about the men.
It is reported that three of them are of Pakistani descent, one of Egyptian heritage and the other of Yemeni origin.
"If they're U.S. citizens, of course we're going to be very interested in the charges they've been arrested for and under what circumstances they're being held," spokesman Ian Kelly said.
FBI spokeswoman Katherine Schweit said the agency was aware of the detentions and was in contact with the families of the missing students.
"We are working with the Pakistani authorities to determine their identities and the nature of their business there, if in effect these are the students who were missing," the spokeswoman said.
The Pakistani Embassy in Washington said the men were detained in a house belonging to the uncle of one of them.
He said the house was already in the crosshairs of the local police and that no charges had yet been filed against the detainees.
Foreign Minister Hillary Clinton declined to comment on the arrests, the Reuters news agency reported, but said the U.S. had to "work more closely with both Afghanistan and Pakistan, to try to eradicate the terrorism infrastructure that continues to recruit and train people."
The families of the five students reported his disappearance in Northern Virginia in late November.
Amnesty International Condemns Iranian ‘Abuses’
Human rights in Iran over the past 20 years have always been equally deficient, according to a report by campaign group Amnesty International.
The report details "the models of abuse" of the regime, before and after the presidential elections held in June.
One man quoted in the report said he had been beaten and burned with cigarettes. Another said they threatened to rape him.
Iran has rejected precedent criticism of its human rights record.
Officials have said such criticisms have a political basis.
Thousands arrested and dozens killed in Iran after President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad’s election sparked the largest street protests since the 1979 Islamic Revolution.
Dozens have been convicted, and prosecutors say at least five people have been sentenced to death.
BBC correspondent in Tehran, Jon Leyne, who is now in London, says that during the protests, Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei acknowledged some accusations of abuse, ordering the closure of Kahrizak's detention centre.
But since then, the authorities have shown very little tolerance for criticism, according to our correspondent.
"False Confession"
Amnesty International cited the account of Ebrahim Mehtari, a 26-year-old computer student, who said he was accused of “working with Facebook’s networks” and protesting the election outcome.
"Often they hit me in the face" in their words.
"They burned me with cigarettes under my eyes, on my neck, my head... They threatened to execute me and humiliated me."
Amnesty said that after five days he signed a fake confession and was released and abandoned on the street, still bleeding and semi-conscious.
In August, defeated presidential candidate Mehdi Karroubi said some protesters detained after the election had been tortured to death in prison, and others had been raped.
Iranian officials denied accusations of rape, but admitted that there had been abuses.
Amnesty also cited the case of a former detainee, who said he was held in a container along with 75 others for more than eight weeks at a detention centre in Kahrizak.
Amnesty acknowledged that the Iranian parliament and the judiciary had established committees to investigate post-election unrest and the government’s response, but said the mandate and powers of the agencies were confusing and the parliamentary committee’s inquiries had not been made public.
The group said at least 90 people had been detained in the past three weeks to prevent further demonstrations.
A ski slope was made in the city park
More than 100 tonnes of artificial snow were used to turn a Bristol site of interest into a competition track.
The Track at the Park event attracted 16 semi-professional skiers and snowboarders who competed for cash prizes.
At the top of the slope at Brandon Hill Park was a short jumping ramp, facing a 100-meter-long runway.
John Hickman, a fourth-year medical student, said there was a beautiful view from the top of the track, but that it was quite frightening.
You can see it under a beautiful view of the city lights twinkling.
Here you’ll find some of county’s best skiers and snowboarders, true talent.”
The awards were given to the best tricks or maneuvers carried out on the track.
After Thursday afternoon’s event, the snow will be allowed to melt.
Still them and us
Can Islam Be French?
Pluralism and pragmatism in a secular state.
At a time when Swiss voters have called for banning the construction of minarets and that there is widespread warning about the alleged Islamization of Europe, John Bowen, an American academic, has written a detailed and studied report on the possible integration of Muslims (which are actually being integrated) into one of the continent’s most opently secular societies.
Some readers will come to this new book as admirers of the author’s latest work “Why the French Don’t Like Headcarves” (2006), an elegant and detailedly argued study of a problem that divided and worried the country for a decade and a half, and whose effects are still perceived today.
Mr. Bowen’s latest book has a broader and more ambitious perspective.
As a good anthropologist, he wants to know not only what politicians and the media are saying about Islam in France, but what’s really happening.
He has spent months in mosques, schools and institutes that now provide France’s 5-6 million Muslims with what Mr. Bowen calls “Islamic spaces.”
He is a good listener who reproduces debates between teachers and students about the questions they are most interested in.
Should a Muslim get married in a mosque or in a town hall (or both)?
Should they teach young Muslims about evolution and gay rights?
Can a Muslim woman marry a non-Muslimus man?
Is it legitimate for a Muslim to use an interest-based banking system to obtain a mortgage?
It is these seemingly mundane questions, he argues, things of daily life, that concern the media more than political dramas.
The author identifies a new generation of imams, professors and intellectuals, none of them renowned, with the possible exception of Tariq Ramadan, an academic and Arab Muslim scholar born in Switzerland.
This new generation tries to open up the debate on how to be at the same time a good Muslim and a good citizen in a modern secular society.
They can't spread this argument entirely.
Conservatives doubt only the idea of French or European Islam.
The thinkers and activists that Mr. Bowen interviews tend to disagree with his counterparts from Salafi, supporters of the purist Sunni Islam associated with Saudi Arabia, who currently have a small but influential presence among Muslims in Europe.
Bowen thinks Muslim values and French secularism could be compatible.
But adaptation requires mutual concessions.
He questions the extent to which French political strategists (and the intellectual elite who so fiercely defend secularism) are truly committed to pluralism.
It suggests that Muslims probably have more unfair treatment than Catholics, Protestants and Jews, who have also had to make their historical commitments to secular republicanism.
More than an increasing pragmatism, it perceives “an adjustment of values.”
Can Islam be French? After reading this book, one tends to say “Yes, but not yet.”
The world's most bleaking landscape
The Arctic is changing faster and more dramatically than any other habitat on the planet.
The ice that defines it is melting at an alarming rate, taking with it the life that cannot survive anywhere else.
The interests of oil, gas, transportation and fishing have focused on recently open water, with diplomats, lawyers and now authors, as a result of this.
In "On Thin Ice" Richard Ellis, a writer and illustrator, paints a natural story of the northern icon, the polar bear.
Knowing of the complicated history and politics of whaling, he describes the long tradition of Arctic explorers, who gave evidence of value confronting the white bear.
Admiral Nelson’s encounter as a courageous 14-year-old cadet, struggling only with the end of his musket, is surely a myth, but others are true.
The young, captured as they swam following the bodies of their newly exterminated mothers, were trapped and sent to zoos and circuses.
A circus presentation in the early 20th century exhibited 75 polar bears at the same time.
Even today, zoo enclosures are still generally one-millionth of a wild adult’s area. The climate is almost never adequate: a polar bear residing in Singapore turned green by algae that grow within the hairless areas of their fur.
Click here to find out more!
Ellis resorts to stories from other writers and historians, and often returns to the threat that hunters present to the survival of species.
Although banned in Norway, U.S. and Russia, the killing continues in Greenland and Canada, where helicopter hunters and Eskimo Indians on snowmobiles use high-power assault rifles to bring down their prey.
Ironically, it is in the waters to the north of those two hunting nations where the ice will last longer.
In his new book, “After the Ice,” Alun Anderson, a former New Scientist editor, offers a clear, chilling account of Arctic science and an overwhelming look at how that place’s future can be transformed.
It’s not that scientists have all the answers.
Neither atmospheric scientists nor oceanographers can adequately explain the speed of changes.
It's not for lack of commitment.
Fridtjof Nansen’s pioneering voyages in the late 19th and early 20th centuries gave indications for the first time about the displacement of ice masses.
Then cold-war submarines plotted a map of ice-laned laces circulating beneath the masses of ice.
Satellite scans and extensive series of iceberg-mounted probes are a useful supplement, but rugged cold-camping individuals still perform many tasks.
Mr. Anderson looks into the extraordinary, tiny world of the affluent system within Arctic ice, formed by splashes of saltwater that is compressed as it freezes.
But from the bear on the surface to the microscopic wonders of the interior, they’re all doomed once the summer ice retreats, what’s thought to happen sometime between 2013 and 2050.
In a few months... these could be us
Marines among the 6,00 who place krones on Arlington's tombs
19-year-old De'Angello Robinson traveled seven hours by bus to place a single wreath in the tomb of a soldier he had never met.
For him and the other Marines who traveled from Camp Johnson in Jacksonville, N.C., to Arlington National Cemetery to decorate graves, it was a day to pay tribute to men and women who were once standing in their boots.
In a few months, from where we're being boarded, these could be us, Robinson said about the fallen soldiers.
If it's us, I'd want someone to do the same for me, therefore I'm just trying to show respect.
He was one of more than 6,000 volunteers who gathered Saturday morning to place crowns on the tombs of veterans in various sections of Arlington Cemetery.
In 1992, Morrill Worcester, owner of the Worcester Crown Company in Harrington, Maine, kicked off the event when he and others decided to decorate several hundred tombs in Arlington Cemetery.
In what is already an 18-year tradition, Morrill and his wife Karen make the trip every year stopping in different cities along the way, to officiate events dedicated to the military and victims of terrorism.
The Worcesters also founded a non-profit organization, Wreaths Across America, which has extended the event to other states.
This year volunteers placed more than 16,000 krones on the tombs of Arlington Cemetery, areas in the Pentagon, tombs at Fayetteville National Cemetery in Arkansas, Battery Park in New York City, and at the United Airlines Flight 93 memorial site in Shanksville, Pennsylvania.
For the second year, the Walmart Foundation donated more than US$150,000 to purchase and transport the kroners.
Charleen Hunt, 70, of Westminster, said she made the trip to Arlington in honor of her husband, a late-year-old career soldier.
As long as her husband is not buried in Arlington, Hunt said this was her way of giving retribution to those who made sacrifices.
This is a modest way civilians can support our troops, he said.
It's like visiting a family tomb.
Wrapped in layers of warm clothing, the volunteers walked through the cemetery's wide meadows and hills.
The volunteers headed to older graves of World War I, World War II, and the Vietnam War, because they don’t get as many visits as the new tombs, the organizers said.
The atmosphere was cheerful while the children played and the pairs held hands, while carrying the fresh pine to the tombstones.
Each circular crown was of a deep green, with a small red bow tied at the top.
Nearby, Section 60, where most veterans of the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan are buried, were virtually silent.
The United Service Organizations donated 1,000 kroners to decorate the section, but the bleak atmosphere of the current wars invaded the area.
People cried openly, others prayed, and families and friends leaned against each other in the glacial air.
Some read aloud names sculpted in the stones, while others leaned to see small black and white plates placed inside the wet mud, for those whose gravestones have not yet been completed.
Sandra Lockwood was one of many mothers who wiped the tears out of her eyes in front of her son’s grave.
He drove eight hours from Zanesfield, Ohio, to visit the tomb of navy gunner Sgto. David Shane Spicer, who died in combat in July.
No one should ever forget why we're free . . my son paid for it, he said.
“Long after my death I want someone to remember it.”
Cuban officials have arrested a U.S. government contractor
The Cuban government has detained a U.S. government contractor circulating mobiles and laptops in the country, State Department officials said Saturday.
The contractor, who has not yet been identified, works for Bethesda-based Development Alternatives Inc.
The company works on projects for clients such as the U.S. International Development Agency and the World Bank.
Consular officers along with the U.S. Interest Section in Havana are seeking access to the detainee, who was arrested Dec. 5.
Specific charges have not been made public, albeit under Cuban law, a Cuban citizen or a foreign visitor may be detained for almost anything that is deemed "dangered."
All so-called counter-revolutionary activities, which include mild protests and critical writings, pose the risk of arrest.
Anti-government graffiti and speeches are considered serious crimes.
Cuba has a fledgling blogger community, led by popular commentator Yoani Sanchez, who often writes about how she and her husband are followed and harassed by government agents.
Sanchez has repeatedly requested permission to leave the country to accept prizes, but permission has been denied.
The detention of a U.S. contractor may likely provoke tensions between the Castro brothers’ communist government in Cuba and the Obama administration, which has been seeking a “slow-paced” rapprochement to improve relations with the island.
The New York Times was the first to release news of the arrest Friday night.
The new U.S. policy emphasizes that if the Cuban government takes concrete steps, such as the release of political prisoners and the creation of more space for the opposition, there will be reciprocity on behalf of the United States.
Mobile phones and laptops are legal in Cuba, even if they are new and coveted products in a country where the average salary of a government worker is 15 dollars per month.
The Cuban government granted ordinary citizens the right to buy mobile phones only this year; they are mostly used for texting, as a 15-minute phone conversation would cost daily salary.
The use of the Internet is extremely limited on the island.
It is available in expensive hotels and for foreign visitors to the hotel, and in some government establishment, such as universities.
Cubans who want to connect often have to give their names to the government.
Access to many websites is restricted.
An American’s detention in Cuba is unusual.
Gloria Berbena, a press officer in the U.S. Interest Section in Havana, said most of the handful of U.S. citizens in prison in Cuba are behind bars for crimes such as drug trafficking.
Berbena said he could not provide further information on the arrest.
Arrest and detention are clearly misguided.
An activity that in any other open society would be legal, such as handing out free phones, in Cuba is a crime, said José Miguel Vivanco, director of the program of the Americas of the organization Human Rights Watch, who recently published a harsh report on freedoms in Cuba, called "Nuevo Castro, same Cuba", a reference to Raul Castro's location as leader of the country, to replace his sick older brother Fidel.
Vivanco said the defendants are often detained, prosecuted and jailed in one day.
He said that any solution would probably be political, and that the Cuban government often provokes a negative reaction in the United States, just as the two countries begin to have more dialogue.
Pakish officials unravel a plot to send men to Afghanistan
On Saturday, Pakistani authorities neutralized the alleged mastermind of a plot to send five men from Northern Virginia to Afghanistan to kill U.S. troops, saying they hope the case could help unravel an extensive network of terrorist recruiters, who roam the Internet in search of radicalized youth.
Investigators said they were chasing a mysterious insurgent figure, known as Saifullah, who invited the men to Pakistan after discovering that one of them made comments approving terrorist attacks on internet video site YouTube.
Saifullah led the men once they were in Pakistan, trying to help them reach the remote area in Pakistan’s tribal belt, which is a familiar place for al Qaeda and its terrorist training camps.
But a Pakistani intelligence official who had been briefed on the case said Saturday that Saifullah was unable to convince al Qaeda commanders that the men were not part of a CIA plot to infiltrate the terrorist network.
Consequently, they were isolated for several days in the eastern city of Sargodha, away from the imposing mountains of the northwest, which have become a terrorist paradise.
They were considered part of a covert operation.
For this reason they were rejected, said the official, who spoke from anonymity due to the delicacy of the case.
The official said the men didn’t flinch and were even trying to get proper approval to access al Qaeda camps, when Pakistani law enforcement authorities arrested them.
The case of the five men, who remain in Pakistan and are being questioned by the FBI, underscores the critical role of recruiters in identifying potential terrorists and, perhaps more importantly, determining those who are trusted.
Since the Sept. 11, 2001 attacks, U.S. intelligence has prioritized trying to infiltrate people into al Qaeda.
The recruiters of the organization act as guards, not letting pass those who do not demonstrate a serious commitment to the holy war, and those who could be spies.
Analysts said al Qaeda looks with special attention to aspiring American recruits.
But for the organization they represent enormous appeal, due to their potential to have access to U.S. targets and because of their propaganda value.
But Evan Kohlmann, a senior analyst for the U.S.-based NEFA Foundation, said terrorist groups have also become much more cautious in recent years regarding those they admit, because U.S. intelligence agencies have become experts in their recruitment methods.
“If you’re trying to infiltrate someone into these groups, what better way than to follow the recruitment model how many they’ve already followed?” said Kohlmann.
It has been transformed into a web-based model.
Tens of thousands protest in Copenhagen demand climate "action"
On Saturday, tens of thousands of protesters marched here through the streets demanding negotiators working at the city’s Bella Center Convention Center, boldest climate actions.
Demonstrators said 100,000 people participated in the event, but police estimated that the amount was closer to 25,000.
The event was relatively peaceful, though a handful of masked activists placed small explosives near a group of government buildings in the city centre.
In a day in which little happened in the UN-sponsored climate talks, thousands of activists walked around the city holding banners in English that said "There is not a planet B" and one in Spanish that declared, "Earth says "it's enough."
Several celebrities joined the protest, including the Danish model and photographer Helena Christensen, who said that trips Peru, her mother’s home country, made me aware of the distressing issues the country must deal with due to the impacts of climate changes that are already happening.”
This is part of the reason why I have decided to join the great demonstration, to spread the word and appeal to the leaders of the world, to present a just, ambitious and binding agreement, he said.
This isn’t an easy task, but you have to do it, there’s no way to circumvent it any longer.
Police prevented protesters from getting too close to the Bella Center, and said they had arrested 19 people, mainly because of wearing masks or wearing pocket knives.
Under Danish law, activities are prohibited during demonstrations.
According to a witness, who asked not to be identified for being implicated in the climate talks, activists wearing sports masks and black outfits placed several explosives near Copenhagen’s main channel, which is close to several ministries.
They were through the buildings, he said, adding that they started as flares, but were followed by “a couple of large explosions.”
Within the convention center, people gathered around TV screens to watch the march all afternoon.
But the protest didn’t seem to penetrate the consciousness of key officials like Su Wei, China’s chief negotiator on climate.
When asked if he thought the demonstration was having a constructive impact on international deliberations, he replied in English, “I wasn’t really aware of the situation.”
Then he went on in Mandarin, "as the meeting place is great, I can't hear what's going on outside."
He pointed out that it depended on everyone’s perspective to define whether the gait was being detrimental or beneficial.
He said that this shows the attention of the general public and different sectors to the issue of climate change.
On the other hand, "it can also be said that they affect negotiations, or the freedom of other people.
The data from a test reveals persistent racial differences
Last week’s federal gradebook on math performance was very good news for Capital District public schools.
Although the District is still far from the country’s best-performing systems, the gradebook showed that fourth- and eighth-grade students struggled at a faster pace over the past two years, compared to the cities of Atlanta, Chicago and New York.
But what’s recorded in the recent National Education Progress Assessment figures is the lingering achievement difference between African-American students and white students, both locally and nationally.
The average scores of D.C. fourth-grade white students over the past two years grew from 262 to 270 (on a scale of 500), but their African-American peers increased only three points, from 209 to 212.
In fact, the performance difference grew between 2007 and 2009, from 53 to 58 points.
The progress of African-Americans in the eighth grade remained essentially fixed, lowering a statistically insignificant point, from 245 to 244.
Average target scores were not included in the test results, because the sample size was not large enough.
Over a six-year extension, the picture is no more encouraging.
The difference that separated fourth-grade white and black students in 2003, when the first ENPE was unveiled in the District, it was 60 points on the scale (262-202).
Although the scores achieved by the children in both groups have increased during this period, the difference has barely dropped to 58.
Some education attorneys in the District expressed concern last week, as the advances made by Rector Michelle A. El Rhee and Mayor Adrian M. Fenty (Democrat), were largely driven by white students, who are already very good students.
This would suggest that we’ve improved the set by catering to those at the top of the scale, which is problematic and conflicting, said Jeff Smith, executive director of D.C. Voice, a nonprofit that advocates educational equity in the District.
I’m not jumping for joy for a difference of two or three points, said D.C. Council member Kwame R. Brown (Dem.—For accumulation), who has a daughter in the fourth grade of the Eaton Elemental.
Brown, an assiduous critic of Rhee’s management style, said the message of test scores is that the city’s high schools have a desperate need for attention.
Clearly you always want to see the positive signs, and I respect it.
But what’s worrying is that we’re not devoting the time or energy necessary for our middle schools.
Rhee said the District needs to continue to find better ways to address the needs of underperforming students.
This fall, for example, some teachers are being trained to use a new reading resume, the Wilson Reading System, aimed at elementary and middle grade students, who did not master the fundamentals of early reading in their school courses and are considerably behind their peers.
Everyday’s mathematics, a math resume from kindergarten to sixth grade, which emphasizes real-life games and experiences, that was introduced into the District under former Superintendent Clifford Janey, which is credited with some progress reported by the ENPE last week.
I just think we need to keep working to deliver the best interventions to those students who are below grade level, Rhee said.
Others say the ENPE’s results highlight the question of whether Rhee can continue to increase the overall performance of the system and still provide additional money and resources for low-performing schools, which will be needed to make a difference.
Here’s a kind of rugged dilemma for Michelle Rhee, said Bruce Fuller, an education professor at the University of California at Berkeley, who studies urban test scores.
The solution of conventional policy is to allocate its resources and administration reforms to schools located in the poorest sections of D.C.
Then the objective can generate political repercussions that go against that important schedule.
But the problem, of course, is that, like other urban superintendents, she tries to cling to the black and white middle class.
Our focus is on ensuring that we built a system of excellent schools.
We have to strengthen our neighborhood schools, so that all families, regardless of where they live, have confidence that their children can access an excellent public education.
Janey, now superintendent of Newark schools, said in an interview this week that an essential element to end the performance difference is lengthening the year of traditional public school, currently 180 days in the District, to compete with charter schools.
Almost 200 days must be reached to have the force of change, said Janey, who reached an agreement with her teachers’ union last year in an 185-day year, which she considers as an “indicator” to seek a greater increase in the next deal.
Making higher education a part of more futures
The sightseeing bus arrived at Gettysburg University with a strong resolve.
Graciela Rodríguez, 12, descended and blinked for a moment before the white columns, brick facades and emerald meadows.
Graciela’s parents had barely graduated from a high school in El Salvador.
Until recently, Graciela herself, who was born in Silver Spring and lives in Riverdale, had never set foot on a college campus.
Even so, when she and the group’s other eighth-graders explored Gettysburg, where tuition costs US$38,690 a year, her attitude was less that of impressed guests than that of enthusiastic potential students.
Here's where I'll be! exclaimed Graciela, who would like to study medicine, when the guide announced that they had entered the science building.
Fanay, really, he said thoughtfully, when the guide talked about the low teacher-student ratio of the school.
The University of Pennsylvania was the 7th that the children had visited on their three-day journey, and they had already completely absorbed their deliberate message: The question is not whether you’re going to college.
The question is to which.
Although there is growing concern about the large number of U.S.-born children of Hispanic immigrants who drop out of high school or become pregnant in adolescence, there are also hundreds of thousands who access the college education they need to access the middle class.
In fact, one in five of these “second-generation” Hispanics graduate from college, a remarkable achievement given that many of their immigrant parents, especially Mexicans and Central Americans, entered the U.S. without finishing high school.
Their success stories are important, the researchers say, because they indicate the way forward for a generation that will play a major role in the country’s workforce.
Those who look at higher-yielders say they often have a natural affinity for school and an innate tendency to succeed.
Many also have parents who set high targets for their children and find ways to compensate for their unfamiliarity with U.S. schools.
But tutoring programs can also play an exceptional role in helping Graciela and millions of children like her get to college, particularly if efforts are sustained over time.
Looking at low-income children, whose parents don’t have the experience and skills to help them navigate the system, it follows that any intervention at any time won’t fix it, said Patricia Gándara, a researcher at the University of California, Davis, who has examined Latino students.
We have to think about providing a support network for these children, from kindergarten to high school.
The federal program that funded Graciela’s college tour is a useful example.
Known as GEAR UP, it provides more than US$300 million per year to local school systems, to run college preparation programs, which begin when low-income students are in middle school and continue to finish high school.
Since 1999, the program has helped more than 10 million students, with more than 60 percent frequenting the university, according to the U.S. Department of Education.
Iraqi Oil Ministry Reaches Deals with 10 Foreign Oil Companies
Despite concerns about violence and political instability, the Iraqi government managed to attract major oil companies to rebuild its fragile infrastructure, during two auctions that concluded on Saturday.
The 10 agreements that the Iraqi oil ministry reached with foreign oil companies suggest that China, Russia and European oil companies are poised to play a major role in restorating Iraq’s oil industry, maimed by decades of war and sanctions.
U.S
Seven U.S. companies had paid to participate in the second auction, which began Friday.
The only one who submitted an offer lost.
Two U.S. companies reached deals for auctioned deposits in June.
Poor representation of U.S. oil giants at the opening of Iraq’s oil industry shocked analysts.
Iraq finally opened its doors after six years of war, and instead of U.S. companies, there are Asians and Europeans in the lead, said Ruba Husari, editor of Iraq’s Petroleum Forum, an online news center.
It’ll be a long time before something else is on offer in Iraq.
Security concerns, accentuated by massive coordinated bombings on Tuesday, and political instability as the U.S. military pulls out, likely prevented U.S. oil companies from risking more forcefully in Iraq, which has the world's third-largest crude reserve, analysts said.
U.S. companies were in some cases at a disadvantage, because rivals, particularly Chinese and other government-controlled energy companies, have considerably lower labor costs and are more likely to take risks because they don’t respond to shareholders.
Exxon-Mobil and Occidental Petroleum Inc were the only U.S. companies that reached agreements with the ministry.
Major U.S. firms, such as Chevron and ConocoPhillips, which have tightened ties with the Iraqi Oil Ministry and provided technical advice in recent years, drifted away empty-handed.
The Russian companies Lukoil and Gazprom were the main protagonists in two of the contracts awarded this weekend.
The National Petroleum Corporation, owned by the Chinese state, offered in more contracts than any other company and achieved large investments in contracts for two main sites.
We know that China is on track to become a major economic and technological power, Oil Ministry spokesman Assam Jihad said.
We are confident that the Chinese company will be on par with its competitors and comply with its commitments towards Iraq.
Pre-selected companies to submit proposals made bids that were then confronted with the barrel grant the ministry was willing to pay to increase production above the current levels at each site.
Al Qaeda's main planner has apparently been killed in Pakistan
An alleged U.S. missile attack on Pakistan's border with Afghanistan is believed to have killed a top al Qaeda operations planner this week, U.S. counterterrorism officials said Friday.
If confirmed, this would be the second deadly attack on a high-ranking terrorist leader this fall.
Saleh al-Somalí was one of two Arab men thought to have died Tuesday when a pair of missiles hit his car near the city of Miran Shah in North Waziristan province, according to U.S. sources and Pakistani officials in the region.
Local authorities said the missiles were launched by an unidentified, CIA-operated aircraft inside Pakistan’s illegal tribal zone.
They were driving a white car, heading towards the Afghan border, when the car received the impact, an official from Pakistan’s civilian intelligence agency said, speaking on the phone from Miran Shah.
The official said the suspected local militants rushed to the scene and quickly confiscated what was left of the "totally shattered bodies."
Local authorities were unable to verify the identities of the victims, but two U.S. counterterrorism officials cited unspecified evidence that Somali was among the dead.
Somali was described as a high-ranking military planner from al Qaeda, who led the terrorist group’s operations outside the Afghanistan-Pakistan region.
He devoted himself to conspiring around the world, said a senior official, who spoke from anonymity, citing the sensitive nature of U.S. air strikes within Pakistani territory.
Considering its central role, this likely included conspiracy attacks against the United States and Europe.
He had strategic guidance from al Qaeda’s upper command and translated it into operational patterns for potential terrorist attacks.
The second U.S. official said Somali had quickly ascended the ranks of al Qaeda and was well-connected with other extremist groups in the region.
It may not be a well-known name for some Americans, but that doesn't in any way diminish the threat it posed to us and our allies, the second official said.
If his death is confirmed, Somali would be the second leader of al Qaeda or Taliban leader killed since September, when a similar attack killed Najmuddin Jalolov, the leader of a militant faction in the tribal area, and three other high-ranking agents.
The pace of CIA-led non-piled aircraft strikes has slowed since the summer, from an average of about six operations a month-to-two, according to an account of The Long War Journal, a website run by a non-profit organization.
The decline is perhaps due to an improved tactic by terrorist groups, which have taken steps to limit their vulnerability while ruthlessly killing suspected whistleblowers, the site said.
U.N. maximum envoy in Kabul about to resign
U.N. top envoy in Afghanistan Kai Eide said on Friday he would step down from his post in March, endining a tumultuous termination that was harmed by accusations of widespread corruption in the U.N.-backed Afghan presidential election.
Eide’s departure occurs when the Obama administration has decided to send an additional 30,000 U.S. troops to Afghanistan.
The U.N. envoy said he supports the increase, but is concerned that the U.S. schedule for military withdrawal that begins in 18 months will prompt other NATO governments to withdraw their forces.
We need to expedite the readiness of the Afghan security forces and send the right signal to Afghans so that they can trust the international community, Eide said in a telephone interview from Kabul.
Committance needs to be long-term.
The Norwegian diplomat also pressured the U.S. and other military powers to increase the number of international civilian officials to collaborate with Afghanistan's political transition.
The increase in the military sector needs to be reflected in the civilian sector, he said.
Eide said he was not resigning, but simply delivering on a promise he made to his family in March 2008, to spend just two years in Kabul.
He said he wanted to notify the Secretary
U.N. General Ban Ki-moon, so he had time to find a replacement.
What I've said is that it's best to start looking for a successor, Eide said.
When I arrived here, there was a two-month gap between the departure of my predecessor and my arrival.
Ban has begun looking for a replacement, according to U.N. officials.
Officials say they have been considering Staffan di Mistura, a Swedish-Italian citizen who recently led the U.N. mission in Baghdad, and Jean-Marie Guéhenno, a Frenchman who previously led UN peacekeeping missions.
Eide’s position in Afghanistan was put to the test before his former delegate, Peter W. Galbraith, who accused him in September of favoring President Hamid Karzai in the country’s presidential vote and covering evidence of mass electoral fraud.
Eide denied the incriminations, but said Galbraith’s accusations, who were fired, “surely harmed the mission, because there was already much skepticism about international interference” in the election.
Eide said it proposed the appointment of a senior civilian representative to coordinate assistance efforts by U.S.-led forces in Afghanistan.
He also boosted U.N. leadership to allow his successor to hire more U.S. and other Western country personnel, who lend themselves to the Afghan mission, stating that this would increase their confidence that their money is being used correctly.
Eide expressed frustration at limitations to its powers in Afghanistan, arguing that cumbersome U.N. hiring regulations undermine its ability to attract talent.
That’s how the U.N. rules allowed me to recruit only one person since May, he said.
That's catastrophic and can't continue.
Gates: Iran to Face Additional Sanctions
Defense Secretary Robert M. Gates said on Friday that world powers will soon impose "significant additional sanctions" against Iran, for its failure to engage in talks on its nuclear ambitions.
Gates, addressing a group of approximately 300 U.S. troops in northern Iraq, during a week-long trip in war zones in Afghanistan and Iraq, downplayed the prospect of military action against the Islamic Republic.
There are no good options in Iran, he said, in response to a soldier’s question about the likelihood of such an event.
One of the things that weighs on me is that, if we’ve learned anything from Iraq over the last six years, it’s the inherent unpredictability of the war.
The Obama administration is considering a sanctions package targeting Iran’s military and political elite, but Gates noted that some sanctions could also affect the wider Iranian population.
He argued that it would take “a package of incentives and disincentives,” “to persuade the Iranian government that they would be really less safe with nuclear weapons,” because “their people will suffer enormously” sanctions.
On Friday, in a statement by White House spokesman Robert Gibbs, the administration joined European leaders to warn that Iran will face "credible" consequences if its nuclear program does not fully respect the indications of the U.N. Security Council and its nuclear watchdog, the International Atomic Energy Agency.
Iran insists it wants to develop nuclear knowledge only for peaceful purposes.
In talks in Geneva on 1 October, he indicated that he would re-negotiate the restriction of his nuclear programme and would agree to leave a substantial portion of his enriched uranium reserve, in exchange for fuel desperately needed for a medical research reactor.
The administration has lobbied for such an agreement, as a way to build trust between the two sides and gain time for negotiations.
But since then, Iran seems to have moved away from the tentative agreements, in part, experts claim, because the Iranian command is divided in relation to the possibility of engaging with the United States.
Frankly, I believe Iran’s tightening with the international community over some proposals they actually accepted in early October has united the international community, including the Russians and the Chinese, in a way they haven’t been in terms of significant additional sanctions against the Iranians, Gates said.
President Obama has set a deadline on December 31 for Iran to respond to the proposals, before rethinking other options, including pursuing what Foreign Minister Hillary Rodham Clinton once called “devastating sanctions.”
In a statement made in New York, Iran’s mission to the United Nations denounced what it called Thursday “unfounded and baseless accusations,” on behalf of some members of the Security Council on Iran’s nuclear activities, and said it is willing to continue talks with the U.S. and five other world powers, “in order to achieve an appropriate, long-term solution.”
Gates, who was to return to Washington last Friday, met Iraqi Prime Minister Nouri Al-Maliki in the morning, before flying to the oil-rich Kurdish region of Iraq, to meet troops in Kirkuk and Kurdish officials in Irbil.
Tensions remain high between the Kurds and the Arab majority of Iraq, in particular with regard to borders, property rights and co-participation.
Gates urged both sides to diminish the potential for the conflict, to avoid any delay in U.S. projects, to reduce the number of U.S. troops from 115,000 to 50,000 by the end of August.
Gates also sought to relieve Kurdish anxiety about the pending reduction. U.S. officials cited Gates' words to Massoud Barzani, president of the Kurdistan Regional Government: "We will preserve their security, prosperity and autonomy within a unified Iraq.
We won’t abandon them.”
In an aging China, a change of course
Wang Weijia and her husband grew up surrounded by propaganda posters that lectured them about the fact that "Mother Earth is too tired to support more children" and "one more baby means one more grave."
They learned the lesson so well that, when Shanghai government officials, alarmed by their city’s low birth rate and aging population, suddenly changed course this summer and began incentivizing young couples to have more than one child, their reaction was instantaneous and steadfast: By no means.
We've already given all our time and energy just for a child.
There's nothing left for a second, said Wang, 31, a human resources manager with an 8-month-old son.
More than 30 years after China’s one-child policy was introduced, creating two generations of notoriously stubby children, spoiled, affectionately dubbed “little emperors,” a demographic crisis is emerging in the country.
The average birth rate has plummeted 1.8 children per couple, compared to six when the policy became effective, according to the Division
U.N. demographic, while the number of 60-year-old and older residents is expected to explode from 16.7 percent of the population in 2020 to 31.1 percent by 2050. This is well above the global average of about 20 percent.
The imbalance is worse in rich coastal cities with very cultured populations, such as Shanghai.
Last year, people aged 60 and older accounted for nearly 22 percent of Shangai’s registered residents, while the birth rate was less than one child per couple.
Xie Lingli, director of the Shanghai Family Planning and Municipal Population Commission, said fertile couples must have babies to “help reduce the proportion of the aging population and mitigate future labor shortages.”
Shanghai is about to be "as old (not as rich, however) as developed countries like Japan and Sweden," he said.
A gradual relief
Drafted in the country’s constitution in 1978, China’s one-child policy is arguably the most contentious mandate introduced to date by the Communist Party.
Couples who violate the policy face huge fines, up to three times their annual salary in some areas, and employment discrimination.
Chinese officials have backed the policy for their contribution to avoiding critical pressure on their natural resources, while human rights defenders have denounced abuses in policing.
In rural areas, some officials have forced pregnant women with a second child to undergo abortions.
In addition, many couples have done abortions according to the sex of the fetus, leading to an unnatural male-female ratio.
In recent years, demographic officials have gradually softened their stance on one-child policy.
In 2004, they allowed for more exceptions to the rule, including urban residents, members of ethnic minorities, and cases in which both husband and wife are only children, and in 2007 attenuated many of their hard-line slogans.
Qiao Xiaochun, a professor at Beijing University’s Demographic Research Institute, said central government officials have recently been debating even more radical changes, such as allowing couples to have two children if one spouse is an only child.
In July, Shangai became the first Chinese city to launch an aggressive campaign to incentivize more births.
Almost at night in the morning, the posters ordering the families to have a single child, were replaced by copies of regulations that detailed who would be suitable to have a second child and how to apply for a permit.
The city government sent family planning officials and volunteers to visit couples at home and slide leaflets under the doors.
It has also promised to provide emotional and financial guidance to those who have chosen to have more than one child.
Family planning officials say the response has been very unoverwhelming.
Disappointing response
Although officials in a rural city on the outskirts of Shanghai argue that they saw a moderate increase in requests from couples who want a second child following the launch of the campaign, more urban districts do not indicate any changes.
Huinan Municipality, with a population of 115,000, for example, is still receiving only four to five applications per month.
Disappointed Shangai officials say that, despite the campaign, the number of births in the city in 2010 is still expected to only reach around 165,000, slightly higher than in 2009, but lower than in 2008.
Feng Juying, head of Caolu municipality’s family planning committee in Shanghai, said financial considerations are probably the main reason why many people don’t want more children.
They want to do their best to their first child, he said.
Yang Jiawei, 27, and his wife Liu Juanjuan, 26, said they would love to have two sons and are legally empowered to do so.
But like many Chinese, they have only insufficient health and life insurance provided by the government.
Without a network of social protection, they argue, that option would be irresponsible.
People in the West incorrectly see single-child policy as a matter of rights, said Yang, a construction engineer whose wife is seven months pregnant with the couple’s first child.
Yes, they're robbing us the possibility of having more than one child.
But the problem is not just a political issue.
It's the money.
Other couples cite psychological motives for their doubts.
Wang, the human resources manager, said he wants an only child because she was, too: “We were the center of our families and we were used to everyone taking care of us.
We’re not used to taking care of others and we don’t really want to take care of others.”
Chen Zijian, 42, owner of a translation agency, explained it more bluntly.
For parents with two middle-class careers, which reduce the birth rate, he said, it’s about being successful enough to be selfish.
People between the ages of 20 and 30 grew up seeing their parents’ struggle during China’s first days’ experiment with capitalism and they don’t want that kind of life for themselves, he said.
Even a single child takes a lot of time from the parents, he said.
A mother has to give up at least two years of her social life.
Then there are issues of space, "they have to remodel their apartment", and the strategic "has to have a curriculum prepared for the best nurseries when the child is 9 months old."
Most of his friends are willing to face this once, Chen said, but not two.
Ours is the first generation with the highest standard of living, he said.
We don't want to make too many sacrifices.
U.N
The U.N.-sponsored climate conference, characterized so far by defiant stances and mutual recriminations, captured renewed attention on Friday with the release of a document that foresees ambitious reductions in greenhouse gases over the next 40 years, with industrialized nations carrying most of the load on their shoulders in the short term.
The text, which could provide the basis for a final political agreement to regulate greenhouse gases, highlighted the remaining obstacles and highlighted the way forward.
But it was seen as a major breakthrough in a negotiation that is running out of time, with more than 100 world leaders coming to Copenhagen next week.
Developed by an ad hoc UN working group, the text doesn’t talk about how much money the rich countries would give to the poor to deal with global warming in the short and long term.
And this offers a variety of options for key questions, including how developed economies and major emerging economies would cut their coal production, and what would be the limit of the global temperature increase that political strategists would be willing to tolerate.
This gives a lot of flexibility to the process, said John Coequyt, a senior representative in Washington for the Sierra Club.
Michael Zammit Cutajar, who drafted the six-page document, summarized a 180-page negotiating text to focus on what U.N. climate official Yvo de Bóer described as “the big picture.”
It shows the profiles for a possible deal, in which industrialized nations would collectively cut their emissions by 2020 by between 25 and 45 percent, compared to 1990 levels, while major developing countries would reduce theirs during the same period by 15 to 30 percent.
Together, countries would cut emissions by 50 to 95 percent by 2050.
The European Union also spurred the talks on Friday, promising to provide US$3.6 billion annually over the next three years, to help poorer countries adapt to the impact of climate change, from floods and droughts to deforestation.
Anyway, the same kind of verbal fireworks that have mastered the conversations last week was presented on Friday.
U.S. Special Climate Envoy Todd Stern rejected claims calling for mandatory greenhouse gas emissions cuts for industrialized countries, compared with volunteers on behalf of major emerging economies, if they weren’t financed by the developed world.
The manoeuvre indicated that the Obama administration is taking a tougher line with China, with respect to what Bush administration officials did just two years ago.
The U.S. will not make an agreement without major developing countries moving forward and take action, Stern said, who also lamented that the text did not do enough to ensure the cuts could be verified by outside observers.
Stern made his comments an hour after Chinese Deputy Foreign Minister He Yafei claimed that America’s top climate negotiator lacked “common sense,” or was “very irresponsible” for saying earlier in the week that the United States wouldn’t help China economically cope with global warming.
With the future economic trajectory of the world’s major powers at stake, fault lines have erupted both within the developing world and between the industrial world and emerging economies.
The current battle is both to save the individual economies and to save the planet, with China and the U.S. confronting their respective obligations, while the poorest nations insist that the world’s twenty-four most influential countries are ignoring the scientific imperative to take the bravest action.
Ricardo Ulate, a delegate from Costa Rica, said it’s not surprising that major powers are discussing who should pay for spending to limit greenhouse gases, even though vulnerable countries have become more aggressive looking to contain the big emitters responsible for their actions.
This is clearly a game where a new economic hegemony is unfolding, said Ulate, who also works as a regional adviser on climate change from Mexico and Central America, for International Conservation.
Some of the countries most vulnerable to the impact of climate change indicated that they would continue to push for a legally binding treaty in Copenhagen, although most major participants say the talks will produce a political agreement.
The 43-member Alliance of Small Island States drew up a 24-page preliminary treaty proposal early on Friday morning.
Artur Runge-Metzger, who is leading international climate negotiations on behalf of the European Commission, said the push for small island nations has "put political pressure on the entire political process," partly because they are now unified and demanding action from emerging economies such as China and India.
The talks took on a new urgency as delegates focused on the fact that they must resolve most of the exceptional issues before heads of government come to close a deal.
High-level officials, such as Indian Environment Minister Jairam Ramesh and the Chinese deputy minister, descended from the planes and rushed into the corridors of the Bella Center, to closed-door meetings and press conferences, in order to delimite the demands that will be arbitrated over the next week.
The absolute disorder of the meeting (where every day 13,000 people enter and leave the convention center, the activists who play the guitar present nightly shows mocking the countries that according to them are being sold, and preliminary hand-in-hand proposals are passed instead of via mail), poses a challenge.
Intensity only builds: almost all key ministers are here right now, and on Wednesday 60 heads of government will be in Copenhagen.
We’re entering the major leagues, said Carlos Manuel Rodriguez, vice president for the global policy of International Conservation.
The heavyweights are coming.
Britain's first train 225 km/h redefines that sentiment on Monday
At 5.13 on Monday morning, a whistle will pierce the darkness to signal a train exit that marks a revolution for travel in Britain.
The country’s first high-speed interurban train will reach 225 km/h, as it passes like lightning from Ashford, Kent, to St Panrcas station in London, covering 93 kilometers in 38 minutes.
Lord Adonis, the Secretary of Transportation, will then be joined by Lady Kelly Holmes, aboard Javelin’s first intercity service outside London.
He hopes that the new service will finally connect Britain to an international network of fast, reliable trains and connect the major conurbations of the Midlands, North and Scotland to London.
Sloppy secluded areas would become thriving suburbs, business travel times would shorten, and changing planes and cars to the railroad would reduce national coal emissions, as well as ease congestion on roads.
In theory, the three main political parties believe in this view.
But the tens of billions of pounds needed to build a new national rail network, the inevitable battle of planning, and fears about environmental damage, can still curb Britain’s high-speed lines.
High Speed Two (HS2), a company created in January to provide the government with a feasibility study, will deliver its report to Lord Adonis on Dec. 30.
This will provide you with a detailed itinerary of the next stage planned for the high-speed network.
The new line linking London to the Western Midlands will be traced within a radius of five metres in urban areas and areas where the environmental impact may be controversial.
In rural areas, the plan will be drawn within a radius of 25 meters of a definitive route.
HS2 is also expected to establish three options for a wider high-speed network heading north.
Lord Adonis will respond in spring.
The favored option that is being proposed is a Y-shaped configuration, in which a single high-speed line would reach the Western Midlands.
The line would be divided into or near Birmingham, with one branch heading west from the Pennines to Manchester and Scotland, and one northeastward to Sheffield, Leeds and Newcastle upon Tyne.
A single line would enter Scotland.
“It looks like this will be the most beneficial option,” said a railroad member.
Once completed, travel times between London and Edinburgh would be reduced to 2 hours and 40 minutes.
The first leg, which would not open before 2025, would allow passengers to travel between London and Birmingham in 49 minutes, compared to just over a current hour.
The proposals would allow trains to travel at 402 kmh, making the British network the fastest in Europe.
The initial track is being designed to support 400 mt long trains capable of carrying 1,100 people.
At least 18 trains could operate each hour on a London-Birmingham line.
This means you would need a London terminal capable of receiving 20,000 passengers per hour.
Considering the capital’s space constraints, an existing station is expected to be expanded to suit the high-speed network.
Experts from the railway industry argue that only St Pancras International or Euston offer that potential.
Lord Adonis will announce whether he thinks the scheme should proceed, but extensive public consultation and planning procedures mean that no final decision will be made until after a general election.
Even if you launch the work, the network would have to be built into sections.
At first the trains would be forced to leave the fast lines through the existing lanes north of Birmingham, keeping the travel times between London and Scotland above three hours.
This is seen as the crucial point necessary to ensure a change from planes to trains, a reference point for the railway revolution.
Contributors face mortgage for £75,000 of a Conservator
Alan Duncan, the spokesman of the Conservative Party, charged the taxpayer thousands of pounds a year for the cost of an orangal he built in his constituency house.
The party’s prison spokesman, who was demoted by David Cameron for complaining that deputies were “rations” living, increased his mortgage by £75,000 to build an oak-framed extension at his second home in Rutland.
They allowed him to charge the taxpayer the supplementary interest, adding hundreds of pounds a month.
The authorities of the House of Commons approved the applications at the time and have not been consulted by Sir Thomas Legg, who conducts a review of the expenses of the members. Last night Mr. Duncan said that the requests “could not be clearer or simpler.”
Mr. Duncan, one of the wealthiest Members of Parliament, owns a two-story house in a village in his Rutland and Melton constituency.
Nearby homes have been sold for nearly a million pounds.
The ground floor comprises a kitchen, living and dining room, but the fee office agreed that Mr. Duncan needed more space.
Last year he added a greenhouse to the house, described in the plans as an "oranjal".
Neighbors described it as “a greenhouse for entertainment.”
Mr. Duncan was not asked to make any reference on the loan increase, during a recent investigation by the House of Commons Standards and Privileges Committee into his previous mortgage arrangements.
Last month the committee acquitted him of violating the rules, after an investigation of his expenses.
In 2004, Mr. Duncan changed the legal guarantee for a 271,406-pound mortgage of his London property to the house in his constituency, which he had purchased in 1991, the year before to become a deputy.
Documents released this week show that Mr. Duncan was requesting approximately 1,400 pounds per month in mortgage interest until March last year.
In April, their applications increased to more than £1,800 per month.
Mr. Duncan’s Royal Bank of Scotcy mortgage interest rate didn’t change at the time, suggesting that the entire increase was going to finance the 75,000-pound loan.
BRE’s standard variable mortgage rate fell from 7.94% in December 2007, to 4% in March of this year, the rate it is now.
The most recent document issued by the House of Commons authorities shows that Mr. Duncan was still requesting £1,250 per month in May.
Mr. Duncan lost his job as House opposition president in September, after an undercover reporter filmed him that deputies had to survive “rations” after the spending scandal.
“Basically this is nationalized, you have to live of rations and be treated as m**rda.”
He said, “I spend my money in my garden and request a small fraction based on what is appropriate.
And I could request a lot more, but I don’t.”
The deputy, a millionaire for his former profession as an oil merchant, was first questioned about his expenses in May, when it turned out that he had requested thousands of pounds for his garden, before agreeing with the salary office that this “could be considered excessive.”
An activist dug a hole with the shape of the sterling symbol on Mr. Duncan's lawn in protest, after it was revealed that he requested 4,000 pounds for three years.
Last night, when asked about the mortgage increase, Mr. Duncan said, “It was for overriding improvements, approved both by the wage office and later by Legg.”
“It’s not about eluding and circumventing or breaking the rules or whatever.
This is absolutely within the limits, according to the rules, authorized and everything.
It couldn't be in any way clearer or simpler."
Come those who have faith, to see the Advention calendar of Brighton's beach palapa
Brighton’s palapas have found a new boost for the winter months in an interactive Advent calendar.
In addition, an alternative religious group that encourages spiritual exploration through creativity, invited 24 owners to decorate their palapas for each day in December, using carols as their theme.
The event began on December 1, with guests who could see the last palapa from 17.30 to 18.30 every day, with stuffed pastries and ponche served for free.
Carools chosen so far include Vi Tres Barcos, Oh Ven, Oh Ven, Emanuel and La Primera Navidad, where the palapa features the work of Janette Tozer, a local artist.
Martin Poole, 50, a Hove TV marketing assistant, is an unpaid cleric from the Diocese of Chichester and the leader of Beyond.
“We want religion to be more relevant to the people, in a post-Christian society.”
"Why do we wait as a Church for people to come to an old strange building? I think the Church should come to people and celebrate in exciting ways.
“The idea of palapa’s Advention calendar began as a conversation with some friends during a dinner.
Brighton is an incredibly creative and vibrant place and we’re trying to test and represent that through spirituality.”
Massive climate mobilisation in Copenhagen and around the world
Tens of thousands of protesters protested on Saturday in demand for an ambitious and binding agreement to fight global warming, around the world and in Copenhagen, where police detained several hundred people.
About thirty thousand people, according to the police; hundreds of thousands, according to the organizers, paraded through the frosty Danish capital in demand of an agreement that the United Nations Conference on climate change must reach no later on Friday. It would come into force on January 1, 2013.
Connie Hedegaard, Danish president of the Climate Conference, the growing mobilisation for climate around the world, represented by these demonstrations, contributed to “the political price” of a failure in Copenhagen being very high.
Incidents erupted shortly after the start of the demonstration in the Danish capital, when a group of 300 people, all dressed in black, smashed storefronts with cobbbles and hammers, an AFP journalist said.
The riot police intervened quickly and firmly.
The police announced the detention of 600 to 700 people in total, according to her, they are especially members of the “Black Blocks”, ultra-violent groups that had already acted during the NATO summit in Strasbourg, eastern France, last April.
The Climate Justice Action (CJA), one of the organizers of the demonstration, denounced the conditions in which hundreds of militants were detained "without distinction", stressing that a hundred of them "were immobilized in the street on Saturday night handcuffed and seated despite the intense cold."
The demonstration, quite peaceful, came out of Parliament and took to the Bella Center, home of negotiations.
Demonstrators stopped about 500 meters from the building without trying to enter.
A stage was set to welcome the guest speakers. The former South African archbishop of Cape Town, Desmond Tutu, headed a candlelight vigil.
Most of the protesters arrived by coach and train from large European cities, but numerous Asians, including Chinese and Koreans, were also present like some Africans.
About 3,000 people, most with celestial raincoats responded by forming a first morning rally in Copenhagen to calling Friends of the Earth to form “blue tides” for “justice and climate.”
Today we took to the streets to seek compensation for ecological debt in favor of the South, explained Lidy Nacpil, a Filipino militant at the Jubilee South Coalition in Copenhagen.
We can’t keep saying: there’s time, Beninese singer Angélique Kidjo said.
In Africa, there are rivers that are dried up, water courses that you can walk like never before.
For the first time in the history of climate diplomacy, created in 1992 with the adoption of the UN Convention, the anti-globalization movement approached environmental organizations.
The French MEP José Bové, figure of anti-globalization, explained that he came to Copenhagen to "join climate justice and social justice": "today the fight against global warming does not oppose the struggle for another world".
The Asia Pacific region, which gathers numerous islands highly vulnerable to warming, kicked down the demonstrations.
According to organizers, some 50,000 people took to the streets in Australia.
In Manila, more than a hundred people, mostly students, paraded dressed in red and wearing handkerchiefs with slogans in favor of solar energy.
Concentrations of hundreds of demonstrators were formed in Hong Kong or Jakarta, as well as in Canada.
In France, demonstrations organized by the network "350" gathered hundreds of people, especially in Paris, Marseille (South), Lille (North), Bordeaux (Western) and Lyon (East).
In Geneva, dozens of people gathered.
Separatist Abkhazia elected president, although Georgia denounces "fraud"
On Saturday Abkhazia, the pro-Russian separatist region of Georgia recognized by Moscow more than a year ago, elected president; however, Tbilisi denounces "fraud".
Some 131,000 people were summoned to participate in the first election after the Russo-Georgian war in August 2008 for control of South Ossetia, another Georgian separatist region recognized by Russia.
The polling stations closed at 17 h GMT.
According to those responsible for the Abkhaz Electoral Commission, the turnout was 58% a few hours before the closure.
The first provisional results would be announced Saturday night through Sunday.
The five running candidates, including outgoing President Sergueï Bagapch, categorically reject the idea of reunification with Georgia, which disapproved of vote scrutiny.
These elections are a farce.
Georgian President Mikheïl Saakachvili "regrets that Abkhazia has fallen under total Russian occupation," his spokesman, Manana Manjgaladzé, said.
In addition to Russia, only Nicaragua and Venezuela recognized the independence of this territory of 216,000 inhabitants, while the rest of the world considers it part of Georgia illegally occupied by Russian troops.
Following their separation from Georgia during the 1992-1993 conflict that ended with several thousand dead, Abkhazia tries to demonstrate through this election that it is ready to join the international community.
This process is a stage of our new life, our new era as an independent state, Svetlana Kvartchia, a 54-year-old historian, told AFP that she claimed to have voted for Bagapch.
She was among the voters who went to the polling station in a school in the capital, Soukhoumi, a beautiful white building with palm trees at the entrance.
The issue of Russian domination is the central issue of the presidential election in this region with sumptuous landscapes, on the shores of the Black Sea.
Large planks show Bagapch next to Russian President and Prime Minister Dmitri Medvedev and Vladimir Putin, in different parts of this small territory of 216,000 inhabitants.
In Gali, to the west of the country, the head of the district, Beslan Archba, warmly welcomed Russian officials who came to control the development of scrutiny.
In a brief ceremony, he said: We, the people of Abkhazia, appreciate Moscow’s support, and most particularly, the decision to recognize Abkhazia’s independence.
I hope that his country will quickly be recognized by the United Nations, replied Alexeï Ostrovski, a member of the Russian parliament, raising his glass of vodka.
On the other hand, the opposition represented in the election by former vice president Raoul Khadjimba and two businessmen, Zaous Ardzinba and Beslan Boutba, criticizes the agreements signed with Moscow last year, as the one who cedes to Russia the control of the bajan railways for 10 years.
The fifth candidate, Vitali Bganba, maintains that he does not support current power or the opposition.
It takes a majority of 50% plus one vote to win the election.
If no candidate reaches this threshold, a runoff will be organized two weeks later.
Climate: demonstrations around the world, incidents in Copenhagen
On Saturday, tens of thousands of people took to the streets of the world to call for an ambitious and binding deal before the Copenhagen Climate Change Conference, where police, on alert, detained more than 700 people around an imposing demonstration.
About thirty thousand people, according to the police; hundreds of thousands, according to the organizers, paraded in the afternoon in the middle of the cold of the Danish capital, which hosts until December 18 the delegates of 193 countries, in search of an agreement that must enter into force on January 1, 2013.
The demonstration, quite peaceful, came out of Parliament and took to the Bella Center, where negotiations take place.
Demonstrators stopped approximately 500 meters from the center, without trying to enter.
A stage was set to welcome the guest speakers. The former South African archbishop of Cape Town, Desmond Tutu, headed a candlelight vigil.
A few moments after the start of the demonstration, a group of hundreds of people, all dressed in black, smashed shop windows with bricks and hammers, an AFP journalist reported.
Riot police quickly surrounded them and intervened firmly, throwing several of them on the floor.
The police announced the detention of 600 to 700 people specifying that they were mainly members of the "Black Blocks", ultraviolent groups that had already acted during the NATO summit in Strasbourg, in eastern France, last April.
Late in the afternoon, a police officer was injured with a cobblestone, and four cars were set on fire near an occupied home during incidents that led to detaining about twenty people, according to police.
We didn’t take our eyes off the extremist groups, police spokesman Henrik Jakobsen warned as law enforcement helicopters flew over the area.
Most of the protesters, who arrived by coach and train from the big cities of Germany, from London, Amsterdam, even Milan, were of European origin.
But numerous Asians, including Chinese and Koreans, were also present like some Africans.
About 3,000 people, most with celestial raincoats responded by forming a first morning rally in Copenhagen to calling Friends of the Earth to form “blue tides” for “justice and climate.”
Today we took to the streets to seek compensation for ecological debt in favor of the South, explained Lidy Nacpil, a Filipino militant at the Jubilee South Coalition in Copenhagen.
We can’t keep saying: there’s time, Beninese singer Angélique Kidjo said.
In Africa, there are rivers that dry up, courses of water that you can walk like never before.
For the first time in the history of climate diplomacy, created in 1992 with the adoption of the UN Convention, the anti-globalization movement approached environmental organizations.
French MEP José Bové, an anti-globalization figure, told AFP that he came to Copenhagen to "join climate justice and social justice": "today the fight against global warming is not opposed to the struggle for another world."
Oxfam along with former U.N. refugee commissioner Mary Robinson, who was supposed to spearhead the crowd, mobilized several celebrities, including Danish model Helena Christensen.
The Asia Pacific region, which gathers numerous islands highly vulnerable to warming, kicked down the demonstrations.
According to organizers, about 5,000 people took to the streets in Australia.
In Manila, hundreds of people, mostly students, manifested dressed in red and wearing handkerchiefs with slogans in favor of solar energy in front of the City Council of the Philippine capital.
Concentrations of hundreds of demonstrators demanding action against climate change were formed in Hong Kong or Jakarta.
In France, demonstrations organized by the network "350" gathered hundreds of people, especially in Paris, Marseille (South), Lille (North), Bordeaux (Western) and Lyon (East).
In Geneva, dozens of people gathered.
The subject of history and geography is reinforced by the reform of secondary education, according to Chatel
The Minister of National Education, Luc Chatel, stated in an interview that it will be broadcast in Sunday’s Informative (JDD, according to its French acronym) that history and geography would be “strengthened” from the reform of general secondary education, despite being eliminated from compulsory education in the second year of scientific baccalaureate.
From now on, all high school students will have four hours per week and the same program for a shared common culture, says Chatel.
For the Minister, "the hypocrisy is enough" because, according to him, "branch C is no longer a scientific branch", but "in general, it summons the best students".
The General and Literary Teaching branches cannot be subramas of the C, it goes on to emphasize even: "I also heard that historians or intellectuals were outraged to have eliminated history and geography second of high school in technological schools."
An IFOP poll published Saturday in L'Humanité revealed that approximately seven French of ten (69%) disapproved of the governmental project to eliminate compulsory history-geography education in second grade C. Numerous intellectuals and teachers, the left and some right-wing elected representatives also criticized this fact.
However, the Minister added that "there is no talk" of eliminating philosophy from the second baccalaureate program C.
Luc Chatel also defended the reform of teacher training, while 16 organizations (students and unions) called for a day of mobilisation on Tuesday to protest against the project, which, according to them, sacrifices the pedagogical training of future teachers.
In Master 2 (M2), at the beginning of the year they will perform an admission test that will aim at their discipline, and at the end of the year, an admission test on their ability to teach, the Minister explained.
In addition they will practice in real conditions during the year of M2, then they will be a first year in front of the classroom as trainee teachers.
If you do all the practical proposals prior to the contest, you will already have a great experience in front of the classroom, that is, more than 300 hours, he added.
Finally, the Minister points out that he will propose "the individual right to training, which does not exist in national education".
It also confirmed the project to remove the school map "before 2012".
In Higher Normal School, experts will have 15 minutes to convincingly explain the evolution of the climate
Fifteen minutes to talk about the challenges of the Copenhagen summit, a challenge that Jean Jouzel, vice-president of the Intergovernmental Group on Climate Evolution (GIEC) invited by the Ulm Street Higher Normal School, accepted Saturday morning.
Jean Jouzel, also Nobel Peace Prize laureate, inaugurated the "Ernest" conference, a day composed of a series of fifteen-minute expert presentations, filmed and open to the public, an AFP journalist reported.
We wanted to do something that appeals to the people and transmit they want to know more, he explained in the framework of the Edouard Hannezzo conference, 22, one of the organizers of this day called as the students baptized the fish of the pond of the school.
We chose about twenty participants among the experts of their discipline and selected them for their charismatic side, he said.
Regarding global warming, "we can talk hours, but we can also talk about key objectives in a few minutes," Jean Jouzel told AFP, which he exposed without notes, but with the help of graphics in front of about fifty people.
It’s part of our scientific role, he stressed hoping the public understands that “the Copenhagen conference is based on an extremely serious scientific record.”
Videos filmed during those lectures will be online on the school’s website starting December 15.
The next "Ernest" day is scheduled for February, followed by others at a bimonthly pace.
Delay in payments: disgust among prison guards, actions are planned
Prison guard unions on Friday called for a "general mobilization" to demand payment ahead of end-of-year, premium and overtime parties, which was postponed until January for budgetary reasons.
The Prison Workers’ Force (2nd union of guards) asked out-of-duty personnel to join the demonstration Tuesday morning in front of prisons and “block” them to denounce the theft of their assets.
The CGT prisons (3rd union of the profession) calls them to "gather in general assemblies in the different prisons to achieve a massive mobilization", because it calls "shameful and unacceptable" the failure to pay in December of the "extra hours, night premiums, Sundays and public holidays" worked in October.
And the first union of guards, the Federal Autonomous Prison Union (Ufap/Unsa), "summons the general mobilization ... in order to increase discontent and fury."
The Directorate of the Prison Administration and the Ministry of Justice explain that overtimes increased greatly in 2009 and not all were accounted for, but ensure that the balance will be paid in January.
Late the day, the Foreign Ministry reported that Michèle Alliot-Marie had sent a letter to the guards’ unions guaranteeing that the payment would take place next month.
I do not doubt the efforts made by the personnel for the operation and modernization of our prison system, writes the Minister of Justice in this letter.
I requested the Directorate of Prison Administration to immediately implement a set of measures aimed at the fact that this situation generated by a poor evaluation of the number of overtimes does not occur again in the future.
The payment of December will then be scarce, except for the personnel of the management that enjoys the benefit of the famous premiums for functions and objectives, and that "always blames the budgetary demands", deplored the Ufap/Unsa.
It’s a real scandal, Marsella’s FO inter-regional union said indignantly, announcing its decision to boycott “all meetings and convocations.”
The staff will not come to work on Sundays and corresponding public holidays if those for the month of October are not paid in December, the same will happen with the overtime hours, also threat FO Rhône-Alpes-Auvergne.
At a time when our tasks and missions increase without taking more strength, it is more than regrettable to dare to attack the salary of the personnel, estimates the CGT prison in Bordeaux.
According to unions, not in all regions the same thing happens, since some are luckier than others.
But in any case, the result is not as expected, Ufap Secretary-General Jean-François Forget said.
For Christmas, Obama says he makes "better gifts" than he receives
U.S. President Barack Obama, who will celebrate his first family Christmas in the White House, confessed to the famous television host, Oprah Winfrey, who reached a simple conclusion regarding gifts: "I make better gifts than I receive."
During a special interview that ABC will broadcast on Sunday night, the President and his wife Michelle explain how they plan to celebrate the holidays in their new and prestigious residence.
Even Bo, the family’s little four-legged partner, will be the departure.
Santa also loves Bo, Michelle Obama said, clarifying that the dog will receive "of course" a gift, according to excerpts from the show broadcast in advance.
And Michelle Obama disagreed with her husband’s claims about the quality of the gifts she receives: “There’s no way, last year I gave you nice gifts,” she said provoking the president.
It's true, he was forced to admit him before answering humorously pointing at the First Lady's pearl necklace: "Who gave you that?"
During the one-hour interview, in a relaxed atmosphere with the famous cheerleader who defenatively supported Barack Obama’s presidential campaign, the president and his wife also remembered their favorite childhood gifts.
Barack Obama recalled a bicycle with two speeds, as well as a basketball that his Kenyan father, who rarely saw, gave him for Christmas.
I remember that time when my father came to see us at Christmas and gave me a basketball, the President said.
Only many years later, I learned that he gave me that ball.
The First Lady remembered her dollhouse.
I had no idea how to organize a house, so I put all the furniture online against the wall, instead of settling them around the home, yet I loved that dollhouse, Michelle Obama said.
Johnny Hallyday again operated, RTL claims he's in an induced coma
Johnny Hallyday was intervened again in Los Angeles due to significant "injuries" stemming from an operation in France, but the situation is "under control" according to his relatives, while RTL claims that the singer was subjected to an induced coma to avoid pain and complications.
The 66-year-old rocker was intervened again Wednesday night on Thursday at Cedars-Sinaï Hospital in Los Angeles where some doctors tested "injuries stemming from the herniated disc operation" held in Paris on Nov. 26, his news service said in the same city, indicating that such injuries "needed a repair operation."
However, the singer’s press service tried to convey peace of mind stating that the situation was “under control”, and reported that a new medical part would communicate within the next 48 hours.
According to RTL, sponsor of the singer's last tour, Johnny Hallyday agreed, however, to be subjected to an induced coma to avoid "pains and complications."
In less than 48 hours when the patient wakes up, we will have more certainty about their status, RTL adds.
The press service, questioned by AFP, did not confirm or comment on the information.
RTL also claims that "according to some sources" no cannula would have been introduced after the first operation in Paris.
The rocker had been operated at Monceau Park’s international clinic due to a herniated disc.
The intervention, scheduled for a few days, was practiced by neurosurgeon Stéphane Delajoux, a well-known physician in the artistic environment.
On Thursday night, Delajoux declined to comment on the information conveyed by RTL.
Singer Johnny Hollyday was admitted Monday to Cedars-Sinaï Hospital in Los Angeles for an infection stemming from Delajoux's intervention.
Singer Eddy Mitchell, close friends with Johnny Hallyday, explained Thursday in RTL that his friend was going through "a bad time", but assured that there was "no problem" because the rocker is "a fighter".
He called me just before traveling to Los Angeles, and I told him it was silly to take twelve hours of a plane after such an operation, Eddy Mitchell said.
On Wednesday night, the musician's producer, Jean-Claude Camus, pretended to reassure that Johnny Hallyday "reacted satisfactorily to antibiotics" and that the infection was "controlled."
Camus claimed to have spoken with Laeticia, the musician’s wife, that he had not noticed any particular concerns.
But the singer’s health problems multiplied since July when he was interned for nine days following a fall in his yacht.
Two months later, to the general surprise, Johnny Hallyday revealed to have been operated on for a "colón cancer", after a mild infection.
In late September, the singer resumed, as planned, his long "Gira 66", presented as his farewell tour.
But since then, every health problem is overly discussed. Fans and journalists noticed the musician’s difficulties in moving on the scene.
In mid-November, his relatives even had to deny a new hospitalization.
Euro-2009/short swimming pool: first Bousquet title in your favourite distance
Frédérick Bousquet, triple medalist in the World Cup-2009 long pool, won on Thursday an international title in a distance for which he feels particular affection, 50 m free, during Euro-2009 in a short swimming pool in Istanbul.
In the first day of this competition in which polyurethane swimsuits were last allowed, only three world records were exceeded when in reality many more were expected.
One was improved by the Hungarian Evelyn Verraszto in 200 m 4 styles; and the other two, by the Russian in relay 4 x 50 m 4 styles (masculine).
A shame for Bousquet, who coveted the world record in 50 m free, a mark he owns in a long pool (20.94).
In one of the two queen distances of swimming, in 50 m free, the 28-year-old Frenchman prevailed with 20''53 during a final where ten swimmers competed instead of eight as usual, a scoop internationally.
Bousquet overtook Croatia’s Duje Draganja (20.70) and Russia’s Sergey Fesikov (20.84), but failed to lower the world mark (20.30), as planned.
We can always aspire to something better and, in that case, I already said that if the world record was within my reach, I was going to try to reach it.
Like Bousquet, almost all swimmers wore polyurethane swimsuits that from January 1st will be banned.
These swimsuits, the origin of 238 world records since February 2008, finish their epic in Istanbul, where they competed for the first time in 1999 for the same test.
This time the use of swimsuits didn’t help the German Paul Biedermann, double long-pool world champion this summer in Rome, consecutive world records.
The young swimmer failed to lower his mark by 400 μss (3:32.77), however, he prevailed with the second-bestest time of his career (3:34.55), against the Russian Nikita Lobintsev (3:35.75) and the Danish Mads Glaesner (3:36.82).
I didn’t give myself thoroughly in this race.
I knew that this time I was not going to be able to break a world record, but I managed to defend my title and fulfilled my mission.
Lobintsev will be a tough rival in the future, Biedermann explained.
U.S.: A sarcophagus panel that belonged to Zola sold at USD 1.5 million
A Roman sarcophagus panel that belonged to French writer Emile Zola was sold on Thursday at 1.5 million dollars, well above the estimated price, Sotheby's art dealer announced.
This panel 2 meters long by 63 cm high, dates from the third century after Christ, and its price had been estimated between 150,000 and 250,000 dollars.
Marble "representing four Dionysian scenes, in a sophisticated architectural framework, surrounded by satyrs and drunken women, is an uncommon piece, since in the world there are only four or five," Florent Heintz, vice president of art auctions of ancient Rome and Egypt at Sotheby's, told AFP on Tuesday.
It sold out among a hundred lots.
The sale continues, but until now, this lot was the most pushed by the buyers, without a doubt, a Sotheby's spokesman, Dan Abernethy, told AFP.
Only a few days ago it was discovered that marble was an acquisition of Emile Zola.
For almost 300 years, the panel was part of the famous collection of the Borges family in Rome, then appeared successively in the house of French actress Cécile Sorel, as an element of his bathtub in his particular Parisian mansion, and in the house of Paul Raynaud, minister of the Third French Republic and president of the Council for a short time, before Marshal Pétain took power.
For about fifty years, his whereabouts were uncertain.
Reviewing the database of the Louvre Museum, Florent Heintz discovered that this panel was part of an "Emile Zola succession" opened in 1903, a year after the death of "Thérèse Raquin" and the fresco of "Rougon-Macquart".
Honduras aborted Zelaya’s departure to Mexico
On Thursday Mexico estimated that the conditions were not given to transfer to its territory the ousted president of Honduras, Manuel Zelaya, to whom the coup government denied him the safe conduct.
At the moment, after a telephone conversation in the evening with President Zelaya, everything seems to indicate that there is no possibility of making the transfer, said Mexican Foreign Minister Patricia Espinosa.
The plane of the Mexican government that arrived in El Salvador on Wednesday to take Zelaya, returned to its place of origin, according to a source from the General Directorate of Migration.
To grant him a saffortune, the coup government wanted to force Zelaya to "renounce" and seek political asylum abroad.
But the ousted president refused because he wanted to arrive in Mexico as guest acting Head of State.
The coup government "suffered a new defeat by asking me to resign my position (...), they wanted me to present my resignation," Zelaya told Globo radio on Thursday, in which he usually makes statements since the beginning of the five-month political crisis in Central America.
Here I can stay ten years, I have my guitar, the deposed president, a refugee in the Brazilian embassy of Tegucigalpa for two and a half months.
The Mexican embassy gave us an application for safe-conduct, but unfortunately we could not accept it because it did not contain request for asylum, the government’s Interior Minister, Oscar Raul Matute, told Honduran radio HRN on Wednesday.
I will not seek asylum from any country, Manuel Zelaya replied in an interview on the Telesur network, whose headquarters are in Caracas.
If I leave Honduras, I'll do it as president of the Honduran people, he said.
Rumors about Zelaya’s departure on Wednesday mobilized supporters of the deposed president, who gathered around Brazil’s embassy, besieged heavily by the military.
Brazilian Foreign Minister Celso Amorim criticised the position of the coup government.
That reflects the marginal character of this government in the face of international norms, he said.
Zelaya, who had approached Venezuela’s socialist president, Hugo Chavez, was overthrown on June 28, the day he had organized a referendum to prepare for his re-election, rejected by the Supreme Court, the military and Congress.
Since his exile in Costa Rica, he returned surprisingly on September 21 and took refuge in Brazil’s embassy.
If he leaves, he will be arrested and will have to answer to Justice for the crime of "high treason", according to the coup government.
The de facto president, Roberto Micheletti, managed to organize a presidential election on November 29 to replace Zelaya.
In the controversial scrutiny, Porfirio Lobo was elected. This election was recognized by the United States, but questioned by countries in Latin America, such as Venezuela, Argentina and Brazil.
Three days later, Congress voted massively against Zelaya’s return to power until he ended his term on Jan. 27.
Wall Street Rises, U.S. Trade Balance Fuels Optimism
The New York Stock Exchange closed higher on Thursday, U.S. trade balance figures witness the economic revival of both the United States and its partners: the Dow Jones won 0.67%; and the Nasdaq, 0.33%.
According to the definitive figures at closing, the Dow Jones Industrial Average advanced 68, 78 points reaching 10,405.83 points, and the Nasdaq, of technological component, 7.13 points reaching 2,190.06 points.
The Expanded Standard & Poor's 500 index rose 0.58% (6.40 points) reaching 1,102.35 points.
We received good news from different sectors of the economy, said Lindsey Piegza of FTN Financial.
But this is anecdotal, he clarified, considering that the market was concentrated "in the glass half full".
According to Avalon Partner’s Peter Cardillo, investors focused mainly on U.S. trade balance figures, “which reflect an improvement in activity in the fourth quarter.”
The U.S. trade deficit fell 7.6 percent in October from September to US$32.9 billion.
Analysts expected, on the contrary, a deepening deficit.
Both imports and exports increased.
The great novelty is the growth of trade volumes, in particular exports of goods, which reflect the growth of the main economies they trade with the United States, said Christopher Cornell of the research company Economy.com.
On the other hand, last week, unemployment enrollments in the United States unexpectedly increased, 474,000 applications were submitted.
But in the course of the last four weeks, the average number of new job seekers “was the lowest in a year,” said Scott Marcouiller of Wells Fargo Advisors.
Obligations market fell.
10-year-old Treasury yield rose to 3.482% against 3.423% on Tuesday night, and 30-year bond yield at 4.492% against 4.408 percent the eave.
Cuba: more than a hundred supporters of power boo the "Ladies in White"
On Thursday, hundreds of supporters of Cuban power booed the wives of political prisoners gathered in Havana for World Human Rights Day and prevented another demonstration to which a dozen dissidents were summoned.
Some forty "Ladies in White", wives or relatives of political prisoners, were assaulted in the street by supporters of Raul Castro's government as they walked towards the center of the capital demanding the release of such detainees, an AFP journalist said.
The Yankees die!, "Mercenaries!", "The street belongs to the revolutionaries!", "Viva Fidel and Raul!" chanted the militants of power with their fist high to the dissidents.
The latter were escorted by plainclothes officers who came to "protect" them.
The day before, a similar demonstration broke out against the "Ladies of White" in Havana, without injuries or arrests.
But in Vedado, a dozen dissidents who were also preparing to demonstrate on Thursday on International Human Rights Day were detained by plainclothes police officers, an AFP reporter said.
Officers forcibly boarded the vehicles to dissidenters who were booed by government supporters, according to that journalist.
In general, the arrest of dissidents in similar circumstances only lasts for a few hours.
The "Ladies in White", awarded the 2005 Sakharov Prize awarded by the European Parliament, had organized an unauthorized march to "request the release of all political prisoners", about 200 according to dissent, declared one of its leaders, Laura Pollan, whose husband, Héctor Maceda, purges a sentence of 20 years in prison.
Maceda is part of the group of 75 Cuban dissidents imprisoned in March 2003, of which 53 remain behind bars.
Cuban authorities call opponents "mercenaries" or "agents" paid by their declared enemy, the United States.
Rare done in the media, Cuban television reported on Thursday’s demonstration by government militants against “counter-revolutionaries.”
Cuba, governed by a single party, the Communist Party, is constantly accused by European or American NGOs of trampling on rights and freedoms, especially those of expression.
An alienation kills an octogenarian with a knife and leaves 5 injured in Clichy-la-Garenne
An unbalanced man with two knives killed an 80-year-old man and injured five other people in Clichy-la-Garenne (Seine Heights) city center on Thursday at the height of the afternoon
According to the first elements of the investigation, the 28-year-old assailant, would have hit with two knives and for reasons not yet determined, to an older, in the pharmacy or in front of it in No. 85 of Jean-Jaurès boulevard, police sources and witnesses reported.
As a result of serious injuries, the 1992-born man died in the hospital, according to an Alliance police union official.
Then in the pharmacy or in front of it, he attacked a pregnant woman who was with her partner.
The husband, about thirty years old, stood in the way and was wounded.
The first two victims, seriously injured, were taken to the hospital where the elder died.
The life of the wounded one, with the vital organs apparently affected, is in danger.
Then the pregnant woman fainted and was interned "in a state of shock," a police source said.
The gunman, who appeared to be under the effects of alcohol or drugs, according to a police source, would have escaped Jean Jaurès Boulevard.
Seemingly at random, he would have wounded two more pedestrians: a thirty-year-old man, and another forties seriously.
At night, the crime scene was still unclear.
He was armed with two butcher knives that he had crossed on his back, an Alliance union official in the Seine, Arnaud Pressé.
Several passers-byers, including young people, tried to control the alienate, according to testimonies obtained by AFP.
I saw a guy of about thirty with a green jacket.
Three boys tried to intercept him.
They didn’t make it because the guy was strong, explained Alberto, a waiter at a nearby bar.
A crowd threw themselves on him, including a police officer who broke his wrist, he added.
Jean-Jaurès, at the height of No. 61, two municipal agents, including the chief, tried to arrest the gunman and were punctured, according to police testimonies.
The events occurred in one of the main arteries of Clichy-la-Garenne, where there are numerous shops.
Some people came to tell me there was an injured man on the street.
I went out and saw a 70-year-old man on the floor, putting him in a safe position, I felt a very important wound in his abdomen, Frédéric Allione, a 35-year-old pharmacist, told AFP.
Five minutes later, there came a second person of about 35 years, with knife wounds.
National police officers arrested the man without identity documents.
The Nanterre Public Prosecutor's Office submitted the investigation to the jurisdiction of the Departmental Service of the Judicial Police of Altos del Sena (SDPJ 92).
The madman was hospitalized Thursday night.
During his pretrial detention, he said no word.
A police source does not exclude the possibility of hospitalizing him in a psychiatric hospital.
According to another police source, it took six policers to dominate him at Clichy police station where he was transferred.
It was "in a state of overexcitement" as "every derangement of this type whose forces increase considerably under the effect of action."
U.S: Polanski's lawyer again asks that the case be dismissed
Franco-Polish filmmaker Roman Polanski’s lawyer claimed again Thursday in Los Angeles (California, West of the United States), that he dismiss the case against his client, who is under house arrest in Switzerland, for a case of sexual abuse that occurred more than 30 years ago.
Chad Hummel, an attorney for “El Pianista” director, denounced serious procedural procedural procedural errors to three judges of the Second District House of Appeal in 1977.
Hummel stated: I claim that at this very moment the charges are dismissed, since the conduct of the judge in charge of the case at the time, Laurence Rittenband, "turned chilling."
According to him, Judge Rittenband (deceased ever since) would have spoken with a prosecutor who would have told him that Roman Polanski deserved to go to prison, before passing sentence, which constitutes a serious lack of procedure.
This conversation was made public in the documentary "Roman Polanski: wanted and wanted" (Roman Polanski, wanted and desired), from the mouth of the prosecutor in question, David Wells.
But finally Wells declared at the end of September "to have lied" to the author of the documentary.
Hummel called for a trial that would allow him to present evidence of his argument in the event that the court rejects the dismissal of the case.
The indictment, for its part, reiterated the argument stated in the first instance, i.e. that no dismissal of the charges could be seriously evaluated as long as Roman Polanski did not appear in court.
The 76-year-old filmmaker fled the U.S
Since then, the United States has never set foot.
Los Angeles Deputy District Attorney Phyllis Asayama said Roman Polanski “has to be here to show up at the hearing.”
With this cause, we want to convey the message, not only to (Roman Polanski) but also to other defendants, that there is a possibility of fleeing?” asked Asayama.
For her, the filmmaker "asks the court for a favor, although he despises his authority."
One of the three judges, who now have 90 days to pronounce his sentence, also stressed "that there were other alternatives to the decision to flee."
Hummel wasn't the only one asking to dismiss the case against Roman Polanski.
Samantha Geimer’s lawyer, the victim, also once again claimed to close the case.
She maintains that her client can no longer bear to harass her whenever the Polanski case resurfaces, adding: "No one in this room can say the procedure was fair.
Thirty-two years, that's enough."
Roman Polanski was arrested Sept. 26 in Switzerland on U.S. arrest warrants.
After more than two months behind bars, he was sentenced to home prison in his Gstaad hut, pending eventual extradition.
Wall Street loses its way and is slowed by the rise of the dollar
The New York Stock Exchange closed this Friday aimlessly, in a market that is debated between indicators better than expected in the United States and the rise of the dollar: the Dow Jones gained 0.63%, but the Nasdaq lost 0.03%.
According to the definitive figures at closing, the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 65.67 points reaching 10,471.50 points, while the Nasdaq, of technological component, lost 0.55 points to stand at 2,190.31 points.
The Extended Standard & Poor's 500 Index rose from 0.37% (4.06 points), reaching 1,106.41 points.
Honestly, the market is very quiet, said Mace Blicksilver of Marblehead Asset Management.
Remains with narrow margins, the volume of trades is decreasing and I think it’s going to stay that way until the end of the year.
The trend continued thanks to the publication of better-than-expected economic indicators, which "helped to improve market opinion on the robustness of the economic revival," Charles Schwab analysts summarized.
In the U.S., retail sales rose 1.3 percent in November for the second month in a row than expected.
In addition, U.S. consumer confidence visibly regained in December, 73.4 against 67.4 the previous month, according to the University of Michigan’s preliminary index estimate.
However, the indices were slowed by a new advance of the dollar that reached the highest level in the last two months against the euro, which "leads the sectors sensitive to the economic situation low," explained Owen Fitzpatrick of Deutsche Bank.
In this way, technological values lost ground.
Obligations market fell.
10-year Treasury’s yield amounted to 3.540% to 3.482% on Wednesday night, and the 30-year bond yield to 4.497% versus 4.492 percent the eve.
The choice of Miss World again in South Africa
After a month tour of South Africa, the 112 Miss Mundo title candidates find themselves in the final stretch to fulfill their dream: to be elected the most beautiful woman in the world in front of a billion viewers.
During this great celebration of beauty organized for the second consecutive year in the surroundings of Johannesburg, five of them already achieved their participation in the semi-final.
Miss Japan Eruza Sasaki, Perla Beltran of Mexico, Mariatu Kargbo of Sierra Leone, Kaiane Aldorino of Gibraltar and Yanbing Ma of Canada were chosen Miss Athlete or even Miss Top Model during their stay in Africa.
It was a real stir.
It was extraordinary to visit beautiful cities such as Port Elizabeth, Durban and Cape Town.
I saw so many things and lived countless experiences, Miss Sierra Leone said astonished.
This contest that for the ninth time takes place in South Africa, will air on Saturday live from Midrand, north of Johannesburg, from 17 h (15 h GMT).
Impossible to envision a better country to deliver my crown.
This experience was fascinating.
I am very happy to have lived twice, says Miss Mundo Ksenia Sukhinova in the southern summer heat.
For the young Russian woman, her second stay in South Africa was marked by numerous exchanges with the candidates and by the possibility of rediscovering "african landscapes that take their breath away".
After a month of galas, rehearsals, press conferences and safaris, the candidates for Miss Mundo concentrate on the contest.
Pooja Chopra hopes to follow in the footsteps of Priyanka Chopra, winner in 2000 and present in this election, as well as those of Aishwarya Rai in 1994, both become Bollywood stars.
Every woman’s dream is to be crowned as the most beautiful in the world, but it’s not just about appearance.
We are the ambassadors of our countries, and I want to return to my country with the crown, says Miss India with a big smile.
According to the organizers, music and dance will mark this 59th edition.
The candidates will parade with traditional dresses and, of course, with a swimsuit.
Chinese host Angela Chow, former South African beauty queen Michelle McLean and Miss World committee manager Steve Douglas will host the event.
South Africa proved capable of welcoming the world with warmth and pride difficult to match, says Julia Morley, president of the Miss World competition.
Just like last year, the candidates made a safari, visited Soweto’s famous township, but this time by bike, and tasted local cuisine.
As an exceptional fact, they met South African President Jacob Zuma, who shook their hands.
Something uncommon for a head of state, Miss Sierra Leone observes.
His best memory will undoubtedly be the draw in Cape Town (Southeast) for the June 2010 World Cup, a way for the country to further promote the event.
I dealt with people I never thought I'd get to know.
It will probably come back for the 2010 World Cup, says Miss Italy, Alice Taticchi.
Johnny Hallyday news "is very positive," according to his son David
News of Johnny Hallyday "is very positive," his son David said Friday when he arrived at the Los Angeles airport to accompany his hospitalized father for several days.
The news is very positive.
Dad is strong and everything is going very well, David Hallyday told the press by getting off the plane that brought him from Paris shortly after 13 a.m
The singer's son, distended, assured that he would move "as soon as possible" to Cedars-Sinaï Hospital in Los Angeles, where Johnny Hallyday was admitted on Monday for an infection stemming from the herniated disc operation on November 26 in Paris.
The musician has "been in an induced coma since that night in Los Angeles for reasons of comfort, to prevent him from suffering and thus be able to administer his treatment," the news service said.
Johnny Hallyday had already undergone an induced coma by doctors at Cedars-Sinaï Hospital in Los Angeles, where he was admitted Monday for an infection, the sequel to a herniated disc operation on November 26 in Paris.
The singer underwent surgery once again the night of Wednesday to Thursday due to serious injuries caused by the previous intervention.
His wife Laeticia went Friday morning to the French lyceum of Los Angeles, attended by his daughter Jade, to witness a school event, an AFP photographer confirmed.
The Head of Orthopaedic Surgery and Traumatology at Pitié-Salpêtrière Hospital, Yves Catoné, announced that he would travel to Los Angeles on Monday to be with the singer.
The producer violently accused Dr. Stéphane Delajoux, the neurosurgeon who operated on the singer on November 26 at Monceau Park’s international private clinic in Paris.
They told us it was a massacre.
Surgeons in Los Angeles said they were outraged, Camus said.
There's talk of a possible lawsuit, he said.
Jean-Claude Camus said Johnny Hallyday had "travelled too quickly to Los Angeles with the authorization of the Parisian surgeon."
I spoke directly to the doctor and I was surprised when he assured me that there was no problem in making the trip, because I was operated on twice as a herniated disc, he added.
Shortly before on RTL, he indicated that apparently the singer had "left without a cannula, without anything", after his Parisian operation.
Dr. Stéphane Delajoux is well-known in the artistic environment.
He operated on actress Marie Trintignant in Vilnius, a few days before his death, at a Neuilly-sur-Seine clinic where Delajoux worked at the time.
In 2007, actress Charlotte Gainsbourg also intervened to reabsorb a hematoma formed from a cerebral hemorrhage.
It is a doctor with a bad reputation, convicted by justice on several occasions by malapraxis, as well as tax fraud and fraud.
Olivier Metzner, an attorney for the Order of Doctors, accused him of being "an unscrupulous man" sued "regularly" before the Council of the Order, and is "far from being recommended."
After several days of silence, Dr. Stéphane Delajoux spoke Friday through his lawyer, David Koubbi.
He indicated that the intervention "was carried out without any inconvenience" and that the results of the postoperative studies were "normal".
The operation "could not cause bleeding and therefore did not need drainage," he added.
However, the singer’s tour, whose health problems have multiplied since the summer, entered a parenthesis.
Undoubtedly, retaking the tour on January 8 (in Amiens, NDLR) as planned, I find it very risky, Camus said.
The UDF association raises the tone and warns the New Center about the use of its acronym
François Bayrou’s UDF (Union for French Democracy), controlled by François Bayrou’s MoDem (Democratic Movement), reacted on Friday tover the attempt by New Center President Hervé Morin to launch an OPA (Public Procurement Offer) about the acronym of the former giscardist party, intimating him to withdraw it from all support, or penalty of being sued.
Hervé Morin publicly announced his intention to recover the acronym UDF, since he considers that François Bayrou was not in a position to defend his political heritage after having extended his hand to the left.
On the other hand, the Minister of Defense, whose party is associated with the UMP, had planned to confirm the strategy on Saturday before the National Council of the NC.
In support of his management, he received the help of Deputy Hervé de Charette (formerly UMP, current NC) who claims ownership of the UDF brand, as he was the first to register it at the National Institute of Industrial Property in 2004.
But on Friday night, the office of the UDF association, controlled by François Bayrou’s friends who do not want to be deprived of it, broke the silence to launch a request to the Minister and the deputy by encouraging them to give up the acronym and to transfer their ownership rights of the brand, under penalty of lawsuit.
In recent days, you have made many statements suggesting that you were going to propose to your political movement to recover the acronym UDF, the association writes in a letter signed by 19 of the 24 members of the office, including François Bayrou and Marielle de Sarnez del MoDem, as well as Senator Jean Athuis of the Centrist Alliance.
They also think it right to continue with the use of the name of the movement, the +Nouveau centre+, and the slogan +l'UDF d'aujourd'hui+ in its militant material and on the presentation page of its site, the signatories of the letter argue.
As you already know, they have no right to use the acronym, explains the UDF office that requires the NC “immediately to recall the UDF’s name of all support, as well as (its) website.”
It also asks Hervé de Charette "to transfer ownership of (the) trademark to the rightful rightholders."
Failure to do so will appeal to justice to enforce the law and the legitimate rights of UDF members, signatories warn.
In the letter, they specify that the UDF "is a true political movement since 1978" and recall that Morin and his friends "chose" to leave in 2007 "to create a new party, as others had already done in 2002 to create the UMP".
Therefore, the New Center has, according to them, no right to use the UDF acronym.
They also recall that the last UDF congress voted in 2007 its accession to the MoDem for three years and the creation of an office in charge of defending "the material and moral interests" of the movement founded by Valéry Giscard d'Estaing.
As for Hervé de Charette, the office continues, "when he registered the brand", he was no longer "a member of the UDF for three years", but of the UMP.
Take advantage in a scandalous and fraudulent way the absence of registration of the acronym UDF to carry it out on your own.
This indiscretion is becoming a crime, they denounce, before mocking the NC's strategy: "We believe that in politics, as in life, it is more worthy to make their own name than to try to leave anonymity by fraudulently taking a name that is not entitled."
Liliane Bettencourt: François-Marie Banier will stand trial for abuse of weakness
On Friday the Court of Nanterre decided to prosecute artist François-Marie Banier to determine if, as affirmed by the daughter of the billionaire Liliane Bettencourt, she took advantage of the state of weakness of the octogenarian to plunder her from a part of her fortune.
The 62-year-old photographer, known for his photos of stars of the show, will be tried on April 15 and 16, 2010 for abuse of weakness, a crime whose sentence consists of a maximum sentence of three years in prison and 375,000 euros of fine.
This means that in this case there will be a process worthy of a saga about one of the richest families in France, in which mother and daughter the reins of the L'Oréal cosmetics empire are disputed.
But Correctional Court No. 15 also ordered 87-year-old Liliane Bettencourt to undergo a medical examination by three medical professionals.
The heiress and main shareholder of L'Oréal always refused to undergo such examinations, even more under the conditions proposed by the Nanterre Prosecutor's Office during the investigation.
Physicians appointed this Friday must submit a report before March 10, 2010 in which they must record if Liliane Bettencourt suffered states of weakness and during what periods.
I think he'll accept.
I'm going to talk to her, Ms. Bettencourt's lawyer, Georges Kiejman, said as she left the hearing.
Françoise Bettencourt-Meyers lawyer Olivier Metzner was pleased by the court’s decision.
It’s excellent news (...) The expertise will show that he (Banier) manipulated this eldest lady, whom we must protect, he said.
For two years, Liliane Bettencourt’s only daughter initiated criminal action against François-Marie Banier, a multifaceted photographer and artist, accusing him of taking advantage for years of his mother’s weakness to obtain one billion euros in donations, through checks, life insurance contracts or works of art.
Liliane Bettencourt always denied being manipulated by the photographer, whom she considers a long-standing friend.
On Friday, while the court was examining the provenance of Mrs. Bettencourt-Meyers’ action, the lawyers’ allegations sometimes resulted in a large family confession.
In this way, Liliane Bettencourt’s lawyer took into account three letters that his client sent (in 1999, 2003 and 2005) to his notary in which he said he was suspicious of Françoise Bettencourt-Meyers’ husband.
In the 2003 letter Liliane Bettencourt added, according to the lawyer: "I could explain to my daughter that I wrote to Banier between once and twice a day for fifteen years.
Since her wedding, I could never talk to Françoise again."
For its part, the Prosecutor's Office of Nanterre considered, once again, that the applicant was not in a position to appear, all the more since a guardianship judge refused, in recent days, to give rise to her request to open a judicial guardianship procedure for her mother.
Liliane Bettencourt "has full capacity to appear in court, is not under guardianship and has no guardian.
No other person will be able to appear in their place," said Marie-Chrsitine Daubigney.
Finally, the court decided that the trial would take place on the admissibility of Mrs. Bettencourt-Meyers.
Managerational revolution or end of business intelligence?
If nothing changes in the management of large French companies, our struggle for the development of business intelligence will have been in vain...
More than ten years ago, the attitude of the management of large companies with respect to their employees recalls the position of companies with respect to consumers before the marketing revolution: it is that collaborators adhere to "values" determined from above as before it was to impose on consumers products designed by factory technicians.
By putting the customer at the center of the company, the marketing revolution gave its place to market conquest strategies ... although the authoritarian temptation to impose products that are unrelated to customer preference factors is regularly repeated.
Demobilization of employees may end in deadly demoralization.
Suicides at France Telecom, PSA or Renault are just the tip of the iceberg of a much more general phenomenon that particularly affects executives.
This demobilization seriously compromises the development of all the intellectual functions of the company, especially R&D and, even more, business intelligence.
Indeed, BI (Business Intelligence) "demands that all parties involved ... have proactive conduct and a forward-looking vision"; it needs from employees a commitment that is entirely opposed to the recommendations of an authoritative leadership.
For this reason, we call for a management revolution, which would consist of putting collaborators at the center of the concerns of the company as well as the client.
Internal opinion polls have a relative utility
Will internal opinion polls, such as Orange’s questionnaire among its employees, incite the management revolution in practice?
Unfortunately, I'm afraid not.
-In principle, because I was able to verify that, in the face of a new situation, and after many laps, all the companies end up doing essentially... what they always did! B989
Secondly, because authoritarianism, one of the pathologies of power, grows proportionally to the importance of its own failures.
Lastly, because most managers have a simplistic view of human behavior.
As they come from the great schools of engineers, they interpret psychology mechanistically.
They adopt, out of intellectual comfort, the ancient behavioral theory that U.S. “neurosciences” recently reactivated, and that the Institutes of Studies and media spread widely behind their backs.
In this way, by suggesting that it is possible to handle the "commands" (sic) of executive engagement, a recent Ipsos investigation published in Le Figaro.fr reaffirms managers in a mechanicist management position, misadapted to the world today.
Identify and Measure Mobilisation Factors
On the contrary, the management revolution necessary for the proper functioning of modern companies, involves adopting a finalist vision of human behavior.
It consists of integrating into every reasoning or business management practice, a fundamental fact: for employees, commitment to the company is only possible if it clearly appears as a means to make part of their dreams, ambitions and projects a reality.
Today, only studies from the Motifunctional Analysis adopt this conception of employee motivations.
The studies showed that they judge their company based on a predetermined doxa that allows them, through the image they have of the company, to evaluate what it offers them to achieve their own life projects.
Thanks to the identification and measurement of mobilizing factors, managers of companies can control, foresee and anticipate their "social performance" and relocate human resources management "in place".
In this way, they create a favorable terrain for the organized development of a strong and effective business intelligence.
Oslo Address: "Fair War" Obama Receives Right Praises
On Friday, the U.S. right, pleased that the 2009 Nobel Peace Prize has defended the concept of "just war" against the enemies of the United States, acclaimed Obama's speech on Thursday.
The conservatives, who had discredited Obama’s Oct. 9 award, this time applauded the presidential speech, as did Sarah Palin, a former vice presidential candidate during the 2008 election.
I loved what she said, Ms. Palin, the main figure on the right wing of the Republican party to the USA Today newspaper, adding that she herself had addressed the issue of "sometimes necessary" war in her recently published memoirs.
In my opinion, war is, of course, the last thing Americans want to be forced to do, but it is necessary.
The terrorists must be stopped, he observed.
Obama, who inherited the two wars of his predecessor George W. Bush in Iraq and Afghanistan, was dedicated, on Thursday in Oslo, to justify the use of force, a way to explain his decision to send 30,000 more troops to Afghanistan nine days earlier.
Another Obama’s adversary, Newt Gingrich, considered that the president had done “a very good job” in Oslo.
I think that the speech was really very good, the former president of the House of Representatives, who appreciated in particular the mention of "the existence of evil in the world" in presidential words.
At times, I found the speech very historic, Gingrich declared, during an interview on the radio program "The Takeaway".
The former elected representative, whom some see as a potential candidate for the Republican inauguration for the 2012 presidential election, was pleased to hear “a left-wing president” when he reminded members of the Nobel Committee “that they would not be free and could not give up a peace prize if recourse to force didn’t exist.”
According to Michael Gerson, author of George W. Bush’s speeches and current chronicler in the Washington Post, Obama delivered “a very American speech.”
In addition to presenting himself as a "citizen of the world", as he "always does," he "spoke as an American president" by remembering Europeans "who live in a security bubble that they didn't create and don't do much to preserve it," Gerson said.
Among the unconvinced the few right-wing, a Bush-era falcon, former U.N. ambassador John Bolton called the speech “monotonous, pretentious and uninspired” on the National Review Online website.
To the president’s left, Democratic Rep. Dennis Kucinich was alarmed to see Barack Obama justifying the use of force.
When we cover the war with the notion of justice, it is easy to justify the slaughter of innocents, he warned.
The center-left press praised the president.
The Los Angeles Time, while still believing that Obama received the Nobel prematurely, called his "overproduction" speech mostly for a speaker known for his talent.
Oslo’s speech “should serve as a roadmap to guide international decisions in the fight against conflicts, poverty and oppression,” the Californian newspaper said enthusiastically.
Obama "pronounced the speech that was necessary," the New York Times said, judging it "at the same time as bleak and uplifting."
Road transport: three marathon negotiations to guarantee Christmas supply
After three marathonic negotiations sessions, the unions of the road sector and the TLF (Transport and Logistics of France), the only employer organization that remained until the end, reached on Friday afternoon an agreement that puts an end to the strike slogan and the threat that falls on the Christmas supply.
During the signing session on Friday night, Transportation Secretary Dominique Bussereau "joyed" by the agreement, after several negotiating sessions that began Wednesday morning.
After a second night of discussions, negotiators said goodbye to Friday in the hope of quickly signing the deal text late in the morning.
Even though three employers’ organizations (FNTR, Unostra, OTRE) withdrewed slain.
But a meeting of more than six hours that began at 11 a.m. it was necessary for everyone to agree and the text was signed at 18 a.m.
The conflict "would have been very detrimental to companies" and "was feared to disrupt the holidays," Bussereau said.
The text foresees an increase of 4% for the lowest wages (in local and regional road transport), and 2.9% for the highest (international transport).
The hourly rate rises from 8.72 euros to 9.06 for low wages.
It’s not much, but we started with a lot of disadvantage, explained Patrice Clos (FO).
If so, we are going to give the order to our militants that there are no more cuts or blockades in the supply centers.
But there can always be some disconforming rebellion, he added.
Philippe Grillot, president of the TLF, acknowledged that it is "a good agreement", although it was "difficult to grant increases to high wages taking into account international competition".
According to him, the increases will be in the order of 170 euros per month for low wages and 100 to 120 euros for the highest.
It’s a common-sense agreement that respects wage earners, Maxime Dumont (CFDT) stated, while Thierry Douine (CFTC) recognized it as “a historic agreement.”
For Richard Jaubert (CGT) it's "an acceptable compromise".
Likewise, the agreement foresees an increase in travel expenses of 3% and the holding of general road transport assemblies in the first half of 2010, which will make it possible to address above all the issue of extraordinary payment and a modernization of the collective agreement.
Bussereau recalled that "a drop of 100 million euros in employer burdens" was the subject of an amendment by the government in the rectification finance bill for 2009, which the National Assembly voted on Thursday night.
The government will ask the Senate to vote “under the same terms.”
TLF’s signing allows the agreement to apply, in the first place, to the companies attached to this organization and, secondly, to the entire profession, in the event that the Minister of Labour makes an extension.
It will be done "as soon as possible," Bussereau said.
Opposition from employer organizations could only delay the process.
The FNTR denounced that it was a "naïve business", since the 100 million euros "had already been promised to the sector to offset part of the CO2 tax".
The Secretary of State for Transport forced us to negotiate in a situation of urgency, not as a conclusion to an analysis discussed by the parties, but not to oppose those who wish to eat turkey at the next Christmas Eve dinner, Unostra lamented.
For the OTRE, the agreement puts at risk "the stability of many SMEs in the sector over the next 12 months".
RER A: conflicts over the weekend, unions unhappy with RATP proposals
The conflicts in RER A, France’s largest urban public transport line, will continue over the weekend, because at Friday’s meeting, 2nd day of strike between unions and the leadership of the RATP, there was no deal and the unions were dissatisfied with regard to their request for premium.
For this important weekend of Christmas shopping, on Saturday and Sunday only trains will circulate between 10 a.m. and 20 p.m
Interconnection with the SNFC will be suspended in Nanterre Préfecture, forcing passengers to change trains.
The Lafayette Galleries, which await 200,000 customers every day, were happy that “RATP strives to provide service to large stores.”
The RER A crosses from West to East the Paris city.
The RATP section runs from Saint-Germain-en-Laye and Nanterre to Boissy-Saint-Léger and Marne-La-Vallée.
On Friday, about one in two trains circulated in peak hours, and almost no train in valley hours, as planned.
On Thursday there was no disorder on the platforms thanks to the organization of the inhabitants of the Île of France.
In this way, at dawn, Saint-Germain-en-Laye (Yvelines) station was much less crowded than normal, especially due to the absence of students.
In Boissy-Saint-Léger (Val-de-Marne), 29-year-old Jack Nbakina explained that he had risen "an hour and a half earlier to get to schedule."
Saint-Lazare station was not saturated, said SNCF, which had provided for police and law enforcement supervision.
The RER A that operates the SNCF from Cergy-Le-Haut or bound for it arrived or departed the Saint-Lazare station to join the meters.
New general assemblies of striking workers will take place on Saturday and Sunday. The results will be analyzed, "unsatisfactory" according to the unions, of a meeting that took place with the leadership on Friday afternoon.
The director of the RER proposes to negotiate a specific protocol in all aspects for RER A: working time, professional development, etc.
That’s not what drivers who claim a premium they’re owed, according to Thierry Garon (Sud).
The RATP even brackets its premium proposals, the CGT commented outraged, noting that the management "was not able to solve a problem that the SNCF and the road sector had already solved."
A new meeting is scheduled on Monday to carry out "a more extensive analysis on the conditions of exercise of the profession," the RATP said.
Which also led the unions to say that the company “didn’t care about weekend users.”
In the morning, about 150 drivers invaded a committee at headquarters in Paris.
Then the leadership, which until then refused to dialogue during the strike, suggested the meeting "to calm the waters".
The conflict could spread to RER B on Tuesday, after Unsa and the Independants have filed a notice.
The unions ask for a premium of 120 euros (Unsa) to 150 euros, of which 30 euros are variables (CGT, FO, CFDT, Sud, Indépendants) and complain about the degradation of working conditions in this line that has one million daily passengers.
RER drivers, all in the second degree of their professional career, charge 2,200 to 2,700 gross euros and 600 to 650 euros of premiums, for schedules with alternating shifts, Sundays and holidays.
Turkish justice dissolves pro-Kord party, a decision with serious consequences
The Turkish Constitutional Court on Friday ruled in favor of dissolving the country’s main pro-Kurdish party, a decision that immediately sparked demonstrations of anger in the Kurdish community, in the southeast of the country and in Istanbul.
This decision could greatly complicate the government’s task, which has just reached out to the Kurdish community trying to end 25 years of conflict.
The Democratic Society (DTP) party was dissolved because it had become the "focus of activities that harmed the independence of the state and its indivisible unity," President Hasim Kiliç told reporters after four days of deliberations.
The decision of the 11 judges was unanimous, although only a majority of seven votes were needed to obtain the dissolution, Kiliç said, adding that 37 of the leaders of the party, among which are its president Ahmet Türk and MP Aysel Tugluk, are removed from political life for five years.
In addition, the judge announced the cessation of parliamentary immunity enjoyed by Türk and Tugluk, and the confiscation of party property by the Treasury.
The DTP has 21 seats in Parliament (of 550).
Its leaders warned before the verdict that deputies would leave Parliament before they were parliamentarians without partisan label.
The decision arises in the wake of a procedure launched by Supreme Court Prosecutor Abdurrahman Yalçinkaya in 2007, which accuses the DTP of obeying the "principles" of the Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK), considered a terrorist organization by Ankara and by many other countries.
Numerous observers believe the DTP is the legal political showcase of the PKK rebels.
However, the party says it has "no organic links" with the PKK, but refuses to qualify it as a terrorist organization and calls on the government to negotiate with it.
The Court’s verdict intervenes at the moment when the government reaches out to the Kurdish community by proposing a series of measures that strengthen their rights, so that the Kurds stop supporting the PKK and thus put an end to the conflict.
DTP leaders claimed that a dissolution could lead to heightened tensions in Southeast Anatolia, where a large number of demonstrations against the detention conditions of PKK chief Abdullah Öcalan degenerated into clashes with police in recent weeks.
Since the announcement of justice, thousands of protesters gathered in front of DTP premises in Diyarbakir, the great city of the Southeast populated mostly by Kurds.
Law Enforcement dropped tear grenades and used water cannons, while protesters dropped Molotov bombs or slanded stones.
In Istanbul, approximately one hundred people were also concentrated.
Following the verdict, Türk claimed that the justice decision was going to "deepend discouragement."
Turkey cannot resolve this question (Kurdish, NDLR) dissolving a party, he told reporters.
The government, formed by a party from the Islamist movement, also laments the decision.
In principle we are opposed to the closure of parties.
So nothing is resolved, said Energy Minister Taner Yildiz.
This decision completely boycotts the democratic openness launched by the government, political analyst Ahmet Insel said.
We can expect, of course, a PKK reaction, he added.
On Thursday, the PKK claimed an attack that killed seven soldiers three days earlier in northern Turkey, one of the bloodiest attacks in recent months.
Sneaks can suddenly begin
A truly cool winter is what Wolfgang Brauburger wants.
It’s not something you want to make life impossible for citizens with, says the FES’ winter road maintenance director
But with it your employees can learn how to test and master the entirety of vehicle technology.
Meteorologists may announce it for the first time over the weekend.
More than 330 employees and 120 vehicles are ready to deal with snow and ice.
In this case the FES acts in accordance with a thorough plan.
As soon as it snows profusely in Frankfurt and temperatures drop below zero degrees, 20 of the so-called sections A with large salted vehicles, Brauburger explains.
They include, for example, the main arteries and avenues such as Neue Weg and Hanauer Landstraße.
But also accesses for the fire department, public transport routes, pedestrian areas and sections with slopes, such as the Atzelbergstraße.
FES service starts around three in the morning
Approximately 1,040 kilometers should be covered, as necessary, in a maximum of three hours with wet salt, which adheres better.
That’s why the FES starts around three o’clock in the morning, “with it the roads are free until the rush hour traffic begins.”
The difficult thing is when it begins to snow around 7:30 hours.
"Then we have a problem," says the director of road maintenance in winter, "because then nobody is ready to clear space to salted vehicles."
Immediately afterwards, when it is no longer snowing, it touches the secondary and private roads, to which the Rat-Beil-Straße also belongs.
The last priority is the lateral and secondary streets.
That’s where the inconvenience is generated, Brauburger knows well.
Around five o’clock in the morning the FES infantry is launched, dangerous points and cruises are mainly cleared.
Usually the FES couldn’t take care of all bike lanes.
In Frankfurt there is a special service, which from 22 to 4:30 a.m
As these quickly become slippery, the salted vehicle starts as soon as the temperature drops below two degrees.
In case of an emergency, the police can also call them.
The Cradle of the Dinosaur
The Tawa has the same appearance as many other carnivorous dinosaurs: an animal two meters long and 1.50 meters high that ran on the two strong hind legs, had an elongated head with sharp and curved teeth, short arms with sickle-shaped claws and a long tail.
If the species had lived a couple of millions of years later, it wouldn’t have been something special.
But Tawa was one of the first dinosaurs with these bodily characteristics, reports Sterling Nesbitt of the University of Texas at Austin and colleagues in Thursday’s journal Science.
The species lived about 215 million years ago in what is now New Mexico, which at the time was in a dry area of the supercontinent Pangea in the equator.
Tawa opened a new window into the early evolution of dinosaurs, Nesbitt says.
It shows us a lot about the genealogical relationships, propagation and characteristics of early dinosaurs.
Nesbitt and other paleontologists began an excavation at Ghost Ranch in northern New Mexico in 2006.
At times, we’ve found in a day a dozen dinosaur bones, this is unprecedentented for Triassic sediments, reports Randall Irmis a member of the Utah Natural History Museum team.
The researchers quickly identified some bones as belonging to a new species.
In the course of the excavation they gathered in total the remains of five specimens.
A finding confirms theories about the ancient history of dinosaurs
The name "Tawa" comes from the sun god of the Hopi Indians.
For Sterling Nesbitt is an exceptional finding: "Triassic dinosaur fossils are extremely rare, usually only individual fragments are found."
Other dinosaur experts don’t fully share such enthusiasm.
This finding is not particularly spectacular, but it confirms some theories about the ancient history of dinosaurs, says for example Martin Sander of the University of Bonn.
According to today’s current, dinosaurs first appeared about 230 million years ago.
But many of the oldest fossils are incomplete, so the classification of these early dinosaurs is quite controversial.
Thus, experts debate, for example, about when saurians were divided into their three most important genealogical lineages: carnivorous theropodes including Tyrannosaurus Rex, Velociraptor and also birds, quadrupedic sauropods known as "long-necked" and their relatives, and herbivorous Ornithishia (saurian
According to Nesbitt's analysis and colleagues, Tawa founde is a primitive theropod.
It shows an interesting combination of primitive and progressive properties, Nesbitt says.
For example, in Tawa’s skull the researchers found indications of air pockets, such as those also possessed by descendants of theropods, birds.
Thanks to air pockets, the respiration of birds is more efficient than that of mammals.
In Tawa’s case he also found the bones of the spine were evidently partially filled with air.
Tawa is the theropod with oldest and most primitive airbags, says Randall Irmis.
Because the related line of subsequent giant sauropods also possessed pockets of air and light bones, it was likely that the common predecessors of both groups already produced this useful innovation.
At least three migratory waves to North America
Because the most primitive dinosaurs so far known lived in South America, the researchers deduce that saurians first emerged there.
Consequently, the three genealogical lineages were initially separated and spread from South America in all corners of the Earth, which at that time still formed a single landmass, the supercontinent Pangea.
However, the facts say that the team found along with Tawa finding the remains of two other theropods not very closely related.
All three can't descend from a uniquished predecessor, the researchers argue.
We believe at least three migratory waves were given to North America, says Alan Turner of the American Museum of Natural History in New York, co-author of the Science study.
Of course, there are also opponents.
An even older species, Silesaurus, belonging to a sister group of dinosaurs, lived in present-day Poland, says Bonn paleontologist Martin Sander.
His opinion could give a distorted image, because simply in South America many dinosaur fossils continue to be preserved, while on other continents there is hardly anything to discover.
The question of how dinosaurs managed to evolve from a relatively rare collateral line of reptiles to the dominant terrestrial vertebrates of the Mesozoic remains an enigma.
Meanwhile, in the Triassic dinosaurs still shared their living space with many other reptiles, for example with the ancestors of today’s crocodiles and pterosaurs and with many other saurians.
Towards the end of the Triassic the dinosaurs spread all over the world and many new species developed, while kinship became extinct at the boundary of the Triassic towards the Jurassic.
The success stories weren't eternal
There are two hypotheses to explain this change, says Martin Sander.
There may have been a mass extinction to which the dinosaurs survived by chance, but the other reptiles did not.
Or dinosaurs were better adapted, so they were able to defeat opponents in the area.
Success stories aren’t always eternal.
150 million years later, a meteorite impact slows the rise of a group of species, which had also already evolved in the Triassic and have since had a miserable existence: mammals.
Solidarity from Vienna to São Paulo
The list is long and international signatories.
Professors from the University of Toronto, Cambridge, Vienna or Berkeley, California participate.
Teachers from Naples, Quebec, Edinburgh, New York, Sao Paulo, Berlin or Bremen participate.
Police eviction from Frankfurt’s Goethe University’s busy casino impacted around the globe.
278 professors and professors from Goethe University, although also from higher education institutions throughout Germany, Europe and North and South America, stand in solidarity with students in protest at the Westend Campus.
In a statement drafted in German and English, they "reject" police eviction promoted by the board of directors.
Werner M�üller-Estler, president of the university, evacuated last week the building occupied by students and teachers on an educational strike, because the walls and works of art were covered in propaganda.
As a result, five students suffered injuries.
The material damage, the signatories judges, does not justify "the violent dissolution of the courses organized by the same students and professors by a police command."
The eviction represented an "unacceptable attack" on freedom of investigation and learning.
The exclusive focus on vandalism served for "delegitimization and criminalization of protest" and diverted attention from content.
The board has refused to enter into the necessary negotiations.
Teachers demand the immediate withdrawal of the judicial claim.
Most occupants have not caused material damage, it is said.
Legitimized Protest
Also the young people of the Verdi union and the German Federation of Trade Unions stand in solidarity with the students.
The protest is the legitimate expression of a growing concern for one's own future, says Verdi's national youth secretary Alexander Klein.
Eva Kühne-Hörmann (CDU), Hessen’s Minister of Sciences defended, however, the performance of the rector of the university and the police.
We are grateful to the rector of the university.
Destruction and occupation are totally unacceptable means, he said in an Aktuelle Stunde broadcast Thursday in the Wiesbaden Province Parliament.
We are sympathetic with the protest, but we are not sympathetic to vandalism, said Peter Beuth, delegate of the provincial parliament by the CDU.
The opinion of the opposition factions, the SPD, the Greens and the left, is different.
The CDU pretexted the painted walls in order to criminalize the protest, said Janine Wissler (Left).
Instead of thoroughly studying the arguments and demands of the students, they are evicted.
The Left has tabled a motion requesting the Minister of the Interior to submit a report on police intervention.
New Silicon Valley
For many years, it was regarded as a secret city, inaccessible to Western visitors.
Selenograd, one of the ten districts of Moscow, was in the Soviet Union a heavily guarded siege for the secret arms investigation.
Today, the community of 216,000 inhabitants, located 37 kilometers from the center of Moscow, is considered the "Russian Silicon Valley".
There are important research and production centers for micro and nanoelectronics here.
Since September 2008, representatives of Selenograd have contacted Riedberg’s scientific headquarters in Frankfurt, university institutes and Frankfurt Innovation Centre (FIZ).
There were a number of mutual visits and now, on Wednesday, Selenograd and Riedberg signed in Frankfurt a "Closer Collaboration Protocol".
Moscovite Deputy Mayor Juri Rosljak and Selengrad Mayor Anatoly Smirnov led the Russian delegation; by Frankfurt the head of planning services Edwin Schwarz signed.
Representatives of FIZ and directors of small and medium-sized enterprises of the natural sciences research centre will travel to a seminar in Selenograd in 2010.
Scientific exchange
It's an ongoing scientific exchange.
But it also addresses business relationships.
FIZ companies will have the possibility to set foot in the growing Russian market.
In return Russian scientific companies wish to have access to contracts in Germany.
Selenograd is the headquarters of important Russian companies and research institutes.
Among them are, for example, the Moscovite Institute of Electronic Technology, the large Sitronics and Rossijskaja Elektronika consortiums.
The community was founded in 1958 as the so-called "City of Socialist Planning" for weapons research.
Selenograd (literally translated as "Green City") received its name from the wooded mountain landscape, in which research facilities were established.
Brakes from Stuttgart
Dimension Stuttgart 21 is not placed at all high enough.
For the supporters of the project it is simply and plainly "the new heart of Europe".
S21 law is much more than the reduction of a railway station, more than a monstrous construction project, which will precipitate into the capital of the Banden-W�ürttemberga region a decade of chaos of irremediable construction sites.
Stuttgart 21 emerged from the spirit of the 1980s.
Terminals with rails, which end up in the middle of the city, were then considered 19th-century engineering fossils, which acted as brakes on modern rail traffic, since an enormous amount of time was formerly needed to hook locomotives back and forth.
Rolling, stop dry continuing the journey; but so it must be done.
And that’s why the terminals must have disappeared: in Frankfurt and Munich.
And in Stuttgart: a passage station, which is located about halfway along the path of the trans-European operating lines between Paris and Budapest/Bratislava; hence the slogan "Heart of Europe".
The S21 will really be initiated, even though the visions of the 80’s are no longer more compatible with reality.
Even the most ambitious designers have known that in the Hessian and Bavarian capitals it is almost priceless to implement reduction.
Railway terminals, of course, also do not have more entry and exit problems, since there are reversible locomotives with tractor unit and driver carriage at both ends.
However: Stuttgart 21 including the new section built ICE towards Ulm will already be started.
Federal and communal politicians have arranged it this way.
The railway continues, also because the risks of the large individual infrastructure projects of the Republic are carried out by the taxpayer and not by the State.
From an urban point of view, the project is still justified to some extent.
It creates new urban space, equipped with many green areas, probably from the computer-generated graphics of the designer.
For the rail transport system the S21 is harmful.
The passage station and the road support are designed for the transport of people, which was attributed to the enormous growth typical of the 1980's.
At that time there was no competition from low-cost flights.
But modern transport policy must first take care of strengthening the transport of goods, as rail transport is considerably more environmentally friendly than that carried out by trucks on the roads.
However, the new sections under construction are good for freight trains due to intense gradients.
Even more serious is that the S21 also indirectly affects many other railway projects.
More than six billion euros must be buried in the Zuavos.
That's the official figure.
Experts consider it to be most realistic between ten and fifteen billion.
It is certain that in the next decade the money will be missing in other crossings and narrow passages on the network.
A total of sixty major projects are listed by the Ministry of Transport.
One of the most important is the extension of more than 180 kilometers of long rails through the Rhine valley.
Since 1987 the layout has been built at a slow pace.
It would be urgent here to step on the accelerator properly.
However, the S21 acts as a brake.
The section of the Rhine Valley will be the bottleneck for the international transport of goods: through the heart of Europe.
The government examines the judgment on the airport.
The federal government leaves the Wiesbaden regional parliament pending, if it will file an appeal before the ruling on the ban on night flights of the Hesse administrative court.
The CDU/FDP coalition appears to have tasked experts with legal investigation of more than 400-page VGH sentencing, Economy Minister Dieter Posch (FDP) said.
The administrative court in Kassel had substantiated in writing on this date, that in the case of the construction of the north-west airstrip planned at the Frankfurt airport, the federal state would have to re-regulate the non-night flights.
The admission of 17 flights between 23 and 5 hours, as foreseen by the federal government, would not be conciliable with the mandatory legal protection of the noise population due to air traffic.
The coalition has to decide until January 4, if it will file an appeal against this sentence.
The opposition criticized this postponement and pressured government representatives to waive the appeal before the nation’s Administrative Court in Leipzig.
The head of the SPD group, Torsten Schäfer-Gümbel, urged the CDU and the FDP to respect their promises to prohibit night air traffic, which they had made before the elections. Otherwise this could be taken as a lack of speech.
It's quite difficult, if one is allowed to keep his word, said Green chief Tarek Al-Wazir, referring to FDP's slogan "We keep our word."
Minister Posch rejected Schäfer-Gümbel’s recrimination, which the federal government would be trying through misrepresentations of law and tricks still to get its overnight flights.
Thorous legal research would take quite a while.
The coalition did not want to act on the basis of a “fast first impression.”
