University careers are cleared
Partial success for students: After a year of education-related strikes, the Ministers of Education and Culture of the Länder finally conceded. In a meeting of the Ministers of Education and Culture with university rectors, both sides agreed on trying to improve the conditions of study in undergraduate and master's cases.
Students in the future should be able to pass their careers.
For example, they agreed to reduce the burden of exams: basically each module of the race must end with a single examination.
In addition, it is intended to examine the workload of the students, and wants to guarantee a "real and justifiable measure".
Politics and universities also want to simplify the recognition of test results between universities inside and outside Germany.
Federated states are committed to more flexibly preparing structural rules together for Bachelor and Master careers.
Regulations of the Länder that diverge from this must no longer exist.
Fewer exams
Since the beginning of the student protests in June the Ministers of Education and Culture and the rectors of the universities continuously talk about the improvement of being able to carry out and successfully conclude a career (student).
Now for the first time there has been talk of specific content.
The so-called accreditation council agreed to dramatically reduce the number of exams: “Each module finishes with a single exam,” it is said in a 30-page document, which aims to partially regulate the approval of university careers.
The "significant reduction in the burden of examinations" is intended to avoid development in the wrong direction, which would significantly affect studyability, this was explained by the director of the accreditation board, Mr. Reinhold Grimm.
In addition in the future "studability" must be the decisive criterion in the configuration.
Modules and contents should be thoroughly examined regarding unnecessary restrictions of students in choosing.
the courses.
Students would have "the right to obtain more spaces, which enable and encourage self-initiation."
These rules would be valid for new races, but also in the examination of existing races.
These must be "accredited" again every five years. But this would mean, that it would take until 2015 to examine all races.
Those responsible for this would be country-wide 10 accreditation agencies, which are under the supervision of the accreditation council, which is installed by the Länder.
Ministers of education and cultury urged the states in October to influence agencies in such a way that the number of subjects and exams are not too strict or demanding.
In some Länder, universities and technical schools have already agreed with ministries to take such measures.
So far only Rhine-Platinate claimed to provide additional means for the subsequent development of the Bologna process.
With this money, universities must implement student mentoring and counseling programs, as well as strengthen examination and overseas offices.
The Lçâth and the universities intend to have 10 million euros for these purposes.
In early 2010 the government wants to bring to parliament a novel of university law.
According to her a single examination should be enough for the completion of a career model.
Career configuration should be made more flexible, in addition we want to guarantee the recognition of evaluations in other universities and facilitate accessibility to the Master.
Until the end of next year it is intended to examine all races about their "studibility".
On Thursday the government and the opposition again discussed the conditions of studying in the federal state.
Apart from the praise there was also criticism about the decisions.
In the board of directors at the federal level of Juso (young socialistic) university groups it was demanded that the configuration of educational policy should no longer be carried out "after closed doors" of ministers and university rectors.
At the latest after the education strikes, students as main stakeholders should now be able to express their opinion as well.
Margret Wintermantel, chairman of the board of university rectors (HRK), supported the students: “We need the experience of the students,” she said Thursday at a meeting with the Ministers of Education, with whom HRK deliberated about a joint reform document.
For the education and science union (GEW) the Bonn agreements were not entirely sufficient: the union requires a legal right for each student with the Bachelor’s degree the ability to access a later Master’s career.
In addition, the Lçade states have to ensure that for the current generation of Bachelor’s students there are enough places of study for Master in the future with the best possible study conditions. This was reported by a member of the GEW, Mr. Andreas Keller.
Federated states have to hire a lot more teachers.
But about greater access to the master’s degree, the Ministers of Education did not comment.
More than a footnote about Herta Müller
"Entering and leaving Germany is practically unchecked" reads the first line of a poem by lyricist Werner Söllner in unbalancing idiomatic fragments, in which he themes the experiences of arrival in Germany lived by a dissident, who won his freedom, but lost the environment of his language.
Werner Söllner, who was born in 1951 in Banat, Romania, initially worked as a German and English teacher in Bucharest, and then as a reader in a children's book publisher, before moving to Frankfurt in 1982.
Söllner was given several prizes of literature, in 1993 he gave the memorable lessons or poetry talks in Frankfurt at Goethe University, and since 2002 he is director of the literature forum at Hesse at the Mousonturm in Frankfurt.
He gave a lot to talk last Tuesday during a meeting in Munich about "German Literature in Romania in the mirror and the disfigured mirror of his Securitate files"
Visibly insecure and embarrassed, Söllner read a statement in which he admitted participation as IM in the Romanian secret service Securitate in front of German-Romanian fellow writers, among others Richard Wagner, William Totok, Franz Hodjak, and Helmuth Frauendorfer.
In conjunction with some of those present in 1989, Söllner had received the German Language Award.
Attempts at recruitment had slowly begun in early 1971.
"Thereforely I'm someone who didn't defend himself sufficiently in the face of Securitate's attempts to provoke fear," Söllner concluded.
Werner Söllner is IM Walter.
Almost at the same time the award of the Nobel Prize for literature to Herta Müller and his unparalleled literary controversy with the mechanisms of dictatorial mastery, the literary scene is shaken and especially the community of German authors from Romania for a case that evidences the abysses and evil of a political system, which is based on control and espionage.
Werner Söllner is, from what is known so far, he is not only a perpetrator, but also a victim.
In his Securitate IM Walter record he had played a role, literary scientist Michael Markel said after Söllner's confession, and stressed the positive influence for him of IM Walter.
He had excused or defended him at all difficult points for him, Markel said.
"I feel the moral need to make this claim," he said.
Söllner’s statement in this form and in front of this public was unexpected, the confession of an ex-dissident and his work with the Romanian secret service, however, is not so surprising.
Germany would have been a comfortable refuge for spies of the Securitate-Spitzel gewesen, wrote Herta Müller as early as July in the "Zeit" weekly, after having read its own 914-page file.
Those involved will surely have speculated and known in some cases, what names were hidden behind the pseudonyms of Sorin, Voicu and also Walter.
Contrary to the review of Stasi dossiers with a controlled procedure in Germany, the treatment with the Securitate’s control files is still quite diffuse.
Systematic clarification was boycotted and hindered in recent years.
A national council for the clarification of Securitate (CNSAS), corresponding to the Birthler authority, was formed in 1999, and the delivery of the files since then is given rather to traces.
Since 2005, there has been a law regulating the administration of files.
An interview, after the award of the Nobel Prize for literature to Herta Müller, proves that the influence of former members of the Securitate in Romania is still quite large. In this interview a former head of the Temesvar Securitate spoke despictively of the decorated.
“She has psychosis,” in the end she was interrogated only once.
The infamy of power seemed to be able to show without turns in the form of freedom of opinion.
Of course, unavoidable questions arise with the Söllner case, as so many times when there are silent guilt.
What just now? To whom was caused harm? The calm reaction of the listeners in the face of Söllner’s confession will hope that the continuation of this story will be governed more by the craving for knowledge and not so much for the adjudication of guilt and shame.
For the genesis of Herta Müller's work, which yesterday accepted the Nobel Prize in literature, it is more than a simple footnote.
