World heavyweight championship fight
Fiery duel in Bern: Again and again challenger Kevin Johnsonn was provoking world champion Vitali Klitschko with words and gestures.
But after twelve hard rounds, the Ukrainian defended his heavyweight title in the WBC version.
Judgments were unanimous.
Hamburg: Vitali Klitschko remains world heavyweight champion.
The 38-year-old WBC version champion beat U.S. Kevin Johnson on Friday-Saturday-to-Saturday by unanimous decision for points (120:108, 120:108, 119:109).
Klitschko was his third successful title defender since his return in October 2008.
Previously, the Ukrainian was already a two-time WBO and WBC world champion.
Unfortunately, it couldn’t perform the knockout everyone had expected.
Klitschko extended his balance to 39 wins in 41 professional fights, twice having to give up due to his injuries.
Challenger Johnson had to acquait the first loss in his #24 fight.
In front of about 17000 spectators in the Bern Arena, Klitschko, with a stature of 2.02 meters, wanted to dictate the fight above all with his left hand.
But Johnson, eleven centimeters lower, who before the duel had proclaimed the end of the Klitschko era, was dodging again and again the blows of the world champion, so Klitschko could not decisively bring his dangerous right-handed target to his goal.
In general terms, the passive Johnson did too little for a challenger, and only tried to pass the rounds.
Whill he was provoking Klitschko with words and gestures, his blows only rarely caught up with the world champion.
However, he is now the second boxer, after Timo Hoffmann in 2000, who endured twelve full rounds with Klitschko.
Prior to the fight, the challenger had already produced some scandal in the Swiss capital.
He had insulted Klitschko as “horrible zombie,” he “can’t do anything.”
In performing the weigh-in, the champion left himself on Friday to remove Johnson’s sunglasses from his nose “so he could see his eyes.”
Fourth victory after Klitschko's return
Also in his fourth fight after his return on 11 October 2008, Klitschko remained invincible.
After a nearly four-year hiatus, he beat Nigerian Samuel Peter in Berlin at the time, and had regained "his" WBC belt, which he had previously had to deliver without a fight.
That year he later won in his defenses of the title to Juan Carlos Gomez, of Cuba, and in September to Chris Arreola, of the United States.
In this fight I only got a hit, and I'm in very good physical condition, Klitschko had explained his quick return to the ring.
But his dream is still to reunite all four major titles in his family.
His brother Vladimir is the WBO and IBF world champion.
However, the belt belongs to the Briton David Haye, after he dethroned in November Nikolai Valujev, 2.13 meters tall.
In the previous summer, Haye had canceled a fight against Vitali Klitschko planned for September.
Supposedly, the company promoting him is already planning the fight against Klitschko at Wembley Stadium in London.
Kunduz case
Opposition frontal attack: The head of the Green group, Jürgen Trittin, called Defense Minister Gutenberg a liar because of false information on the Kunduz affair.
The SPD and the leftists demand explanation from the chancellor.
Hamburg/Berlin – After the most recent revelations about Kunduz’s air attack, the opposition has reproached the Minister of Defense, Karl-Theodor zu Gutenberg (CSU), to have knownly deceived.
In Saturday's "Tagesschau" news broadcast, aired on the ARD television channel, the head of the Green group, Jürgen Trittin, said the minister "knowingly hadn't told the truth" about the deadly two-convoy bombing.
In such a case, it is usually said: he told lies.
The order to kill would have been taken into account the rules of the International Protection Troop for Afghanistan (ISAF).
This is the essence of NATO's report, which Mr. Gutenberg has read, Trittin said.
On Friday it had been announced that in this attack it was not only about destroying the hijacked trucks, but also intentionally killing two Taliban leaders.
In the U.S. airstrike, requested by Kunduz’s commander, Colonel Klein, according to the NATO investigation report, was killed or injured up to 142 people.
The Greens, the SPD and the left on Saturday urged U.S. Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) to speak immediately before the federal parliament and to clarify what Gutenbeng really knew about the incident.
Ms. Merkel has to clarify whether a strategy of intentional murder is part of the federal government’s Afghanistan policy, and if the chancery, army and information service have approved this new strategy, Jürgen Trittin and his Green colleague Renate Künast demanded.
Government spokesman Ulrich Wilhelm decidedly rejected the criticism.
The chancellory had no influence on concrete actions by the German army in Afghanistan, he said.
On Saturday, doubts had alsourded doubts about the Ministry's presentation on the reasons for the firing of Inspector General Wolfgang Schneiderhan and Secretary of State Peter Wichert.
According to SPIEGEL information, both had informed Gutenberg correctly and completely about the background of the attack on the convoys.
It had officially been said that they had denied the existence of NATO reports and had concealed sensitive details from the Minister.
However, from the vicinity of the two high-ranking officials it transpired that they had even pointed out to the Ministry of Defense the existence of several reports, including a two-page document of Colonel Klein, who was in command, and a report of a field patrol.
Meanwhile, Wichert reportedly asked in a letter to clarify the matter, reports the newspaper "Frankfurter Allgemeiner Sonntagszeitung".
So far he has not received a reply, it was said.
The ministry did not express itself on Saturday with regard to the reproaches that are made, and said that this would be clarified in the commission of inquiry of the Federal Parliament, which will be constituted next Wednesday.
Onyric worlds
In a state of wakefulness, some people see images and hear voices that others don’t see or hear.
Not only in the case of the mentally ill and in drug addicts the boundaries between imagination and reality vanish.
As long as it happens during sleep, everything is in order.
When we’re lying in our dark rooms with our eyes closed, we can unhinderedly give ourselves to the wildest imaginations and creations of fantasy.
However, whoever experiences sleep-like states during wakefulness, or sees and hears things that others don’t see or hear, is most often regarded in the modern Western world as crazy or drug addict, or at least as a maniac.
But such images and voices do not only arise in the mentally ill or drugged.
Guilt has our imagination: the same neural processes that allow us to design houses, painting paintings or writing novels, sometimes blur the boundaries between sleep, madness and reality.
Onyrically altered states of alert consciousness can appear in the most diverse forms, at best we call them visions, and at worst, madness.
Although the triggers and the effects can be distinguished, you can refer to similar processes the visions of the mystic Hildegard von Bingen, the psychedelic experiences of the New Age prophet Carlos Castañeda, the states of shamanic possession, and the psychosis and hallucinations.
Hallucinations or delusions arise when the balance of neuronal communication substances in the brain – especially dopamine, noradrenaline, serotonin and acetylcholine – has been derailed and the interaction between various parts of it is disturbed.
During schizophrenic psychosis very often complex hallucinations and crazed thoughts appear.
Gravity no longer has power over him
The relationship between madness and sleep has occupied philosophers and physicians since ancient times.
Aristotle conceived hallucinations as a form of waking sleep, Wilhelm Griesinger, one of the founders of modern psychiatry, observed in 1861 a "great likeness of the state of madness with the onyrical states", for Sigmund Freud, the dream was even "a psychosis, with all nonsenses, crippled formations,
It’s the fall of 2001, when Henning T. becomes a comic book figure.
His body is two-dimensional and outrageously colorful.
Gravity no longer has power over it, it's floating near the ceiling of the room.
He's feeling a kick.
Behind him, figures of demons with wings, with red grimaces like blood, deformed, grinding their teeth.
They're chasing him, they kick him with heavy and flaming boots, they throw him against the walls, he feels the pains all over his body.
Strident and evil voices resonate from walls and radio.
You can't stand them anymore, the voices that insult and offend you.
Henning T. rips two ropes from the stereo, mooring them to form a hanging knot, and fixes them on a hook on the ceiling.
Get into a honk and put the knob around your neck.
Jump.
Rope's released.
Lesshandly, shout the voices in a choir that blows up the eardrums, “Go to the clinic and make them heal you!”
Now, Henning T. is 40 years old and has experienced six acute psychoses, the last two years ago, the first almost 20 years ago.
The cause that produced it was LSD.
Its diagnosis is schizo-affective-paranoid psychosis, it is also maniaco-depressive, it takes medications, against depression, mania, schizophrenia.
He has learned to live with his illness, for three years he has been married.
In psychosis I have experienced the most terrible anguish and deepest desperation, he says.
Hallucinations are a cruel reality
Approximately one percent of the population falls ill at least once in schizophrenia life; in psychiatric clinics it is treated as the second most frequent disease after depression.
In an acute schizophrenic psychosis, most often those affected can no longer distinguish between the outside and the inner world, and fall into a state of overexcitation that can lead to hallucinations, delusions of greatness, disturbances in the relationship, or persecutory mania.
Especially widespread is the hearing of voices: voices that give orders, comment or dialogue.
Optical hallucinations are also frequent.
The affected person is no longer able to distinguish how important it is, the brain can no longer filter the disturbing signals, consciousness is flooded with impressions.
Like the dream, psychosis also opens the floodgates to a torrent of ideas and fantasies that come from the deepest layers of consciousness.
The perception of reality in psychosis is centered on the subject, says Professor Thomas Fuchs, chief physician at Heidelberg University Hospital’s general psychiatry clinic.
Schizophrenic is at the center of events, everything has a meaning addressed to him.
At the same time it enters into a passive role, as in a dream, is overwhelmed by events and cannot actively organize them.
That is why schizophrenics often feel threatened, controlled and manipulated.
In general it is not possible to adjust reality to them, hallucinations are a cruel reality, to awaken, impossible.
Have experienced a hallucination once in a lifetime
Contrary, in a dream, Fuchs says, "the spatial and sensory structure of reality that are perceived in psychosis remain in principle as is."
Paranoid thoughts and hallucinations are based on reality, the distancing from experience takes place in reality.
For researchers still an unknown why our brains make cabrioles.
According to Fuchs, states of emergency can lead anyone to lose contact with reality provisionally.
But not everyone develops psychosis.
Apparently there is a genetic disposition for schizophrenia, but studies in twins have shown that in addition to genes there are also other determining factors.
Investigators assume that those affected suffer minimal brain damage.
We suspect that the neuronal maturity of the brain is impaired in an early stage of development, such as through a viral disease of the mother during pregnancy, Fuchs explains.
But it is still an unknown how this alteration of the neural system occurs, which also does not manifest at all for many years.
Also the influence of the environment plays an important role – stress or a traumatic experience can be the triggers of a psychosis when there is already a previous disposition.
Likewise, hallucinogenic drugs can lead to psychosis, since they interfere with the brain’s transmitting system.
When the brain heats up
The illogical thing about all this is that psychotic people suffer with their hallucinations and take very expensive medications to get rid of them, while others pay for drugs that allow them to experience some, says Lübeck’s Erich Kasten, professor of psychology.
Perceiving a hallucination as pleasing or not often depends on the point of view.
Kasten has collected reports on hallucinations and visions for ten years and has analyzed the results in the book Die irreale Welt in unserem Kopf, published last year.
Hallucinations are among the typical accompanying symptoms of psychosis, but also, surprisingly, of many other diseases.
Among them are, along with psychic disorders such as borderline personality disorder or depressions, diseases such as migraine, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis, Alzheimer’s and head trauma.
Surprisingly and under certain conditions, the brains of healthy people also sufferes hallucinations.
Hallucinations, according to Kasten, are made up of memories that are normally repressed.
They arise when areas of the brain in which sensory perceptions are processed are hyperactive or poorly cohibited; when our brain is hot, but also when stimulis are lacking.
Drugs can cause this state in the same way as brain damage or psychosis.
But also stimulis restriction, stress, evil of loves or other exceptional brain states.
In jüngeren Studien gab bis zu einem Drittel der Befragten an, minestens einmal im Leben eine Halluzination erlebt zu haben.
In the summer of 2005, Sarah K was 16 years old, living in a small town in Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania and went to high school.
An ordinary girl.
If it weren’t for those images and the voices.
Sometimes, during recess, it happens that an awkward boy with long, dark hair addresses the word.
When she responds, the other students look at her surprised; no one, apart from her, hears or sees the boy.
They think the shy girl's a strange dreamer, but Sarah K knows she's not dreaming.
He's waked and he sees and hears that boy, the same way he sees and hears the girl sitting on the bench next door.
You may even feel your presence, at least a few moments.
It's a nice feeling, he likes the boy.
Sometimes she wonders if she's crazy.
He also knows that the boy and other visions are not real and disappear.
At night, apparitions are alarming.
Visions are often a burden.
When you’re in class, sometimes you can’t follow the lesson because the teacher – in your eyes and ears – is presenting a new partner, while for the rest you visibly and audibly explain mathematical equations.
At night, apparitions are alarming.
While waiting in the dark room for the dream, which is often late and is scarce, he does not know how to distinguish if he is awake or dreaming, if the images he sees, the grimaces or the girl with bleeding cuts in arms and legs, appear in front of his eyes open or closed.
Leave me alone, yell at the figures.
His parents and friends haven't told them anything, they'd think she's crazy.
Autumn 2009, Sarah K. is today20 years old.
She approved selectivity last year and lives with her boyfriend.
It's been a year since hallucinations disappeared.
Since she's come into contact online with psychologist Kasten, she knows she's not crazy.
Madness is also a work of art out of desperation.
According to Kasten, when a person is socially isolated or suffers a restriction of stimuli, it is very likely that after a while he will begin to have hallucinations.
I think that the brain is unoccupied and takes care of looking for entertainment.
Sarah K., an one daughter, was in a small fear of the other children and often played alone.
His hallucinations began when, at the age of 13, he learned that the man he calls dad since he has used reason isn’t his biological father.
Since visual and acoustic hallucinations are active in the same areas of the brain that also process real impressions and noises, those affected as Sarah K. They find it difficult to distinguish between visions and reality. “Each experience,” Kasten says, “takes place in the brain, reality is projected there.
Hallucinations are equally images that the brain produces, that's why they seem so real."
Kasten also classifies nocturnal fantasies as a form of hallucination.
Hallucinations of healthy people do not fundamentally differentiate from acute psychosis.
Contrary to schizophrenics, psychically healthy people are able to recognize their visions as unreal.
Kasten therefore advises his patients to appreciate their hallucinations as a special skill.
Situation boundary between sleep and hallucination
In this overly consciously-focused world, imaginary experiences are usually pathologized, says Michael Schmidt-Degenhard, professor at Heidelberg and chief physician at the Florence-Nightingale-Krankenhauses hospital psychiatry and psychotherapy clinic in Düsseldorf.
A psychosis is a hard and painful disease but it also entails something positive and creative – madness is a work of desperation.
Schmidt-Degenhard has researched the so-called oneirism, a special form of consciousness, which is perceived as a dream.
Dream hallucinations are almost very complex and detailed dreams that the person who experiences them considers real and who subsequently cannot differentiate from being awake.
Onyrical conscious states arise in extreme situations associated with trauma or disease, such as in patients with polyradiculoneuropathy, who experience motor paralysis while completely conscious. die bei vollem Bewusstsein eine motorische Lähmung erleben.
According to Schmidt-Degenhardt, when a person is threatened by a loss of oneself or the world due to a total and permanent motor incapacity, it replaces a very dangerous real situation with its own and imaginary world that is decoupled from reality.
A form of boundary between sleep and hallucination.
Since it is not possible to overcome a situation in reality, there is a change of world, although the real situation is still present in the imagination.
An intensive way to give meaning and interpretation to an unbearable situation in itself that our own capacity for creation allows us not to sink into the nothingness of the loss of consciousness.
Everything is real, even later in memory
Art historian Peter Cornelius Claussen of Zurich also describes his dreamlike experiences in his book Herzenwechsel as a soul-saving action.
She was 49 years old, when her conscience embarked on multi-day journeys after a heart valve operation with heart transplant included, while her body was doomed to immobility at the intensive care station.
Claussen immersed himself in a world created by him and visited strange places of other times: he was bedridden, an older man aboard a sanatorium on a train, a robot watched over his health.
It was seen in the High Middle Ages, surrounded by nobles producing narcotics from blood.
A Greek on a motorcycle abducted him in his country of origin, surfed on his hospital bed in the Alps with firn snow and found a Korean mobster.
It gives everything in these imaginary facts, everything is real, even later in memories.
For Claussen, these inner journeys are fundamentally different from any dream.
Dreaming is a different state, thus describing your experience.
On the contrary, the memories of mental travel are, many years later, much clearer, even in irrelevant details.
Clearer and more intensive even than real experiences.
The intensity of this experience dilutes the horizon from the rest of our everyday experiences, adds psychiatrist Schmidt-Degenhard.
Finally, oneirism, psychosis, hallucinations, and narcoticity consumption show how fragile the inner reproduction of outer reality is, even when we are awake.
Each modification of the state of consciousness, psychologist Kasten summarizes, "can lead to a deformation of what we call reality."
An experience that, on the other hand, can be completely enriching.
