Burda
The children will take charge of the publisher at the age of 27
The new publisher Kallen has reached a provisional solution: Hubert Burda wants to leave his company in the future in the hands of his two children, he told SPIEGEL.
100% of the holding will also be in the hands of the family.
Hamburg – Publisher Hubert Burda sees his company in the hands of his children in the future.
The two sons become heirs at the age of 27 and have the firm will to enter the company, Burda, 69, confirmed to SPIEGEL.
Details have been arranged as a precaution.
The next question is whether they will actively participate in the company.
Also, if they're trained for it.
One can’t put their children in front of 7,500 workers, if they’re not trained enough to do so.
That would end with the children and also with the company.
Burda announced Thursday that he will retire from January of the presidency of the board of directors and that Paul-Bernhard Kallen has been appointed his successor.
This member of the Kallen family, of 52, has been a member of Burda's management since 1999.
At first he was responsible for technology.
Later, he took care of the treasury and with this came the responsibility for the assets of the company. After the departure of Jürgen Todenhöfer also took charge of direct marketing, abroad, printing and financies.
For the first time, no member of the family occupies the cusp of the publishing house.
Burda’s children are 19 and 17 years old. His son Felix, who would currently be 42 years old, died a few years ago.
Kallen's designation also serves to, "to bridge the differences of 10, 15 years", according to Burda.
100% of the holding will also be in the hands of the family.
Looking at Schickedanz and Schaeffle, Burda said that family businesses are also not safe from decay: "This has always affected families who are contagious because of the quest for capital, which they have misjudged and thought they could rise as well as the largest player.
This has never been my strategy.
I've never had "delusions of greatness," Burda says.
I don’t see myself as a political preceptor of this country, as Springer was, and I don’t want to play “Time” in the world league either; those businesses have become too big.
Integral Beauty
