FonPeek's promise
Peek is extremely limited, a device that only serves to look at the mail, with no possibility even to see a certain type of attachments or surf the network.
He enjoyed his five minutes of fame in the U.S. when Time magazine named him one of the products of the year.
Seeing him on the street is more difficult, the truth, but the idea can be interesting, especially for young people.
Unlike smartphones, Peek has a very low price and a very low monthly fee, less than half of what is paid, for example, by the iPhone’s data plan.
Given the success, the company has released two new devices: one exclusively intended for Twitter, the other with a free lifetime data service in exchange for paying six more times for the Peek.
FonPeek, presented yesterday at the Le Web event, is the European version of the invention and its third incarnation.
It's just as limited as his U.S. counterpart, but it comes with a surprise.
The monthly maintenance price of about 12 euros per month includes roaming throughout Europe.
Those who travel with a smartphone already know what it’s like to give up the data when you cross the border or desperately look for a coffee shop with WiFi (or a Fon connection) to be able to query mail.
The idea of fixed-priced roaming is incredibly appealing and it would have to be up and running for a long time.
Yes, some operators have special agreements and travel plans, but in my experience it takes a doctorate in physics to understand them and good glasses to read the fine print.
The European Commission’s repeated earings to achieve affordable roaming rates across Europe have barely had a noticeable effect.
Outside the country of origin there is fear of using the data, and rightly so.
That an apparatus like FonPeek (it is still curious that it bears Fon’s name when it lacks Wifi) achieves this type of fees means that operators begin to think differently and study different proposals with which to amortize their data network.
Kindle is another good example, it includes book download traffic and other queries on the Internet.
No contracts and no bagpipes.
It is a connected device and you pay for it when you buy it, not month by month.
And that’s what I hope to see one day with FonPeek, a purchase option with access for life like Peek already has in the U.S.
Not because I'm going to buy it.
I'll look at my mail on the phone, thank you.
I look forward to it because I think it’s a fairer model for consumers.
It would be interesting to see a computer with this same system.
Michelle's Fetish Color
Yellow is, without a doubt, the favorite shade of the American First Lady.
If you have already come to the inauguration of Barack Obama as president of the United States with a dress and a coat of this color, he has chosen the same to accompany him in, without a doubt, the second most important moment of his life: that collected in Oslo from the Nobel Peace Prize.
Michelle Obama has worn a very similar model on both occasions: in addition to being a similar color -green yellow with bright reflections-, twice the outfit was composed of three pieces -dress, jacket and coat of the same fabric-, and was adorned with a large embroidery or necklace.
So much so, that at first glance it seemed that the first lady of the United States followed in the wake of the Princess of Asturias and had chosen to repeat obstacle.
But the fabric in which the two suits are made makes them very different.
If for the inauguration it was a fat lace, in Oslo it is a velvet with print.
The accessories have also varied: half-heeled green shoes over again, and a similar model, but in golded, now.
And equally different is the hairstyle: one collected on this occasion, mane releases the previous one.
75 per cent of working mothers have had work problems due to maternity
Discrimination at work has increased in recent years, especially for women from younger generations who want to become mothers.
This is revealed by the study 'Fertility and work trajectory of women in Spain', prepared by the CSIC in collaboration with the Institute of Women.
The survey of 9,737 women aged 15 to 75 leaves no doubt about it: 75% of working mothers have seen limited employment opportunities due to maternity.
The study points out that there are many differences in the onset of coexistence as a couple and the number of children, depending on whether they work or not and the type of work they develop.
Those who do not have an activity outside the home, work part-time, or have a non-stable job, start the coexistence as a couple, advance the first motherhood, and have more children.
On the contrary, women with stable employment, mainly in the public sector, delay coexistence and motherhood, and have less offspring.
"Women between 35 and 49 years with fixed work have the first child, on average, 3.7 years after initiating coexistence, a figure that increases to 4.1 women with fixed jobs have needed more time than their vital trajectory to achieve them, which has led them to postpone the union and the arrival of children.
In addition, the type of work carried out exerts a lot of influence on maternity although less on the schedule of pairing, "explains CSIC researcher Margarita Delgado.
The level of studies is another factor that differentiates women’s ages when marrying and having the first offspring.
According to the survey, the higher the level of education, the higher the age at which they are married and those with the first child.
The difference also persists between women of the same generation.
For example, in the group between 35 and 49 years old, those with elementary and higher studies have been mothers for the first time at 25.1 and 32.1 on average respectively.
"Another division is also established among women according to their level of education.
While those who have elementary studies get married and have their first child before getting a stable job, those of medium or higher levels reverse this sequence and give priority to obtaining a stable job as a pre-marriage condition and the arrival of a child.
