Manuel Vicent's Poker
Manuel Vicent gives away in 'Póquer de ases' (Alfaguara, illustrations by Fernando Vicente) about thirty x-rays of writers who have gone through his literary life (and, in cases, also for the other).
It does not do so in the predictable and fearsome genres (biographical semblance, life and work), but looking for something similar to the ignition core of the creative character.
The journey has its excitations and Vincent does not save scares or illuminations.
In addition to an exquisite pleasure, the book is a moving invitation to readings and relectures.
Here are some snapshots.
About Albert Camus: "In its pages I discovered that the Mediterranean was not a sea, but a spiritual drive, almost physical, the same one that I felt unnamed: the pleasure against dark fate, morality without guilt and innocence without any god."
About Samuel Beckett: "Nihilista, an allegorical Christian, wrote what he had in the blood, not the intellect, between powerlessness and ignorance, with a dazzling poetic, meaningless humour, like the knife blade he was about to kill him."
About Graham Greene: “Our man was abducted by this woman with a passion that lasted thirteen years, in whose flesh he combined the emotion of adultery with the pleasure of remorse, a spiritual privilege that consisted of reaching heaven through the path of perdition.”
About James Joyce: "The 'Ulysses' was published in Paris in 1922 by Sylvia Beach. It is one of the eight thousand-meter summits of universal literature, which must be climbed by the northern wall, from which the best mountaineers come again and again."
William Faulkner: "He was a weird guy.
Of himself, he sometimes said that he was the heir of a landowner of the county and others that he was the son of a Negro and a crocodile.
Both dreams were of grandeur."
Louis-Ferdinand Céline: "This writer picked up the demented scream thrown from a bridge by that character from Munich to take his echo to the bottom of 20th-century night in his literary form and did so on a journey running away from himself and invoking posterity with a violent speech to the worms."
About Dorothy Parker: "One day she got down on her knees and prayed, 'Dear God, I beg you to stop writing like a woman.'
About Joseph Conrad: "On his tomb were engraved these verses of Spenser: 'Dream after effort, / after the storm the port, / the rest after the war, / after life and death pleases."
About Virginia Wolf: "In the luggage he also brought his depressions.
The hursband normally accepted that she told him that Edward VII spied on her among the azaleas or that the birds sang in Greek.
There has never been the case for a man so patient and in love with a neurotic whose literary talent went ahead of his madness."
About Pío Baroja: "What did Martín-Santos but try to inoculate Joyce in Baroja?
What Benet didn't try to go through Baroja over Faulkner's weapons?
What did Cela but abduct the fame of that man so that it would serve as his own pedestal?"
Coward aggression
Last week, my 70-year-old mother was strolling through our neighborhood in the municipality of Fuenlabrada when a group of young people between 15 and 18 years old passed by her and our dog, an exemplary half-breed, rescued from the kennel and with no more than six kilos of weight and, without a word, gave her two kicks that left the dog badly injured.
Our outrage is big because we didn’t find the fun in a gratuitous aggression to a small, helpless animal, which by the way, was tied up.
To this we must add the suffocation that provoked a lady who walked quietly and that, when rebuked these individuals, came to fear for his physical integrity, since an energumen who attacks an animal, is able to do the same with a woman, a child, an old man or, in short, with whatever is weaker than himself.
The incident occurred at five o’clock in the afternoon on an ordinary Tuesday in a traditionally quiet neighborhood.
The dog was crying all day.
By the way, we filed a complaint at a nearby police station that we doubt that it serves much and not to generate susceptibilities, only to clarify that the coward killers were Spanish.
Outrage has led us to denounce this fact.
We don’t understand what’s happening among young people to consider it fun to abuse a helpless animal that gives us affection, companionship and friendship.
We don’t know where well-education and respect for animals and people have stayed.
Michelin and Fesvial together for the use of the hull
The Michelin tire manufacturer, with the collaboration of the Spanish Foundation for Road Safety (Fesvial), has launched a campaign to promote hull use, mainly among the youngest motorcyclists, both entities reported today.
This initiative seeks to raise awareness about the importance of using the helmet, since it is the only element of protection capable of avoiding head injuries or even death in the case of suffering an accident while riding a motorcycle.
Michelin indicated that this campaign is part of its commitment to road safety, which "goes beyond designing and manufacturing safer products every day" and affirmed that it also contributes to this goal through other pedagogical and informative actions.
This new campaign to promote the use of the helmet on the motorcycle, under the slogan 'To be or not to be', will have a first phase in which posters will be distributed in more than 500 motorcycle workshops, with the aim of achieving maximum dissemination.
Finally, Michelin stressed that road safety "is a constant concern" for the company, since being a manufacturer of tires for two-wheeled vehicles, one of its main purposes is to develop increasingly safer products.
Upload the sale of light commercials
Almost two years later, the light commercial vehicle market begins to see some light at the end of the tunnel.
The 9,425 units that were registered in November in Spain represent an increase of 12.6% compared to 2008 and, more importantly, they represent the first increase after 22 consecutive months of declines, according to figures from the employers of manufacturers Anfac and that of Ganvam sellers.
The good data, however, should be quarantined since the reference with which the comparison is made -November 2008- was particularly bad, with a decrease of 60% compared to the previous year.
As a result of this is that, despite the rebound of the last month, accumulated registrations in these 11 months are 40% below those of the previous year.
Since January 95,853 units have been sold together all the vehicles that make up the tailor’s drawer of light commercials: derived from passenger cars, pick-ups, vans, light vans and light trucks/chassis.
Likewise, the clouds that most affect their demand have not disappeared, because although they are also acquired by individuals, most of the purchases are made by small and medium-sized companies, which pay in almost 100% of the cases through external financing.
So to the restrictions involved in the closed faucet of bank loans, we must add other factors such as the increase in levels of unemployment or the persistence of the bad economic situation.
Faced with this panorama and unliking to passenger cars, the aid for the purchase of 2000E has been of little use.
In addition to private individuals, self-employed people and SMEs that are going to acquire a light commercial of up to 3.5 tons with a level of CO2 emissions that does not exceed 160 grams per kilometer, but the number of operations has been testimonial.
Yesterday, Ganvam again recalled the need to dedicate special attention on the part of the Administration to these vehicles and also to industrial vehicles (trucks and buses).
The seller’s association forecasts a 2010 similar to the ending year, for which it calculates a fall in sales (of passenger cars and 4x4) of 19%.
Emergency braking assistance is already mandatory
As happened with the anti-locking of ABS brakes and it will happen with ESP stability control, emergency braking assistance systems will have to be an obligatory element in European cars.
In fact, they are already in all passenger cars and light commercial vehicles of new registration, while for the rest a period of lack is set until the end of February 2011.
The measure, imposed by the European Union, aims to increase the protection of pedestrians although, due to its way of working, it can also help to avoid many collisions due to reach.
The emergency braking attendant what he does is to increase the pressure on the brakes in case of an emergency, which the system detects by the stomp given by the driver.
But he doesn't always keep that pressure to the end.
According to a study carried out by the car components manufacturer Bosch, a third of the drivers involved in an accident had not reached the brake before the collision and half of them did not take advantage of the full force.
Up to 1,100 accidents
The intervention of the aid, on the other hand, requires the maximum to the braking system, reducing the distance used by the car to stop.
According to an EU study, if all cars were equipped with this type of elements, up to 1,100 fatal accidents involving pedestrians could be avoided each year.
The reduction will be even greater – it is estimated that one out of every four claims would be avoided with damage to people – when the so-called intelligent emergency braking system is popularized.
In this case, the vehicle is equipped with a system that (through a radar or a camera) detects the obstacles and warns the driver, in addition to acting slightly on the brakes.
If the driver reacts, it helps by increasing the pressure on the pedal, if necessary.
In case it does not react, and even if the collision is unavoidable, the device exerts maximum effort to the brakes to minimize damage.
General Motors Delays Opel Plan Until January
While workers at Opel’s plant in Figueruelas (Zaragoza) have already been notified by Nick Reilly, the new president of General Motors (GM) Europe, that the production of the new Meriva will begin in the first week of April - the day 8 probably - according to Javier Ortega, the U.S. manufacturer warns that it will need more time to conclude the recovery plan for Opel.
