Behind the wheel of the Porsche Boxster Spyder
To develop their last, and expensive jewel, the Porsche engineers took the Boxster S as a base and managed to get them to lose weight no less than 80 kilos, which has left the scale in just 1,275 kilos.
This led them to dispense, in the first place, with the automatic roof that has been supplied by one of canvas with manual actuation, which saved 21 kilos; using aluminum doors such as those of the GT3, 'fall' another 15; removing the air conditioner, 13; reducing the fuel tank from 64 to 54 liters, they gained 7 kilos more; riding sports bacquets another 12 and with the light wheels
Finally, giving up the CDR-30 audio system contributed another six kilograms and changing the door handles for some fabric, the remaining kilo.
Although also offered with dual-clutch PDK shifting, the test was conducted on a unit with the six-speed manual case, mainly on California’s secondary roads, on mountain stretches with actually reversed traces
Their behavior can only be described as blameless.
Weight loss doesn’t mean the car doesn’t step on asphalt with aplome.
Rather, quite the opposite: we hadn’t tested such a small and light model for a long time that, at the same time, it transmitted so much safety in cornering, even at high speeds.
He is a small athlete, able to heat his muscles in a short gait for the necessary time, until we order him to sprint, brake, recover and squeeze his engine again...
What we ask. Thanks to his direction, as communicative as necessary, what we indicate with our hands on the steering wheel is what the car will do.
And he will do it with surgical precision.
Engine sound at will
The sound of its V6 boxer, with 320 horses (10 more than the Boxster S) is very successful and can be enhanced with the 'sport' option and through a system that modifies the bellowing of the engine, already released previously in the Panamera.
Accelerating capacity is brutal, either from standing or in recoveries.
Suspensions are firm, but not hard or uncomfortable.
The asphalt of the roads we went through with the Boxster Spyder was quite punished, but the car digested the bumps well.
The electronic aids barely made an act of presence throughout the journey; and that in one of the curves we found ice at dawn and gravel, well into the morning.
In short, an ultra-light car, of the most fun to drive that one can in the market and that is more than a simple modification of the Boxster S.
The car will go on sale in February 2010, with a price of 70,831 euros.
