How Much Should You Charge for Your Facebook Profile?
Facebook users can know the salary they deserve according to their profile, thanks to a new Trovit application, the search engine with the highest number of classified ads of real estate, employment and engine in Spain.
The tool How much should you collect? calculates the salaries of users from the more than 140. 000 job offers that Trovit has in Spain, from a form available on Facebook, where they must introduce their profession, years of experience, age and city where they want to work.
In the week that the application has been in operation, the seeker has detected that in general the Spanish worker "feels unpaid", explained in a statement the product manager of Trovit, Albert Ribera.
According to the data that Trovit has its search engines in the United Kingdom and France, Spaniards are the least paid.
A British computer programmer collects 45,226 euros gross per year and one French, 31,059, compared to 24,000 received by a Spanish one.
In the same way, a driver charges 34,247 euros net annually in England, 25,751 in France and 16,420 in Spain.
According to Trovit, the lowest-paid job in Spain is that of waiter, with 11,592 euros net annually; followed by dependent, with 14,725 euros; and that of operator, with 15,667 euros.
The most wanted jobs in the network in the last three months are that of driver, part-time jobs and that of social workers.
Brazil sets a date for Zelaya's departure
The Brazilian government has sentered Manuel Zelaya’s stay in his embassy in Tegucigalpa.
The deposed President must leave the diplomatic legation by 27 January 2010, when he would officially complete his term of office.
Francisco Catunda, chargé d'affairs of Brazil's embassy in the Honduran capital, confirmed to TV Globo that "Mel" knows that he must leave before that date of the embassy, where he has been a refugee since September 21.
"He's aware that he has to leave on Jan. 27, at the end of his term, he has to look for another destiny."
The station also quoted the former president, who confirmed his intention to leave the embassy before the established deadline: "My position is to leave as soon as possible, logically with the support of the government of Brazil," Zelaya said by phone.
Following the November 29 elections (where Porfirio Lobo was elected), Zelaya assured that he would remain in the Brazilian embassy until January.
However, this week he tried to seek refuge in Brazil.
The operation was finally frustrated by rejecting Zelaya the status of political assilation that the "de facto" government that Roberto Micheletti presides over him.
Thank you very much
After the days of wine and roses since last spring in which asset inflation has been the dominant tonic in the markets, again the uncertainty reappears in the markets, which should not surprise since it is their natural state, in an environment in which agents make decisions about the future by uncertain nature.
The medicine used by central banks to stabilize markets was to monetize debt with direct purchase of assets and indirectly giving liquidity open bars at rates close to zero for financial institutions to buy assets to discount.
The central banks have already confirmed that direct asset purchases have practically ended and, without questioning their heterodox policy of liquidity, they are beginning to throw some sand in the gears how the ECB did with its year-on-year auction next week.
Therefore, investors doubt the ability of the global economy to maintain economic recovery without such heterodox measures and we are witnessing a reversal of the reduction of risk premiums.
Stock market volatilities and corporate bond spreads have rebounded again, although they are still far removed from the paranormal levels they reached earlier this year.
The states were forced to socialize private risk and public debt is again affected.
Once again there is a flight to quality and the United States, Japan and Germany have been the refuge chosen by investors, causing an extension of the spreads of the eurozone countries with respect to Germany.
Spain had barely been affected by this episode of instability but Standard & Poor's arrived and threatened a new downward revision of the Kingdom of Spain's rating.
On the other hand, some uncertainty is not wrong to silence the defenders of moral risk who were again trying to create a position of conscience about those responsible for global economic policymakers for curbing the destructive effects derived from the credit restriction on job destruction, when what would have to be done is to decree a new Thanksgiving day.
However, Dubai paints a tribal struggle between the Emirs to recompose power in the emirates and that of Greece we have seen before.
A government that hides public deficits, elections and a new government that pulls the blanket.
Prodi did it in Italy after Berlusconi and Socrates in Portugal after Durão Barroso.
It does not make sense for the Ecofin to save Hungary from non-compliance with its foreign payments at the beginning of the year and to allow a euro member country to default on its commitments.
What is logical is that the Ecofin has forced the Greek government to be transparent and make its citizens co-responsible for the need for extraordinary measures to maintain the stability of their public finances.
If, as we expect, we are facing an episode of normalization of transitory risk premiums, after some excesses in leveraged positions, it would be within expectations.
In the Spanish case, as long as our public debt differentials expand just as European corporate bonds we must not worry.
If European differentials decrease and ours continue to expand, then it would be Spanish idiosyncratic risk.
Standard & Poor's is an anecdote. When our advanced indicators are forcing most study services to anticipate their recovery scenarios and to revise their scenario upwards for 2011, including the OECD and the European Commission, to return to the load with a Japanese scenario for the Spanish economy does not augurate much life expectancy to their warning.
Again, it has all the look that it will be an excellent opportunity to buy Spanish assets, albeit at the moment "prudency, driving friend".
Related sectors
The president of Iberdrola, Ignacio Sánchez Galán, was the first to intervene yesterday before the subcommittee of the Congress of Deputies in which Spain’s energy strategy is debated these days in the next two decades.
After his intervention, which was followed by the president of Gas Natural-Fenosa, Rafael Villaseca, Galán gave the press some brushstrokes of what he had proposed in the subcommittee behind closed doors.
For the president of Iberdrola, Spain needs to invest 50,000 million euros until 2020 and another 60,000 million between that date and 2030, provided that nuclear power plants are kept open today in operation.
If not, the necessary investments will not be 60,000 million, but up to 95,000 million.
Of course, Iberdrola believes that in the next decade investments should not be used to increase capacity in generation, but in distribution networks and international interconnections
From 2020, the issue changes and power will have to be increased.
His bet focuses on both gas and coal plants with carbon dioxide capture mechanisms and, above all, renewable energies.
In this case, he considers that 18,000 new MW are needed, of course, with other "supporting energies".
Galán, who believes that the Sustainable Economity Bill that the Government has just approved is in line with what his company has been demanding, did not want to make blood with the royal decree to incentivize the consumption of national coal, which contradicts policies to combat climate change.
In his opinion, there is "a clear long-term bet" in this regard, despite the "punctual" measures, as the president of Iberdrola called them.
Anyway, Galán believes that investment decisions, although they are long-term, must begin to be taken because they are slow to mature and a stable remunerative framework is necessary to address them.
Ferrovial wins Aer Lingus 'handling' at ten airports
Ferrovial’s subsidiary for on-the-ground airport services, Swissport Switzerland, will play the role of preferred partner of Irish airline Aer Lingus for cargo services at ten European airports where it has a presence.
Paris, London, Frankfurt or Brussels are some of the highlights.
The agreement, of which economic terms have not transcended, lasts for five years.
On the other hand, Ferrovial, the Junta de Castilla-La Mancha and the University of Alcalá de Henares yesterday signed a collaboration protocol to create the Intelligent Infrastructure Innovation Center
The alliance contemplates an investment of 20 million until 2012, of which 50% corresponds to Ferrovial.
One of the tasks of the center will be to investigate energy efficiency in highways and airports.
Acciona disputes its first highway in Australia with Hochtief and Bouygues
Acciona fights for its first concession motorway in Australia, a market in which it already works as an energy company and water manager.
The work at stake is a tunnel valued at 1,700 million Australian dollars (about 1,047 million euros).
France’s Bouygues and German Hochtief are their rivals.
A 5-kilometre tunnel in the Australian city of Brisbane on the East Coast has become the closestest option for Acciona to settle in the country as a construction company and highway concessionaire.
The City Council wants to have it ready at the end of 2014
After opting for public-private collaboration (PPP) to carry out the project, in a business plan supervised by Erns & Young, the Administration already has a final list of candidates to take the work and exploit the toll.
Waiting to present their offers next May, the planned investment amounts to 1,700 million Australian dollars (1,047 million euros) and in the last phase three consortiums competing with a remarkable presence of European construction companies.
The group called Transcity is led by Acciona, the only Spaniard on the list, and its companions are BMD and Ghella.
The first is one of the largest Australian construction companies, while the second, based in Italy, is one of the largestest European specialists in underground works.
Against the Acciona team plays the Northern Direct consortium, with the presence of the British Laing O'Rourke, the Australian infrastructure manager Transfield and the French construction group Bouygues.
The third and last consortium that has passed the sieve is the so-called LBRJV, where the Australian Leighton (a subsidiary of the German construction company Hochtief, in turn owned by ACS), the local Baulderstone (owned by the German group Bilfinger Berger) and the gala firm specialized in civil works Razel.
The Australian infrastructure market promises great works and privatizations, but it entails a clear difficulty of entry if not through the acquisition of a local construction firm.
A strategy followed by the aforementioned Hochtief or Bilfinger, in addition to Asian references, and which continues to be a pending subject for the Spanish giants of the sector.
Only ACS has achieved a position in that market through its 30% stake in Hochtief, owner of the aforementioned Leighton.
Millionaire Assets
Acciona is committed to a geographical area of the utmost importance for the sector, in which it already has an outstanding presence through its activity as an energy supplier and in the desalination business.
Among other assets, it has its largest wind farm, that of Waubra (192 MW) in Victoria, and leads the group of companies in charge of lifting and managing the Adelaide desalination plant, with a budget of 700 million.
His intention now is to consolidate these two areas and take advantage of synergies to enter the fields of infrastructure construction and operation.
The underground toll road, promoted by the Brisbane City Infrastructure Office and dubbed Northern Link, is called to link the western Toowong motorway with the ring road in Kelvin Grove (see map) along 5-kilometres of tunnel. This is the greatest action to reduce congestion in Brisbane traffic.
The calendar marked by the Administration sets the award for the summer of 2010 and the start of the works in December next year.
Although this payment step would open to traffic in the last months of 2014, it is not expected that the work will be finished until 2016.
Tunnel began to be designed in 2005.
It will include two lanes per direction, electronic toll system and a complex ventilation system.
The Government of Queensland will contribute 500 million Australian dollars (308 million euros) to its financing.
Brisbane now offers business to Hochtief
Australia continues to bear notable fruits to Hochtief, who announced yesterday to obtain a contract with the Government valued at 154 million.
ACS will enable, through its local subsidiary Leighton, the infrastructure of the national broadband network with fiber optic cable.
This action will offer fast internet access to 400,000 people and will give work to the German for 18 months.
Moreover, the company has transferred the 27-storey King George Tower construction project in the Australian city of Brisbane. The works were yet to begin and Hochtief has closed the sale at 129 million.
Already in the United Kingdom, specifically in Manchester, he will share with Laing O'Rourke the construction and operation of two schools for 25 years.
The initial investment will be 75 million euros.
FCC awarded a tunnel in Slovenia for 64 million
FCC, through its subsidiary Alpine, has been awarded the construction of a 2.1-kilometere-long tunnel for a Slovenian motorway amounting to 64.5 million euros.
